WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.070
- Welcome to the Huberman Lab podcast

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.920
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.218
for everyday life.

00:00:06.218 --> 00:00:09.150
[upbeat music]

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.170
I'm Andrew Huberman,

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.260
and I'm a professor of
neurobiology and ophthalmology

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.210
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.640
Today, my guest is Dr. Eddie Chang.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.460
Dr. Eddie Chang is the chair
of the neurosurgery department

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.760
at the University of
California at San Francisco.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.340
Dr. Chang's clinical group
focuses on the treatment

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.190
of movement disorders including epilepsy.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:30.600
He is also a world expert

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.370
in the treatment of speech disorders

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:34.800
and relieving paralysis
that prevents speech

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.350
and other forms of
movement and communication.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:40.500
Indeed, his laboratory is
credited with discovering ways

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.110
to allow people who have
fully locked-in syndrome,

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:45.090
that is, who cannot speak or move,

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:48.270
to communicate through
computers and AI devices

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.030
in order to be able to speak
to others in their world

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.760
and understand what
others are saying to them.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.610
It is a truly remarkable
achievement that we discuss today,

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.800
in addition to his discoveries
about critical periods,

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.900
which are periods of
time during one's life

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.390
when one can learn things,
in particular, languages,

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:05.910
with great ease as
opposed to later in life,

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:07.590
and we talk about the basis of things

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:10.170
like bilingualism and trilingualism.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.480
We talk about how the
brain controls movement

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.030
of the very muscles that
allow for speech and language

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.130
and how those can be modified over time.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:18.510
We also talk about stutter,

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.240
and we talk about a number of
aspects of speech and language

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.550
that give insight into
not just how we create

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.050
this incredible thing called speech

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.330
or how we understand speech and language,

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.370
but how the brain works more generally.

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.200
Dr. Chang is also one of the world leaders

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.580
in bioengineering,

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.550
that is, the creation of
devices that allow the brain

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.070
to function at supraphysiological levels

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.240
and that can allow people

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.860
with various syndromes and disorders

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.810
to overcome their deficits.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.400
So if you are somebody who is interested

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.350
in how the brain works normally,

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.930
how it breaks down and
how it can be repaired,

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.750
and if you are interested
in speech and language,

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:54.630
reading and comprehension
of information of any kind,

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.880
today's episode ought to
include some information

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.620
of deep interest to you.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.350
Dr. Chang is indeed the top of his field

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.060
in terms of understanding these issues

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.980
of how the brain encodes
speech and language

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.840
and creates speech and language

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.810
and, as I mentioned, movement
disorders and epilepsy.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:12.180
We even talk about things
such as the ketogenic diet,

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.460
the future of companies like Neuralink,

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.530
which are interested in bioengineering

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.800
and augmenting the human
brain, and much more.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.900
One thing that I would like
to note is that in addition

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.300
to being a world-class
neuroscience researcher

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.820
and world-class clinician, neurosurgeon,

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.500
and chair of neurosurgery,

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.260
Dr. Eddie Chang has also
been a close, personal friend

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.480
of mine since we were nine years old.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.220
We attended elementary school together,

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.500
and we actually had a science club

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.550
when we were nine years old

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:40.230
focused on a very particular topic.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:41.940
You'll have to listen
in to today's episode

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.470
to discover what that topic was

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:46.770
and what membership to that club required.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:50.400
That aside, Dr. Chang
is an absolute phenom

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.380
with respect to his scientific prowess,

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.410
that is, both his research
and his clinical abilities,

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.330
and he's one of these rare individuals

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.480
that, whenever he opens
his mouth, we learn.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.090
Before we begin, I'd like to
emphasize that this podcast

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:05.790
is separate from my teaching
and research roles at Stanford.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.710
It is, however, part
of my desire and effort

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:09.570
to bring zero-cost-to-consumer information

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:11.640
about science and science-related tools

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:12.900
to the general public.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:13.950
In keeping with that theme,

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:16.710
I'd like to thank the
sponsors of today's podcast.

