00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:18.810
And now for my discussion
with Dr. Eddie Chang.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:19.740
Eddie, welcome.

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:21.240
- Hi. Hi, Andrew.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:22.650
- Great to be here with you.

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:24.300
This has been a long time coming.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:27.600
Just to come clean,

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:29.670
we've known each other since
we were nine years old.

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:30.503
- Yeah.

00:07:30.503 --> 00:07:31.530
- But then there was a long gap

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:33.840
in which we didn't talk to one another.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:35.100
I heard things about you,

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:38.160
and, presumably, you heard
a thing or two about me,

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:39.480
for better or for worse.
[Eddie laughs]

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.760
And then we reconnected years later

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:44.700
when I was a PhD student and
you were a medical student.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:46.380
We literally ran into each other

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:49.080
in the halls of the University
of California San Francisco

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:50.820
where you're now the
chair of neurosurgery,

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:53.580
so it all comes full circle.

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:56.850
When you were at UCSF, you were
working with Mike Merzenich,

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:57.683
and I know that name

00:07:57.683 --> 00:07:59.130
might not be familiar to a lot of people,

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:02.310
but he's sort of synonymous
with neuroplasticity,

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:03.870
the ability of the
brain and nervous system

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:05.970
to change in response to experience.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:06.840
So for our listeners,

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:09.840
I would just love for you
to give a brief overview

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:11.220
of what you were doing at that time

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:12.990
because I find that work so fascinating,

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:16.440
and it really points to some
of the things that can promote

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:20.040
and maybe hinder our
brain's ability to change.

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:22.650
- Oh, wow. That's fantastic.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:26.610
So we did bump into each other
serendipitously back then,

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:30.300
and, at the time, I was
a medical student at UCSF

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:31.580
studying with Mike Merzenich.

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:36.513
In particular, I was studying
how the brain organizes

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:40.110
when you have patterns of sound.

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:43.800
And in particular, we were
studying the brain of rodents

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:45.780
and trying to understand
how different sound patterns

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:49.080
organize the frequency representation

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:52.050
from low to middle to high frequency maps

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:54.240
in the brains of baby rodents.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:57.030
And one of the things that
I was very interested in

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:57.870
was trying to understand

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:01.500
how the patterns of the
natural environment,

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:03.930
let's say the vocalizations
of the environment

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:05.970
that the rat pups were raised in,

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:07.800
or just the natural sounds that they hear,

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:11.610
how that shapes the
structure of the brain.

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:14.850
And one of the things we
did was to try an experiment

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:19.050
where we raised some of these
rat pups in white noise,

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:22.170
continuous white noise that
was essentially masking

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:24.660
all of those environmental sounds.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:27.210
- And what was the consequence
of animals being raised

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:28.770
in white noise environment?

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:30.660
- Well, one of the things
that we didn't expect

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:32.670
but we found, which was quite striking,

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:37.670
is that there's this early
period in brain development

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:39.600
where we're very susceptible

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:42.420
to the patterns that we hear or see.

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:44.700
In neuroscience, we call
this a critical period

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:46.830
or a sensitive period.

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:49.260
And we have this for our eyes,

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:51.060
but we also have it for our ears.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:54.660
And one of the most
striking examples of this

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:58.290
is that any human can
essentially grow up in a culture

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:00.240
where they hear different speech sounds

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:01.770
from one language to another,

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:03.870
and it's like after a couple of years,

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:05.910
you lose sensitivity to sounds

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:07.620
that are not part of your native language

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:10.347
and you have high sensitivity

00:10:10.347 --> 00:10:13.080
for the languages of your native culture.

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:16.020
And that's pretty extraordinary

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:18.960
that human brain has that flexibility

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:22.290
yet, at the same time, has that
specialization for language.

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:25.380
And so we were trying to think
about how do we model this,

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:28.530
for example, in rodents,
who obviously don't speak,

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:31.680
but we're just understanding how sounds

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:33.030
and environmental sounds

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:36.210
modulate and organize the auditory cortex.

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:38.820
And one of the things that we
found that was quite striking

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:43.820
was that if you basically
mask environmental sounds

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:46.200
from these rat pups,

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:47.640
the critical period,

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:50.820
this sensitive period where
it's open to plasticity,

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.703
it's open to change, it's
open to reorganization,

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:59.014
that window can stay
open much, much longer.

00:10:59.014 --> 00:11:01.410
And in one way, it sounds
like that's a good thing,

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:05.250
but on the other hand,
it's also a retardation.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:09.090
It actually slowed the maturation
of the auditory cortex.

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:13.050
It was ready to close when these
rat pups were really young,

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:14.730
but by raising them in white noise,

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:17.280
we found out that you could keep it open

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:22.230
for months beyond the time
period that it normally closes.

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:24.510
And so I think one of
the things it taught me

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:28.620
was that it's not just about
the genetic programming

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:31.350
that specifies some of
this sensitive period,

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:33.900
but it's also a little
bit about the nature

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:36.030
of the sounds that we hear

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:38.970
that help keep that window
for the critical period

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:40.440
open and closed.

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:41.280
- That's fascinating.

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:43.500
And I know it's difficult
to make a direct leap

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:46.230
from animal research to human research,

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:49.650
but if we could speculate a little bit,

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:51.540
I can imagine that some
people grow up in homes

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:54.660
where there's a lot of shouting
and a lot of inflection,

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:56.460
maybe people are very verbose.

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:58.230
Maybe others grow up in a home

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:00.963
where it's quieter and more peaceful.

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:03.870
Some people are going
to grow up in cities.

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:04.980
I just came back from New York City,

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:06.180
it's like all night long,

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:09.300
there's honking and sirens
and it's just nonstop,

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:12.933
and then I return here where
it's quite quiet at night.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:16.050
Can we imagine that the human brain

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:17.310
is going to be shaped differently

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:19.410
depending on whether or not one grows up

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:20.520
in one environment or another?

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:23.490
And would that impact their tendency

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:26.930
to speak in a certain way as
well as hear in a certain way?

00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:28.140
What do we know about that?

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:32.580
- Well, I think that, from my perspective,

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:36.390
it's really clear that those
sounds that we are exposed to

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:40.740
from the very earliest time,
even in utero, in the womb,

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:45.450
where the sound is hearing the
mother or father or friends

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:46.283
while in the womb

00:12:46.283 --> 00:12:49.353
actually will influence
how these things organize.

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:54.360
And so there's no question
that the sounds that we hear

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:55.620
are going to have some influence,

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:58.170
and those sounds are going to structure

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:00.840
the way that those neural
networks actually lay down

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:05.160
and will forever influence
how you hear sounds,

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:06.210
and speech and language

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:09.033
is probably one of the most
profound examples of that.

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:10.800
- I get a lot of questions

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:13.860
about the use of white noise during sleep.

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:15.240
In particular, people want to know

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:17.550
whether or not using a white noise machine

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:19.590
or a machine or a program

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:21.600
that makes the sound
of waves, for instance,

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:24.360
if it assists their infant in sleeping,

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:25.830
is it going to be bad for them

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:27.900
because it's flooding the auditory system

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:29.940
with a bunch of essentially white noise

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:32.370
or disorganized noise?

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:34.800
Do we have an answer to that question?

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:35.633
- Not yet.

00:13:35.633 --> 00:13:36.810
I think that what you're asking

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:38.310
is a really important question

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:41.790
because parents are using
white noise generators

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:44.760
almost universally now,
and for good reasons.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:47.430
You know, it is hard to
have kids up at night.

00:13:47.430 --> 00:13:51.120
I've got three kids of my
own and was very tempted

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:53.340
to think about how to
use some of these tools

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:55.890
to just soothe them and get them to bed,

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:58.773
especially when I was, like,
so tired and exhausted.

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:03.180
But I think that there is a cost,

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:04.620
you know, to think a little bit about.

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:06.690
You know, we're not exposed

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:10.470
to continuous white noise naturally.

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:14.940
There is a value to having
really salient, structured sounds

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:16.190
that are part of our natural environment

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:18.900
to actually have the
brain develop normally.

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:22.740
So whether or not that has an impact,

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:26.220
you know, while you're
sleeping, it's not clear.

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:28.410
I don't think that those
studies have been done.

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:32.910
What was really clear was that
if you raise these baby rats

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:35.430
in continuous white noise, not super loud,

00:14:35.430 --> 00:14:38.430
but just enough to mask
the environmental sounds

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:41.880
that that was enough to keep,
you know, the auditory cortex,

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:44.220
the part of the brain that hears,

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:45.660
in this really delayed state

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:48.480
which could essentially
slow down the development

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:50.520
and maturation of the brain.

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:53.100
- And one could probably assume

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:55.773
that slowing the maturation
of areas of the brain

00:14:55.773 --> 00:14:57.480
that are responsible for hearing

00:14:57.480 --> 00:14:58.860
might, I want to underscore,

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:01.230
might impact one's
ability to speak, right?

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:04.800
Because isn't it the case
that if people can't hear,

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:07.260
they actually have a
harder time enunciating

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:09.120
in a particular way, is that right?

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:12.120
If I were to not be able
to hear my own voice,

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:14.940
would my speech patterns change?

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:16.040
- Well, I think part of it

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:19.230
is that, over time, we develop sensitivity

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:22.590
to the very specific speech
sounds in a given language,

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:26.400
and the sensitivity improves

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:28.527
as we hear more and more and more of it.

00:15:28.527 --> 00:15:30.300
And then on the other hand,

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:33.720
we lose sensitivity to other
speech sounds at the same time.

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:35.520
But as part of that process,

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:39.900
we also have a selectivity, a gain,

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:44.250
a specialization even for those sounds,

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:46.290
even relative to noise,

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:48.420
noisy backgrounds and things like that.

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:49.590
I tend to think about it

00:15:49.590 --> 00:15:52.590
like what is the signal-to-noise ratio?

00:15:52.590 --> 00:15:55.230
And so the brain has its own ways

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:58.500
of trying to increase
that signal-to-noise ratio

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:00.720
in order to make it more clear.

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:03.537
Part of that is how we hear

00:16:03.537 --> 00:16:05.490
and how it lays down a foundation

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:07.200
for that signal-to-noise ratio,

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:10.133
and so you can imagine a child

00:16:10.133 --> 00:16:13.230
that's raised continuously in white noise

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:15.750
would be really deprived
of those kind of sounds

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:18.510
that are really necessary
for it to develop properly.

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:23.160
So I think with regard to
those tools for babies,

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:24.570
I think we should study it,

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:26.370
we should try to understand
this definitively.

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:28.140
I think what we saw in rodents

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:30.660
would tell us that there is potential,

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:32.520
you know, things that we
should be concerned about.

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:34.080
But, again, it's not really clear,

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:37.802
if you're just using at night,
whether it has those effects.

00:16:37.802 --> 00:16:38.880
- I guess the critical question

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:41.460
that a number of people
are going to be asking

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:46.350
is did you decide to use a
white noise machine or not

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:50.970
to help keep any of your
three children asleep?

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:53.670
- Well, I think the short answer is no.

00:16:53.670 --> 00:16:56.010
I mean, I obviously did
a lot of work thinking

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:59.640
and work on this and
thought about it carefully,

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:03.540
but there are other kinds of noise,

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:05.370
or, I wouldn't even call it noise,

00:17:05.370 --> 00:17:06.873
other sounds that you can use

00:17:06.873 --> 00:17:09.930
that can be equally soothing to a baby.

00:17:09.930 --> 00:17:12.150
It's just that white
noise has no structure,

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:13.080
and what it's doing

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:16.650
is essentially masking out
all of the natural sounds.

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:17.910
And I think the goal

00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:20.520
should really be about
how do we replace that

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:22.740
with other more natural sounds
that structure the brain

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:24.890
in the way that we want
to be more healthy.

00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:27.330
- Well, I know that

00:17:27.330 --> 00:17:29.520
after you finished your medical training,

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:32.280
you went on to, of course,
specialize in neurosurgery.

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:36.360
And last I checked, you
spend most of your days

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:38.190
either running your
laboratory or in the clinic

00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:39.420
or running the department,

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:41.460
and your clinical work
and your laboratory work

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:46.170
involves often removing
pieces of the skull of humans

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:48.560
and going in and either removing things

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:50.373
or stimulating neurons,

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:54.780
treating various ailments
of different kinds,

00:17:54.780 --> 00:17:57.750
but your main focus these days, of course,

00:17:57.750 --> 00:18:01.920
is the neurobiology of
speech and language.

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:03.570
And so for those that aren't familiar,

00:18:03.570 --> 00:18:07.770
could you please distinguish
for us speech versus language

00:18:07.770 --> 00:18:09.600
in terms of whether or not

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:11.640
different brain areas control them?

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:13.800
And I know that there's a lot of interest

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:16.230
in how speech and language and hearing

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:18.180
all relate to one another.

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:19.590
And then we'll talk a bit

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:22.110
about, for instance, emotions

00:18:22.110 --> 00:18:24.510
and how facial expressions
could play into this,

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:26.490
or hand gestures, et cetera.

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:29.490
But for the uninformed person,

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:32.373
and for me, to be quite direct,

00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:37.263
what are the brain areas that
control speech and language?

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:39.630
What are they really,

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:41.730
and especially in humans,
how are they different?

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:44.430
I mean, we have such
sophisticated language

00:18:44.430 --> 00:18:46.440
compared to a number of other species.

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:48.690
What does all this landscape
look like in there?

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:52.650
- Yeah, well, that's a
fascinating question,

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:55.890
and I'm going to just try to
connect a couple of the dots here,

00:18:55.890 --> 00:19:00.060
which is that in that earlier
work during medical school,

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:02.730
I was doing a lot of what
we call neurophysiology,

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:04.890
putting electrodes into
the auditory cortex

00:19:04.890 --> 00:19:08.430
and understanding how the
brain responds to sounds,

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:10.980
and that's how we actually
mapped out these things

00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:14.250
about the sensitivity
to sensitive periods.

00:19:14.250 --> 00:19:17.250
That experience with Mike Merzenich

00:19:17.250 --> 00:19:20.640
and thinking about how plasticity
is regulated in the brain

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:23.100
and particular about how sound

00:19:23.100 --> 00:19:24.960
is represented by brain activity

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:28.740
was something that, you know,
was really formative for me.

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:30.690
And because I was a medical student

00:19:30.690 --> 00:19:32.740
and was going back to my medical studies,

00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:35.640
it was that in combination

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:40.640
with seeing some awake brain surgeries

00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:43.050
that our department is
really well known for.

00:19:43.050 --> 00:19:44.610
One of my mentors, Mitch Berger,

00:19:44.610 --> 00:19:46.350
really pioneered these methods

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:48.480
for taking care of
patients with brain tumor

00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:52.230
and be able to do these surgeries safely

00:19:52.230 --> 00:19:55.470
by keeping patients awake
and by mapping out language.

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:57.030
- So they're talking and listening,

00:19:57.030 --> 00:19:59.250
and you're essentially in
conversation with these patients

00:19:59.250 --> 00:20:01.530
while there's a portion
of their skull removed,

00:20:01.530 --> 00:20:02.580
and you are stimulating

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:05.630
or, in some cases, removing
areas of their brain,

00:20:05.630 --> 00:20:07.080
is that right?

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:10.440
- That's exactly right, and
the only thing off there

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:13.110
is it's not essentially, it is just that.

00:20:13.110 --> 00:20:15.510
The only difference
between the conversation

00:20:15.510 --> 00:20:16.680
that I might have with my patient

00:20:16.680 --> 00:20:19.140
who's undergoing awake brain surgery

00:20:19.140 --> 00:20:23.100
is that I can't see their face
and they can't see my face.

00:20:23.100 --> 00:20:25.650
We actually have a sterile drape

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:27.450
that actually separates
the operating field,

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:28.800
and they're looking and interacting

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:30.420
with our neuropsychologist,

00:20:30.420 --> 00:20:33.240
but I can talk to them
and they can hear my voice

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:34.830
and vice versa.

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:36.600
And it's a really, really important way

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:38.853
of how we can protect some of those areas

00:20:38.853 --> 00:20:41.400
that are really critical for language,

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:43.620
at the same time, accomplish a mission

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:45.240
of getting the seizures under control

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:46.950
or getting a brain tumor removed.

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:48.060
- And is that because occasionally

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:49.410
you'll encounter a brain area,

00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:50.460
maybe you're stimulating

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:52.620
or considering removing that brain area,

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:56.100
and suddenly a patient
will start stuttering

00:20:56.100 --> 00:20:58.530
or will have a hard time
formulating a sentence?

00:20:58.530 --> 00:21:00.690
Is that essentially
what you're looking for?

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:03.270
You're looking for regions

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:06.510
in which it is okay or not okay to probe?

00:21:06.510 --> 00:21:09.330
- Exactly, so the first thing that we do

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:11.730
is that we use a small
electrical stimulator

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:13.560
to probe different parts of the areas

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:16.890
that we think might be related
and important for language

00:21:16.890 --> 00:21:19.920
or talking or even movements
of your arm and leg.

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:21.450
That's what we call brain mapping.

00:21:21.450 --> 00:21:24.540
And we use a small electrical current

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:25.950
that's delivered through a probe

00:21:25.950 --> 00:21:28.380
that we can just put at each spot.

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:29.820
And the areas that we're
really interested in

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:32.910
are, of course, the areas
that are right around the part

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:35.370
that is pathological,
the part that's injured,

00:21:35.370 --> 00:21:38.340
or the part that has a brain
tumor that we want to remove,

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:39.720
so we can apply that probe

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:44.010
and transiently, meaning
temporarily, activate it.

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:46.083
So if you're stimulating
the part of the brain

00:21:46.083 --> 00:21:50.040
that controls the hand, the
hand will move, it will jerk.

00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:53.430
Sometimes a fist will be
made, something like that.

00:21:53.430 --> 00:21:57.570
Other times, while someone is counting

00:21:57.570 --> 00:21:59.160
or just saying the days of the week,

00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:01.560
you can stimulate in a different area

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:04.200
that stops their speech altogether.

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:06.330
That's what we call speech arrest.

00:22:06.330 --> 00:22:09.300
Or if someone is looking at pictures

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:10.830
and they're describing the pictures

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:12.660
and you stimulate in a particular area,

00:22:12.660 --> 00:22:16.290
they stop speaking or the
words start coming out slurred

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:19.440
or they can't remember
the name of the object

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:20.760
that they're seeing in the picture.

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:23.610
These are all things that we're
listening really carefully

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:26.160
while we apply that focal stimulation.

00:22:26.160 --> 00:22:27.840
That's what we call brain mapping.

00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:29.940
- What are some of the more surprising,

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:31.230
or maybe even if you want to offer

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:33.600
one of the more outrageous
examples of things

00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:36.990
that people have suddenly done
or failed to be able to do

00:22:36.990 --> 00:22:39.330
as a consequence of this brain mapping?

00:22:39.330 --> 00:22:40.890
- Well, I think the thing to me

00:22:40.890 --> 00:22:43.920
that has been the most striking

00:22:43.920 --> 00:22:46.830
is that, you know, some of
these areas you stimulate,

00:22:46.830 --> 00:22:50.070
and altogether, you can
shut down someone's talking.

00:22:50.070 --> 00:22:55.050
So a person says, "I wanted to say it,

00:22:55.050 --> 00:22:57.120
but I couldn't get the words out."

00:22:57.120 --> 00:23:01.710
And even though I've seen
this thousands of times now,

00:23:01.710 --> 00:23:05.673
it's still exciting every
time that I see it because,

00:23:09.502 --> 00:23:12.270
it's exciting because
you're seeing the brain,

00:23:12.270 --> 00:23:14.763
it's a physical organ,
it's part of the body,

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:17.610
outside of the veins on top of it,

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:21.540
it doesn't look like a machine.

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:23.940
But when you do something like that

00:23:23.940 --> 00:23:27.420
and you focally change the way it works,

00:23:27.420 --> 00:23:29.490
and you see that because the
person can't talk anymore

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:31.380
and they say, "I know what I want to say,

00:23:31.380 --> 00:23:33.080
but I couldn't get the words out,"

00:23:34.560 --> 00:23:37.030
you're confronted with this idea

00:23:38.415 --> 00:23:43.140
that that organ is the
basis of speech and language

00:23:43.140 --> 00:23:44.910
and way beyond that, obviously,

00:23:44.910 --> 00:23:46.200
you know, for all the other functions

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:48.090
that we have for thinking

00:23:48.090 --> 00:23:50.670
and feeling our emotions, everything.

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:54.270
So that, to me, is a constant reminder

00:23:54.270 --> 00:23:58.060
of, you know, this really
special thing that the brain does

00:23:58.060 --> 00:24:01.497
which is compute so many
of the things that we do,

00:24:01.497 --> 00:24:02.970
and in particular in the area

00:24:02.970 --> 00:24:05.880
around speech and
language, generating words,

00:24:05.880 --> 00:24:09.123
something that is really
unique to our species,

00:24:10.620 --> 00:24:13.110
is just extraordinary to see.

00:24:13.110 --> 00:24:15.870
Again, even though I've
seen it thousands of times,

00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:18.960
it's just having that connection

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:20.400
because it doesn't look like a machine,

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:23.970
but it is doing something
that is quite complicated,

00:24:23.970 --> 00:24:25.620
precise, and remarkable.

00:24:25.620 --> 00:24:27.240
- Do you ever see emotional responses

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:29.310
from stimulation in particular areas?

00:24:29.310 --> 00:24:34.080
And do you ever hear or
see emotional responses

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:36.780
that are associated with
particular types of speech?

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:41.730
Because, for instance,
curse words are known to,

00:24:41.730 --> 00:24:43.320
people with Tourette's often will curse,

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:46.290
not always, sometimes they'll
have tics or other things.

00:24:46.290 --> 00:24:48.630
But what I learned from
a colleague of ours

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:51.150
is that curse words have a
certain structure to them.

00:24:51.150 --> 00:24:52.830
There's usually a heavy

00:24:52.830 --> 00:24:55.890
or kind of a sharp
consonant up front, right,

00:24:55.890 --> 00:24:59.040
that allows people, at least
as it was described to me,

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:00.450
to have some sort of emotional release.

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:02.280
It's not a word like murmur,

00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:04.140
which has kind of a soft entry here,

00:25:04.140 --> 00:25:06.000
I'm not using the technical language,

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:08.190
and you pick your favorite
curse word out there, folks.

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:11.160
I'm not going to shout out
any now or say any now,

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:15.360
but that certain words
have a structure to them

00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:16.980
that, because of the motor patterns

00:25:16.980 --> 00:25:20.010
that are involved in saying that word,

00:25:20.010 --> 00:25:23.670
you could imagine it has an
emotional response unto itself.

00:25:23.670 --> 00:25:24.810
So when stimulating

00:25:24.810 --> 00:25:26.340
or when blocking these
different brain areas,

00:25:26.340 --> 00:25:28.320
do you ever see people get angry or sad

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:30.900
or happy or more relaxed?

00:25:30.900 --> 00:25:35.900
- Oh, well, definitely I've seen cases

00:25:36.060 --> 00:25:39.880
where you can invoke anxiety, stress,

00:25:41.970 --> 00:25:46.647
and I think that there are also
areas that you can stimulate

00:25:46.647 --> 00:25:50.160
and you can also evoke
the opposite of that,

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:51.873
sort of like a calm state.

00:25:52.740 --> 00:25:55.950
- I think that brain area is
slightly hyperactive in you,

00:25:55.950 --> 00:25:57.830
or at least more than me.

00:25:57.830 --> 00:25:59.790
In all the years I've known you,

00:25:59.790 --> 00:26:02.603
you've always been, at least
externally, a very calm person.

00:26:02.603 --> 00:26:04.290
I mean, I always find it amusing

00:26:04.290 --> 00:26:05.307
that you work on speech and language

00:26:05.307 --> 00:26:07.383
and you have a very calming voice.

00:26:08.790 --> 00:26:10.110
And I'm being really serious.

00:26:10.110 --> 00:26:12.720
I think that there's a huge
variation in that, right,

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:15.840
in terms of how people speak
and how they accent words.

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:16.860
- Absolutely, yeah.

00:26:16.860 --> 00:26:18.750
So there are areas,

00:26:18.750 --> 00:26:23.750
for example, the orbitofrontal
cortex that we showed

00:26:23.820 --> 00:26:25.140
that if you stimulate there...

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:27.000
The orbitofrontal cortex
is a part of the brain

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:27.870
that's above the eyes.

00:26:27.870 --> 00:26:30.180
That's why they call it orbitofrontal,

00:26:30.180 --> 00:26:32.460
meaning it's above the eye or the orbit

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:35.823
and in the frontal lobe, and
it's this area right in here.

00:26:36.690 --> 00:26:38.790
It has really complex functions.

00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:41.670
It's really important
for learning and memory.

00:26:41.670 --> 00:26:43.530
But one of the things that we observed

00:26:43.530 --> 00:26:44.940
is when you stimulate in there,

00:26:44.940 --> 00:26:48.990
people tended to have a
reduction in their stress,

00:26:48.990 --> 00:26:53.490
and it was very much related
to their state of being,

00:26:53.490 --> 00:26:57.720
meaning that if someone was
already kind of feeling normal

00:26:57.720 --> 00:26:59.670
and you stimulate there,
it didn't do much.

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:02.010
But if someone was in
a very anxious state,

00:27:02.010 --> 00:27:03.870
it actually relieved that.

00:27:03.870 --> 00:27:06.420
And then we've seen the corollary of that

00:27:06.420 --> 00:27:07.740
which is true, too,

00:27:07.740 --> 00:27:09.810
which is that there are other areas

00:27:09.810 --> 00:27:12.630
like the amygdala or parts of the insula

00:27:12.630 --> 00:27:14.190
that if you stimulate,

00:27:14.190 --> 00:27:18.460
you can cause an acute temporary anxiety,

00:27:20.370 --> 00:27:21.900
a nervous feeling,

00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:23.550
or if you stimulate the insula,

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:26.280
people can have an acute
feeling of disgust.

00:27:26.280 --> 00:27:30.780
So, you know, the brain
has different functions

00:27:30.780 --> 00:27:34.740
and these different nodes that
help process the way we feel.

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:36.660
Certainly, I think that, to some degree,

00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:39.480
neuropsychiatric conditions
reflect an imbalance

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:42.873
of the electrical
activities in these areas.

00:27:44.760 --> 00:27:48.720
One of the things that was
something I will never forget

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:53.720
was taking care of a young woman
with uncontrolled seizures.

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:55.050
We call that epilepsy.

00:27:55.050 --> 00:27:56.460
It's a medical condition where someone

00:27:56.460 --> 00:27:59.670
has uncontrolled electrical
activity in the brain.

00:27:59.670 --> 00:28:01.680
Sometimes you can see that as convulsions

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:04.740
where people are shaking
and lose consciousness.

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:07.110
There are other kind of
seizures that people can have

00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:08.610
where they don't lose consciousness,

00:28:08.610 --> 00:28:11.560
but they can have experiences
that just come out of nowhere

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:15.000
just as a result of electrical activity

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:16.770
coming from the brain.

00:28:16.770 --> 00:28:20.790
And about six years ago, I
took care of a young woman

00:28:20.790 --> 00:28:25.260
who was diagnosed psychiatrically

00:28:25.260 --> 00:28:27.690
with anxiety disorder for several years.

00:28:27.690 --> 00:28:31.050
It turns out that it wasn't
really an anxiety disorder.

00:28:31.050 --> 00:28:33.960
It was actually that she
had underlying seizures,

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:37.110
an epilepsy activating a part of her brain

00:28:37.110 --> 00:28:40.260
that evokes, you know, anxious feelings.

00:28:40.260 --> 00:28:41.430
- How was that discovered?

00:28:41.430 --> 00:28:43.290
Because I know a lot of
people out there have anxiety.

00:28:43.290 --> 00:28:46.140
I mean, in the absence of a brain scan,

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:50.910
how or why would one suspect
that maybe they have a tumor

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:53.700
or some other condition

00:28:53.700 --> 00:28:55.740
that was causing those
neurons to become hyperactive?

00:28:55.740 --> 00:28:56.970
- Yeah, that's really important

00:28:56.970 --> 00:28:58.950
because so many people have anxiety,

00:28:58.950 --> 00:29:01.980
and the vast, vast majority
are not having that

00:29:01.980 --> 00:29:04.170
because they're having
seizures in the brain.

00:29:04.170 --> 00:29:06.960
I think one of the ways
that this was diagnosed

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:09.960
was that the nature

00:29:09.960 --> 00:29:12.960
of when she was having these panic attacks

00:29:12.960 --> 00:29:14.910
was not triggered by anything.

00:29:14.910 --> 00:29:17.550
They would just happen spontaneously.

00:29:17.550 --> 00:29:19.530
And that's what can happen
with seizures sometimes.

00:29:19.530 --> 00:29:21.300
They just come out of nowhere.

00:29:21.300 --> 00:29:23.730
We don't fully understand
what can trigger them,

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:24.780
but they weren't things

00:29:24.780 --> 00:29:28.380
that were typically anxiety-provoking.

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:31.590
This is something that just
happened all of a sudden.

00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:35.430
And because you brought it up,

00:29:35.430 --> 00:29:38.640
this is not something that
you can see on an MRI.

00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:42.150
We could not see and look at
the structure of her brain,

00:29:42.150 --> 00:29:45.180
with an MRI, that she was having seizures.

00:29:45.180 --> 00:29:48.000
The only way that we
could actually prove this

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:51.600
was actually putting
electrodes into her brain

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:56.600
and proving that these
attacks that she was having

00:29:56.850 --> 00:30:00.390
were localized to a part
called the amygdala,

00:30:00.390 --> 00:30:03.450
it's a medial part of the
temporal lobe, which is here,

00:30:03.450 --> 00:30:06.720
and associating the electrical activity

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:08.310
that we were seeing on those electrodes

00:30:08.310 --> 00:30:10.830
with the symptoms that she had,

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:13.320
and she ultimately
needed a kind of surgery

00:30:13.320 --> 00:30:18.317
where she was awake in
order to remove this safely.

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:21.330
- Speaking of epilepsy,

00:30:21.330 --> 00:30:23.400
a number of people out there have epilepsy

00:30:23.400 --> 00:30:25.140
or know people who do.

00:30:25.140 --> 00:30:28.230
Are the drugs for epilepsy satisfactory?

00:30:28.230 --> 00:30:30.090
You know, I think about
things like Depakote,

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:32.040
you know, and adjusting the excitation

00:30:32.040 --> 00:30:33.000
and inhibition of the brain.

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:35.880
I mean, are there good drugs for epilepsy?

00:30:35.880 --> 00:30:37.680
We know there are not great drugs

00:30:37.680 --> 00:30:39.180
for a lot of other conditions.

00:30:40.260 --> 00:30:44.580
And how often does one need neurosurgery

00:30:44.580 --> 00:30:45.660
in order to treat epilepsy?

00:30:45.660 --> 00:30:49.530
Or can it be treated most
often just using pharmacology?

00:30:49.530 --> 00:30:50.670
- Yeah, great question.

00:30:50.670 --> 00:30:55.350
Well, a lot of people have seizures

00:30:55.350 --> 00:30:56.640
that can be completely controlled

00:30:56.640 --> 00:30:58.743
by their medications, a lot.

00:30:59.700 --> 00:31:03.210
But there's about a one-third
of people who have epilepsy,

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:07.540
which we define as anyone who's
had three or more seizures

00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:10.980
that, you know, about a third of them

00:31:10.980 --> 00:31:13.320
actually don't have control

00:31:13.320 --> 00:31:16.203
with all of the modern
medications that we have nowadays.

00:31:17.160 --> 00:31:18.570
And some of the data suggests

00:31:18.570 --> 00:31:22.740
that if you have two or three medications,

00:31:22.740 --> 00:31:24.210
it actually doesn't matter necessarily

00:31:24.210 --> 00:31:27.540
which of the anti-seizure
medications it is,

00:31:27.540 --> 00:31:28.890
but there is data suggests

00:31:28.890 --> 00:31:32.040
if you've just tried two or three,

00:31:32.040 --> 00:31:35.760
the fourth, fifth, sixth, and beyond

00:31:35.760 --> 00:31:38.010
is not likely to help control it.

00:31:38.010 --> 00:31:41.340
So we are in a situation, unfortunately,

00:31:41.340 --> 00:31:44.610
where a lot of the medications
are great for some people,

00:31:44.610 --> 00:31:47.460
but for another subset,
they can't control it,

00:31:47.460 --> 00:31:49.500
and it comes from a
particular part of the brain.

00:31:49.500 --> 00:31:53.730
Now, fortunately, in that subset,

00:31:53.730 --> 00:31:56.010
there's another part of that group

00:31:56.010 --> 00:31:57.690
that can benefit from a surgery

00:31:57.690 --> 00:32:00.390
that actually either removes
that part of the brain,

00:32:00.390 --> 00:32:02.640
and nowadays, we'll use stimulators now

00:32:02.640 --> 00:32:06.000
to sometimes put electrical stimulation

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:08.400
in that part of the brain
to help reduce the seizures.

00:32:08.400 --> 00:32:10.830
- And you said a third
of people with epilepsy

00:32:10.830 --> 00:32:12.330
might need neurosurgery?

00:32:12.330 --> 00:32:14.010
- Well, what I mean by that

00:32:14.010 --> 00:32:16.380
is, like, they continue to have seizures

00:32:16.380 --> 00:32:18.540
that are not controlled
by all medications,

00:32:18.540 --> 00:32:21.390
and there's going to be
another subset of those

00:32:21.390 --> 00:32:24.810
that may benefit from a surgery.

00:32:24.810 --> 00:32:28.200
It's probably not that whole
third, it's a subset of that.

00:32:28.200 --> 00:32:29.790
It's just to say that epilepsy

00:32:29.790 --> 00:32:31.710
can be really hard to get fixed.

00:32:31.710 --> 00:32:36.150
And for people where the
seizures come from one spot

00:32:36.150 --> 00:32:39.483
or, you know, an area,
then surgery can do great.

00:32:40.460 --> 00:32:42.330
If it comes from multiple areas

00:32:42.330 --> 00:32:43.530
or if it comes from the whole brain,

00:32:43.530 --> 00:32:46.200
then we have to think about
other methods to control it.

00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:49.410
Fortunately, nowadays,
there's actually other ways.

00:32:49.410 --> 00:32:50.520
Surgery now, to us,

00:32:50.520 --> 00:32:53.463
doesn't just mean removing
part of the brain.

00:32:54.660 --> 00:32:57.180
Half of what we do now is use stimulators

00:32:57.180 --> 00:32:58.500
that modulate the state of the brain

00:32:58.500 --> 00:33:00.333
that can help reduce the seizures.

00:33:01.170 --> 00:33:04.740
- I've heard before
that the ketogenic diet

00:33:04.740 --> 00:33:09.510
was originally formulated
in order to treat epilepsy

00:33:09.510 --> 00:33:11.430
and, in particular, in kids.

00:33:11.430 --> 00:33:15.600
Is that true, and why would
being in a ketogenic state

00:33:15.600 --> 00:33:18.690
with low blood glucose reduce seizures?

00:33:18.690 --> 00:33:19.950
- That's a great question.

00:33:19.950 --> 00:33:22.530
And to be honest, I don't know actually

00:33:22.530 --> 00:33:25.680
if it was originally
designed to treat seizures,

00:33:25.680 --> 00:33:29.040
but I can tell you for
sure that for some people,

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:30.810
just like with some medications,

00:33:30.810 --> 00:33:32.580
it can be a life-changing thing.

00:33:32.580 --> 00:33:36.540
It can completely change the
way that the brain works.

00:33:36.540 --> 00:33:39.660
And it's not something
that's for everybody,

00:33:39.660 --> 00:33:41.400
but for some people, there's no question,

00:33:41.400 --> 00:33:43.710
and it has some very beneficial effects.

00:33:43.710 --> 00:33:45.570
I think it's to be determined still,

00:33:45.570 --> 00:33:48.840
like why and how that works.

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:50.850
- I've heard similar things
about the ketogenic diet

00:33:50.850 --> 00:33:53.250
for people with Alzheimer's dementia,

00:33:53.250 --> 00:33:57.630
that there's nothing particularly relevant

00:33:57.630 --> 00:33:59.880
about ketosis to Alzheimer's per se,

00:33:59.880 --> 00:34:03.000
but because Alzheimer's changes the way

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:06.060
that neurons metabolize energy

00:34:06.060 --> 00:34:07.980
that shifting to an alternate fuel source

00:34:07.980 --> 00:34:09.717
can sometimes make people feel better,

00:34:09.717 --> 00:34:11.370
and so a number of
people are now trying it.

00:34:11.370 --> 00:34:16.230
But it's not as if blood
glucose and having carbohydrates

00:34:16.230 --> 00:34:17.640
is causing Alzheimer's.

00:34:17.640 --> 00:34:18.960
And people get confused often

00:34:18.960 --> 00:34:21.630
that just because something can help

00:34:21.630 --> 00:34:24.750
doesn't mean that the
opposite is harming somebody.

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:26.610
So I find this really interesting.

00:34:26.610 --> 00:34:28.500
Sometime I'll check back with
you about what's happening

00:34:28.500 --> 00:34:31.320
in terms of ketogenic diets and epilepsy.

00:34:31.320 --> 00:34:34.890
But you said that in
some cases, it can help.

00:34:34.890 --> 00:34:36.180
Has that observation been made

00:34:36.180 --> 00:34:38.100
both for children and for adults?

00:34:38.100 --> 00:34:39.360
Because I thought that, originally,

00:34:39.360 --> 00:34:41.580
the ketogenic diet for epilepsy

00:34:41.580 --> 00:34:44.040
was really for pediatric epilepsy.

00:34:44.040 --> 00:34:44.880
- Yeah, that's right.

00:34:44.880 --> 00:34:49.470
So a lot of its focus has really
been on kids with epilepsy,

00:34:49.470 --> 00:34:52.230
but certainly it's a safe thing to try,

00:34:52.230 --> 00:34:54.900
so a lot of adults, you
know, will try it as well.

00:34:54.900 --> 00:34:55.830
- Interesting.

