00:36:10.350 --> 00:36:14.250
I'm curious about epilepsy
for another reason.

00:36:14.250 --> 00:36:16.470
I was taught that epilepsy is an imbalance

00:36:16.470 --> 00:36:18.968
in the excitation and
inhibition in the brain.

00:36:18.968 --> 00:36:20.400
So you think about these electrical storms

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:22.140
that give people either grand mal,

00:36:22.140 --> 00:36:25.710
you know, shaking and kind of convulsions.

00:36:25.710 --> 00:36:27.780
But years ago, I was reading a book,

00:36:27.780 --> 00:36:29.190
a wonderful book actually,

00:36:29.190 --> 00:36:31.530
called "Einstein in
Love" by Dennis Overbye.

00:36:31.530 --> 00:36:36.530
It was about Einstein and
I guess his personal life.

00:36:36.690 --> 00:36:38.070
People who knew him

00:36:38.070 --> 00:36:40.650
claimed that he would
sometimes walk along,

00:36:40.650 --> 00:36:42.870
and then every once in
a while would just stop

00:36:42.870 --> 00:36:44.190
and kind of stare off into space

00:36:44.190 --> 00:36:47.400
for anywhere from a minute
to three to five minutes,

00:36:47.400 --> 00:36:51.750
and it was speculated that
he had absence seizures.

00:36:51.750 --> 00:36:53.340
What is an absence seizure?

00:36:53.340 --> 00:36:56.730
And the reason I ask is I
occasionally will be walking along

00:36:56.730 --> 00:36:58.680
and I'll be thinking about
something and I'll stop.

00:36:58.680 --> 00:37:03.300
But in my mind, I'm
thinking during that time,

00:37:03.300 --> 00:37:05.430
but I realize that if I were
to see myself from the outside,

00:37:05.430 --> 00:37:07.980
it might appear that I
was just kind of absent.

00:37:07.980 --> 00:37:10.020
What is an absence seizure?

00:37:10.020 --> 00:37:12.750
Because it's so strikingly
different in its description

00:37:12.750 --> 00:37:15.660
from, say, a grand mal convulsive seizure.

00:37:15.660 --> 00:37:19.800
- Sure, well, like I mentioned before,

00:37:19.800 --> 00:37:23.070
depending on how the seizure
activity spreads in the brain

00:37:23.070 --> 00:37:26.280
or how it actually propagates,

00:37:26.280 --> 00:37:27.990
if it stays in one particular spot

00:37:27.990 --> 00:37:29.670
and doesn't spread to the entire brain,

00:37:29.670 --> 00:37:32.670
it can have really
different manifestation.

00:37:32.670 --> 00:37:34.560
It can represent really differently.

00:37:34.560 --> 00:37:37.530
So absence seizure is just one category

00:37:37.530 --> 00:37:38.700
of different kind of seizures

00:37:38.700 --> 00:37:42.180
where you can lose
consciousness basically,

00:37:42.180 --> 00:37:45.960
and what I mean by that is
that you're not fully aware

00:37:45.960 --> 00:37:48.150
of what's going on in
your environment, okay?

00:37:48.150 --> 00:37:49.710
So you're sort of taken offline

00:37:49.710 --> 00:37:52.200
temporarily from consciousness,

00:37:52.200 --> 00:37:55.470
but you could still be,
for example, standing,

00:37:55.470 --> 00:37:58.050
and to people who are
not paying attention,

00:37:58.050 --> 00:38:00.633
they may not even be aware
that that's happening.

00:38:01.530 --> 00:38:03.480
- What are some other types of seizures?

00:38:03.480 --> 00:38:07.830
- Well, you know, I think
some of the other kinds,

00:38:07.830 --> 00:38:09.990
the classic ones are
temporal lobe seizures.

00:38:09.990 --> 00:38:13.410
So these are ones that come
from the medial structures

00:38:13.410 --> 00:38:15.720
like the amygdala and hippocampus.

00:38:15.720 --> 00:38:18.780
Oftentimes people, when they
have seizures coming from that,

00:38:18.780 --> 00:38:23.780
they may taste something very
unusual like a metallic taste

00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:27.420
or smell something like
the smell of burning toast,

00:38:27.420 --> 00:38:28.473
something like that.

00:38:29.790 --> 00:38:32.220
There are some people, with
temporal lobe seizures,

00:38:32.220 --> 00:38:33.360
will have deja vu.

00:38:33.360 --> 00:38:34.650
They will have that experience

00:38:34.650 --> 00:38:36.720
that you've been somewhere before,

00:38:36.720 --> 00:38:39.870
but that's just a
precursor to the seizure.

00:38:39.870 --> 00:38:43.170
And it just highlights that
when people have seizures

00:38:43.170 --> 00:38:44.700
coming from these areas,

00:38:44.700 --> 00:38:48.690
they sometimes hijack what
that part of the brain

00:38:48.690 --> 00:38:49.530
is really for.

00:38:49.530 --> 00:38:52.740
So the amygdala and
hippocampus, for example,

00:38:52.740 --> 00:38:55.170
are really important
for learning and memory.

00:38:55.170 --> 00:38:57.630
It's not surprising that when
people have seizures there

00:38:57.630 --> 00:39:00.780
that it can evoke a feeling of deja vu

00:39:00.780 --> 00:39:03.843
or that it can evoke a feeling of anxiety.

00:39:05.310 --> 00:39:09.393
And the areas that are right
next to it, for example,

00:39:10.920 --> 00:39:14.790
these areas are really
important for processing smell.

00:39:14.790 --> 00:39:17.400
So these areas are
right next to each other

00:39:17.400 --> 00:39:20.130
so you can have these kind
of complex set of symptoms,

00:39:20.130 --> 00:39:23.970
the weird taste, the smell of toast,

00:39:23.970 --> 00:39:25.290
and then a feeling of deja vu,

00:39:25.290 --> 00:39:28.140
that's classic for a
temporal lobe seizure,

00:39:28.140 --> 00:39:29.463
and it's because those parts of the brain

00:39:29.463 --> 00:39:32.363
that process those functions
are right next to each other.

00:39:33.750 --> 00:39:37.050
- I'm told that I've
had nocturnal seizures,

00:39:37.050 --> 00:39:38.940
and I've woken up sometimes from sleep

00:39:38.940 --> 00:39:41.190
having felt as if I was
having a convulsion,

00:39:41.190 --> 00:39:44.250
the sort of sense of buzzing
in the back of the head.

00:39:44.250 --> 00:39:46.733
This happened to me two
or three times in college.

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:50.610
Well, I woke up and my
girlfriend was very distraught,

00:39:50.610 --> 00:39:51.720
like, "You were having a seizure."

00:39:51.720 --> 00:39:53.770
I was having full convulsion in my sleep.

00:39:55.230 --> 00:39:57.060
Is that correct?

00:39:57.060 --> 00:39:58.980
Is there such a thing
as nocturnal seizures?

00:39:58.980 --> 00:40:00.390
What do they reflect?

00:40:00.390 --> 00:40:02.460
They eventually stopped happening,

00:40:02.460 --> 00:40:04.950
and I couldn't tether them
to any kind of life event.

00:40:04.950 --> 00:40:07.470
I wasn't doing any kind of combat sport

00:40:07.470 --> 00:40:08.430
or anything at the time,

00:40:08.430 --> 00:40:11.250
I wasn't drinking alcohol much,

00:40:11.250 --> 00:40:12.850
it's never really been my thing.

00:40:14.220 --> 00:40:16.050
What are nocturnal seizures about?

00:40:16.050 --> 00:40:17.730
- [Eddie] Oh, well-

00:40:17.730 --> 00:40:18.822
- And do I need brain surgery?

00:40:18.822 --> 00:40:20.580
[Andrew laughs]
[Eddie laughs]

00:40:20.580 --> 00:40:23.640
- Nocturnal seizures
are just another form.

00:40:23.640 --> 00:40:25.710
Like, again, epilepsy and seizures

00:40:25.710 --> 00:40:27.210
can have so many different forms

00:40:27.210 --> 00:40:30.150
and not just, like, where in the brain,

00:40:30.150 --> 00:40:31.770
but also when they happen.

00:40:31.770 --> 00:40:35.640
And there are some people
who, for whatever reason,

00:40:35.640 --> 00:40:38.490
it's very timed to the circadian rhythm.

00:40:38.490 --> 00:40:40.140
It's actually not just happening at night,

00:40:40.140 --> 00:40:41.490
but a certain period at night

00:40:41.490 --> 00:40:44.400
when people are in a
certain stage of sleep

00:40:44.400 --> 00:40:45.840
that the brain is in a state

00:40:45.840 --> 00:40:49.830
that it's vulnerable to having a seizure,

00:40:49.830 --> 00:40:53.010
and so that's basically
just one form of that.

00:40:53.010 --> 00:40:55.080
Again, it's not just about
where it's coming from,

00:40:55.080 --> 00:40:57.600
but also when it's happening
and how that's timed

00:40:57.600 --> 00:41:00.360
with other things that are
happening with the body.

00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:01.193
- Interesting.

00:41:01.193 --> 00:41:02.430
Well, it eventually stopped happening

00:41:02.430 --> 00:41:04.113
so I stopped worrying about it.

00:41:05.250 --> 00:41:07.203
I haven't had seizures since.

00:41:08.160 --> 00:41:10.410
Returning to speech and language,

00:41:10.410 --> 00:41:13.770
when I was getting weaned in neuroscience,

00:41:13.770 --> 00:41:15.360
I learned that we have
an area of the brain

00:41:15.360 --> 00:41:16.560
for producing speech

00:41:16.560 --> 00:41:19.460
and we have an area of the
brain for comprehending speech.

00:41:20.910 --> 00:41:22.080
What's the story there?

00:41:22.080 --> 00:41:23.280
Is it still true

00:41:23.280 --> 00:41:26.190
that we have a Broca's
and a Wernicke's area?

00:41:26.190 --> 00:41:28.380
Those are names of
neurologists, presumably,

00:41:28.380 --> 00:41:29.580
or neurosurgeons that discovered

00:41:29.580 --> 00:41:31.230
these different brain areas.

00:41:31.230 --> 00:41:32.490
Maybe you could familiarize us

00:41:32.490 --> 00:41:35.970
with some of the sort of textbook version

00:41:35.970 --> 00:41:39.060
of how speech and language
are organized in the brain,

00:41:39.060 --> 00:41:40.980
maybe share with us a little
bit of the lesion studies

00:41:40.980 --> 00:41:42.330
that led to that understanding,

00:41:42.330 --> 00:41:44.490
and then I would love to hear a bit

00:41:44.490 --> 00:41:46.230
about what your laboratory is discovering

00:41:46.230 --> 00:41:48.660
about how things are actually organized,

00:41:48.660 --> 00:41:51.780
because from some discussions
you and I have had

00:41:51.780 --> 00:41:52.890
over the last year or so,

00:41:52.890 --> 00:41:55.050
it seems like, well, let's just be blunt,

00:41:55.050 --> 00:41:57.570
it seems that much of what
we know from the textbooks

00:41:57.570 --> 00:41:59.640
could be wrong.

00:41:59.640 --> 00:42:02.910
- Well, I love that question

00:42:02.910 --> 00:42:07.050
because, for me, it's very
central to the research we do,

00:42:07.050 --> 00:42:08.190
and it's where the intersection

00:42:08.190 --> 00:42:11.940
between what we do in the
laboratory in our research

00:42:11.940 --> 00:42:15.033
interfaces with what I see in patients.

00:42:16.080 --> 00:42:18.570
And one of the things that fascinated me

00:42:18.570 --> 00:42:20.430
early on in my medical training

00:42:20.430 --> 00:42:23.400
was in doing some of these brain mapping

00:42:23.400 --> 00:42:25.020
or watching them with my mentor

00:42:25.020 --> 00:42:28.890
or taking care of patients that
had, you know, brain tumors

00:42:28.890 --> 00:42:30.780
in a certain part of the brain

00:42:30.780 --> 00:42:33.300
was that, a lot of times,
what I was seeing in a patient

00:42:33.300 --> 00:42:36.333
did not correlate with what I
was taught in medical school.

00:42:37.380 --> 00:42:40.260
And, you know, some people will think,

00:42:40.260 --> 00:42:43.080
well, this might be an exception,

00:42:43.080 --> 00:42:44.787
but after you see it for a couple times

00:42:44.787 --> 00:42:47.253
and if you're kind of
interested in this problem,

00:42:49.380 --> 00:42:51.600
you know, it poses a serious challenge

00:42:51.600 --> 00:42:53.580
to what you've learned

00:42:53.580 --> 00:42:57.060
and how you think about
how these things operate.

00:42:57.060 --> 00:42:59.790
And that actually got me really interested

00:42:59.790 --> 00:43:01.650
in trying to figure this out

00:43:01.650 --> 00:43:04.050
because, earlier, we talked about

00:43:04.050 --> 00:43:07.080
just this extraordinary
thing that the brain is doing

00:43:07.080 --> 00:43:09.780
to create words and sentences,

00:43:09.780 --> 00:43:13.230
and that's the process by
which I'm getting ideas out

00:43:13.230 --> 00:43:15.900
from my mind into yours.

00:43:15.900 --> 00:43:16.950
It's an incredible thing, right?

00:43:16.950 --> 00:43:19.083
It's the basis of communication,

00:43:21.180 --> 00:43:25.020
high information communication
between two individuals

00:43:25.020 --> 00:43:27.210
that's really unique to humans.

00:43:27.210 --> 00:43:32.210
So in historical times,

00:43:33.300 --> 00:43:36.090
how this works has been very controversial

00:43:36.090 --> 00:43:38.703
from day one of neuroscience.

00:43:41.816 --> 00:43:45.060
A long time ago, people
thought the bumps on your head

00:43:45.060 --> 00:43:49.410
corresponded to the different
faculties of the mind.

00:43:49.410 --> 00:43:51.360
So for example, if you had a bump here,

00:43:51.360 --> 00:43:54.090
it might be corresponding to intelligence

00:43:54.090 --> 00:43:56.730
or another one over
here, you know, to vision

00:43:56.730 --> 00:43:58.860
and these kind of things.

00:43:58.860 --> 00:44:01.140
That's what we nowadays call phrenology,

00:44:01.140 --> 00:44:05.130
and that was kind of the starting point.

00:44:05.130 --> 00:44:07.260
A lot of that has been,
of course, debunked,

00:44:07.260 --> 00:44:08.910
but when you see those little statues

00:44:08.910 --> 00:44:12.630
of different brain
partitions on someone's head,

00:44:12.630 --> 00:44:15.360
that's essentially how people

00:44:15.360 --> 00:44:18.450
were thinking about how
the brain worked back then,

00:44:18.450 --> 00:44:20.103
a couple hundred years ago.

00:44:21.540 --> 00:44:25.350
Modern neuroscience began when,

00:44:25.350 --> 00:44:27.030
actually, it was very much related

00:44:27.030 --> 00:44:28.800
to the discovery of language.

00:44:28.800 --> 00:44:30.390
So modern neuroscience,

00:44:30.390 --> 00:44:34.020
meaning moving beyond this idea
that the bumps on the scalp

00:44:34.020 --> 00:44:36.180
corresponded to the faculties of the mind,

00:44:36.180 --> 00:44:37.013
but there were things

00:44:37.013 --> 00:44:38.910
that actually were in
the brain themselves,

00:44:38.910 --> 00:44:40.620
and they weren't corresponding to things

00:44:40.620 --> 00:44:42.690
that you could see superficially,

00:44:42.690 --> 00:44:46.290
like on the scalp or externally,

00:44:46.290 --> 00:44:48.390
that it was something
about the brain itself.

00:44:48.390 --> 00:44:50.190
I mean, it seems so obvious now,

00:44:50.190 --> 00:44:55.190
but back then, this was the
big academic, you know, debate.

00:44:55.200 --> 00:44:58.260
And the first observation

00:44:58.260 --> 00:45:02.070
that I think was really
impactful in the area of language

00:45:02.070 --> 00:45:05.940
was an observation by
a French neurosurgeon

00:45:05.940 --> 00:45:07.023
named Pierre Broca.

00:45:07.890 --> 00:45:11.040
And what he observed
was that in a patient,

00:45:11.040 --> 00:45:12.180
not that he did surgery in,

00:45:12.180 --> 00:45:15.512
but that he had seen and taken care of,

00:45:15.512 --> 00:45:17.820
that the person couldn't talk.

00:45:17.820 --> 00:45:21.240
And, in particular, they
called this individual Tan

00:45:21.240 --> 00:45:26.240
because the only words that
he could produce was tan, tan.

00:45:26.430 --> 00:45:27.263
For the most part,

00:45:27.263 --> 00:45:29.400
he could generally
understand the kind of things

00:45:29.400 --> 00:45:32.040
that people were asking him about,

00:45:32.040 --> 00:45:35.430
but the only thing that he
could utter from his mouth

00:45:35.430 --> 00:45:37.893
were these words, tan, tan.

00:45:38.760 --> 00:45:41.430
And what eventually had happened

00:45:41.430 --> 00:45:43.473
was this individual passed away,

00:45:44.490 --> 00:45:47.160
and the way that neuroscience
was done back then

00:45:47.160 --> 00:45:49.890
was basically to wait until that happened

00:45:49.890 --> 00:45:51.180
and then to remove the brain

00:45:51.180 --> 00:45:54.300
and to see what part of
the brain was affected

00:45:54.300 --> 00:45:56.580
in this patient that they called Tan.

00:45:56.580 --> 00:46:00.480
And what Broca found was
that there was a part

00:46:00.480 --> 00:46:02.253
in the left frontal lobe,

00:46:03.510 --> 00:46:05.820
so the frontal lobe is this
area like I described earlier,

00:46:05.820 --> 00:46:09.960
which is, you know, up
behind our forehead, up here,

00:46:09.960 --> 00:46:14.960
and in the back of that frontal lobe,

00:46:15.810 --> 00:46:20.810
he claimed that this was
the seat of articulation

00:46:21.060 --> 00:46:21.893
in the brain.

00:46:21.893 --> 00:46:23.640
He literally used something
like that in French,

00:46:23.640 --> 00:46:25.410
the seat of articulation,

00:46:25.410 --> 00:46:27.150
meaning that this is the part of the brain

00:46:27.150 --> 00:46:30.663
that is responsible for
us to generate words.

00:46:31.830 --> 00:46:36.830
About 50 years later, the
story becomes more complicated

00:46:37.050 --> 00:46:39.753
with a German neurologist
named Carl Wernicke.

00:46:41.070 --> 00:46:43.170
And what Wernicke described

00:46:43.170 --> 00:46:46.620
was a different set of symptoms

00:46:46.620 --> 00:46:50.910
in patients that he observed
a different phenomenon

00:46:50.910 --> 00:46:55.830
where people could produce words,

00:46:55.830 --> 00:46:56.993
but a lot of the words,

00:46:56.993 --> 00:46:59.250
and they were fluent in the sense

00:46:59.250 --> 00:47:04.250
that, like, they sound like
they could be real words

00:47:04.560 --> 00:47:07.470
but from a different
language, for example.

00:47:07.470 --> 00:47:11.661
And some of us call that,
like, word salad or jargon.

00:47:11.661 --> 00:47:14.160
They were essentially making up words,

00:47:14.160 --> 00:47:15.210
but it was not intentional.

00:47:15.210 --> 00:47:17.670
It was just the way
that the words came out.

00:47:17.670 --> 00:47:21.000
But in addition to that, he
observed that these people

00:47:21.000 --> 00:47:24.930
also could not understand
what was being said to them.

00:47:24.930 --> 00:47:27.870
So we could be having a conversation,

00:47:27.870 --> 00:47:30.840
and I'd be asking you, "Am I a woman?"

00:47:30.840 --> 00:47:32.190
And you might nod your head,

00:47:32.190 --> 00:47:36.570
you know, just because you're
not processing the question.

00:47:36.570 --> 00:47:41.570
And so here are two observations.

00:47:43.020 --> 00:47:45.660
One is that the frontal lobe is important

00:47:45.660 --> 00:47:47.910
for articulating speech,

00:47:47.910 --> 00:47:51.150
creating the words and
expressing them fluently.

00:47:51.150 --> 00:47:52.710
And then a different part of the brain

00:47:52.710 --> 00:47:55.110
called the left temporal lobe,

00:47:55.110 --> 00:47:57.010
which is this area right above my ear,

00:47:59.910 --> 00:48:03.300
that is an area that I think was claimed

00:48:03.300 --> 00:48:05.970
to be really important for understanding.

00:48:05.970 --> 00:48:09.000
So the two major functions in language,

00:48:09.000 --> 00:48:11.880
to speak and to understand,

00:48:11.880 --> 00:48:13.290
were kind of pinned down to that,

00:48:13.290 --> 00:48:18.290
and we've had that basic
idea in the textbooks

00:48:18.570 --> 00:48:20.580
for, you know, over 200 years.

00:48:20.580 --> 00:48:21.990
- It's certainly what I was taught.

00:48:21.990 --> 00:48:22.823
- [Eddie] Is that right?

00:48:22.823 --> 00:48:24.990
- Oh, yeah, and certainly what we still,

00:48:24.990 --> 00:48:27.450
we still teach undergraduates,
graduate students,

00:48:27.450 --> 00:48:29.220
and medical students that.

00:48:29.220 --> 00:48:31.530
- Well, that's what I learned,
too, in medical school.

00:48:31.530 --> 00:48:33.810
And what I saw in reality

00:48:33.810 --> 00:48:35.250
when I started taking care of patients

00:48:35.250 --> 00:48:37.263
was that it's not so simple.

00:48:38.790 --> 00:48:40.860
In fact, part of it is
fundamentally wrong.

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:42.660
So just in a nutshell,

00:48:42.660 --> 00:48:46.230
nowadays, after, you know,
looking at this very carefully

00:48:46.230 --> 00:48:47.490
over hundreds of patients,

00:48:47.490 --> 00:48:49.800
we've shown that surgeries,

00:48:49.800 --> 00:48:52.893
for example, in the posterior
part of the frontal lobe,

00:48:53.850 --> 00:48:56.730
a lot of times, people have
no problem talking at all

00:48:56.730 --> 00:48:59.910
whatsoever after those kind of surgeries,

00:48:59.910 --> 00:49:01.860
and that it's a different
part of the brain

00:49:01.860 --> 00:49:04.473
that we call the precentral gyrus.

00:49:05.310 --> 00:49:07.950
The precentral gyrus
is a part of the brain

00:49:07.950 --> 00:49:12.090
that is intimately associated
with the motor cortex.

00:49:12.090 --> 00:49:13.233
The motor cortex is the part of the brain

00:49:13.233 --> 00:49:16.740
that has a map of your entire body

00:49:16.740 --> 00:49:19.380
so that it has a part that
corresponds to your feet,

00:49:19.380 --> 00:49:21.540
it has a part that
corresponds to your hands.

00:49:21.540 --> 00:49:22.800
But then there's another part

00:49:22.800 --> 00:49:25.440
that comes out more laterally
on the side of the brain

00:49:25.440 --> 00:49:29.550
that corresponds to your
lips, your jaw, your larynx,

00:49:29.550 --> 00:49:33.630
and we have seen that when
patients have surgeries

00:49:33.630 --> 00:49:35.610
or injuries to that part of the brain,

00:49:35.610 --> 00:49:37.500
it actually can really interrupt language,

00:49:37.500 --> 00:49:38.370
so it's not as simple

00:49:38.370 --> 00:49:41.370
as just moving the muscles
of the vocal tract,

00:49:41.370 --> 00:49:43.620
but it's also important for formulating

00:49:43.620 --> 00:49:45.420
and expressing words.

00:49:45.420 --> 00:49:49.050
So that's Broca's area

00:49:49.050 --> 00:49:52.260
that I think the field now recognizes

00:49:52.260 --> 00:49:53.310
not just because of our work,

00:49:53.310 --> 00:49:55.290
but many other people
that have studied this

00:49:55.290 --> 00:49:56.910
in stroke and beyond,

00:49:56.910 --> 00:49:58.500
is that the idea

00:49:58.500 --> 00:50:02.490
that that is the basis of
speaking in Broca's area

00:50:02.490 --> 00:50:05.547
is fundamentally wrong right now,

00:50:05.547 --> 00:50:08.100
and we have to figure out
how to correct the textbooks

00:50:08.100 --> 00:50:09.223
that we kind of understand that

00:50:09.223 --> 00:50:13.290
so that we can continue to make progress.

00:50:13.290 --> 00:50:15.780
Now, in terms of the other major area

00:50:15.780 --> 00:50:18.780
that we call Wernicke's area
in the posterior temporal lobe,

00:50:20.280 --> 00:50:21.873
that has held,

00:50:24.990 --> 00:50:27.750
I think, quite legitimately for some time.

00:50:27.750 --> 00:50:32.280
So that is an area that you
have to be super careful

00:50:32.280 --> 00:50:34.230
when you do surgery there.

00:50:34.230 --> 00:50:36.210
That's an area where,

00:50:36.210 --> 00:50:40.170
if you have a mistake there
and you cause a stroke

00:50:40.170 --> 00:50:42.840
or you remove too much of the tumor there,

00:50:42.840 --> 00:50:44.403
you go too far beyond it,

00:50:45.300 --> 00:50:48.660
then the person can be
really, really hurt.

00:50:48.660 --> 00:50:51.600
Like, they'll have a
condition that we call aphasia

00:50:51.600 --> 00:50:54.000
where they may not be
able to understand words,

00:50:54.000 --> 00:50:57.010
they may not be able to remember the word

00:50:58.007 --> 00:50:59.160
that they're trying to say.

00:50:59.160 --> 00:51:00.660
They know what they're trying to say,

00:51:00.660 --> 00:51:03.120
but they can't remember the precise word

00:51:03.120 --> 00:51:05.580
that goes with the object that
they're trying to think of.

00:51:05.580 --> 00:51:07.950
They may even produce words

00:51:07.950 --> 00:51:12.060
that I described before are
like word salad or very jargony.

00:51:12.060 --> 00:51:15.633
So, you know, they might say
something like tamiranai.

00:51:17.490 --> 00:51:18.930
That's not a real word,

00:51:18.930 --> 00:51:21.420
but it sounds like it could be, you know?

00:51:21.420 --> 00:51:23.250
And that's just because
that part of the brain

00:51:23.250 --> 00:51:27.510
has some role not just in
understanding what we hear,

00:51:27.510 --> 00:51:30.150
but also actually has
a really important role

00:51:30.150 --> 00:51:32.370
in sending the commands to
different parts of the brain

00:51:32.370 --> 00:51:34.770
to control what we say.

00:51:34.770 --> 00:51:38.610
- Not long ago, you and me

00:51:38.610 --> 00:51:41.010
and my good friend Rick Rubin

00:51:41.010 --> 00:51:45.030
were having a conversation
about medicine and science,

00:51:45.030 --> 00:51:47.587
and Rick asked the question,

00:51:47.587 --> 00:51:50.820
"What percentage of what you learned

00:51:50.820 --> 00:51:55.820
in graduate and/or medical
school do you think is correct?"

00:51:57.030 --> 00:51:58.930
And you had a very interesting answer.

00:51:59.940 --> 00:52:01.290
Would you share it with us?

00:52:02.760 --> 00:52:04.530
- I don't know. I don't
remember the exact.

00:52:04.530 --> 00:52:05.940
But I would say

00:52:05.940 --> 00:52:10.940
that with regard to the
brain in particular,

00:52:11.970 --> 00:52:13.950
I would say about 50%

00:52:13.950 --> 00:52:18.030
gets it right and accurate and is helpful,

00:52:18.030 --> 00:52:21.240
but another 50% is just the approximation

00:52:21.240 --> 00:52:24.360
and oversimplification of what's going on.

00:52:24.360 --> 00:52:25.470
The example that we talked about,

00:52:25.470 --> 00:52:27.060
language is just an example of that.

00:52:27.060 --> 00:52:31.770
It's just there are things
that make it easier to learn

00:52:31.770 --> 00:52:34.623
and easier to teach and
easier to even think about,

00:52:35.730 --> 00:52:37.830
and that's probably why
we continue teaching

00:52:37.830 --> 00:52:39.330
in the way that we do.

00:52:39.330 --> 00:52:41.430
But I think as time goes on,

00:52:41.430 --> 00:52:44.910
the complexity of reality
of how the brain works

00:52:44.910 --> 00:52:47.610
is, well, first of all,

00:52:47.610 --> 00:52:49.620
we're still trying to figure it out,

00:52:49.620 --> 00:52:51.420
and second of all, it is complex

00:52:51.420 --> 00:52:54.210
and it's still incomplete story.

00:52:54.210 --> 00:52:55.043
- It's early days.

00:52:55.043 --> 00:52:57.600
And we'll get into some
of the technical advances

00:52:57.600 --> 00:53:01.170
that are allowing some
correction of the errors

00:53:01.170 --> 00:53:02.820
that the field has made.

00:53:02.820 --> 00:53:05.910
And, look, no disrespect
to the brain explorers

00:53:05.910 --> 00:53:07.020
that came before us,

00:53:07.020 --> 00:53:10.590
and the ones that come after
us will correct us, right?

00:53:10.590 --> 00:53:12.600
That's the way the game is played.

00:53:12.600 --> 00:53:13.800
But what I'm hearing

00:53:13.800 --> 00:53:16.080
is that there are certain
truths that people accept,

00:53:16.080 --> 00:53:18.450
and then there's about
half of the information

00:53:18.450 --> 00:53:19.980
that is still open for debate

00:53:19.980 --> 00:53:21.830
and maybe even for complete revision.

00:53:22.800 --> 00:53:25.410
One thing that I learned about language

00:53:25.410 --> 00:53:27.720
and the neural circuits
underlying language

00:53:27.720 --> 00:53:29.610
is that it's heavily lateralized,

00:53:29.610 --> 00:53:31.740
that these structures,
Broca's and Wernicke's

00:53:31.740 --> 00:53:32.850
and other structures in the brain

00:53:32.850 --> 00:53:35.940
responsible for speech and
comprehension of speech

00:53:35.940 --> 00:53:38.670
sit mainly on one side of the brain,

00:53:38.670 --> 00:53:42.060
but they do not have a
mirror representation

00:53:42.060 --> 00:53:44.010
or another equivalent area

00:53:44.010 --> 00:53:45.120
on the opposite side of the brain.

00:53:45.120 --> 00:53:50.120
And for those that haven't
poked around in a lot of brains,

00:53:50.130 --> 00:53:51.900
certainly you, Eddie,

00:53:51.900 --> 00:53:53.310
have done far more of that than I have,

00:53:53.310 --> 00:53:56.190
but I've done my fair
share in nonhuman species

00:53:56.190 --> 00:53:58.110
and a little bit in humans,

00:53:58.110 --> 00:53:59.310
almost every structure,

00:53:59.310 --> 00:54:02.910
almost every structure
has a matching structure

00:54:02.910 --> 00:54:03.960
on the other side of the brain,

00:54:03.960 --> 00:54:05.310
so when we say the hippocampus,

00:54:05.310 --> 00:54:07.230
we really mean two hippocampi,

00:54:07.230 --> 00:54:08.790
one on each side of the brain.

00:54:08.790 --> 00:54:11.640
But language, I was taught,
is heavily lateralized,

00:54:11.640 --> 00:54:13.500
that is, that there's only one.

00:54:13.500 --> 00:54:16.140
So that raises two
questions. One, is that true?

00:54:16.140 --> 00:54:20.520
And if it is true, then what
is the equivalent real estate

00:54:20.520 --> 00:54:22.620
on the opposite side of the brain doing

00:54:22.620 --> 00:54:24.030
if it's not doing the same function

00:54:24.030 --> 00:54:26.430
that the one on, say, the
left side is performing?

00:54:26.430 --> 00:54:27.660
- Well, that's one of those things

00:54:27.660 --> 00:54:31.350
that is, again, like
mostly true, not 100%.

00:54:31.350 --> 00:54:35.190
And what I mean by that
is that it's complicated.

00:54:35.190 --> 00:54:38.520
So for people who are right-handed,

00:54:38.520 --> 00:54:41.160
99% of the time, the
language part of the brain

00:54:41.160 --> 00:54:43.740
is on the left side.

00:54:43.740 --> 00:54:45.870
- And what is the equivalent brain area

00:54:45.870 --> 00:54:48.930
on the right side doing if
it's not doing language?

00:54:48.930 --> 00:54:50.400
- Well, you know, the
thing that's incredible

00:54:50.400 --> 00:54:52.590
is if you look at the right side

00:54:52.590 --> 00:54:53.670
and you look at it very carefully,

00:54:53.670 --> 00:54:55.740
either under an MRI

00:54:55.740 --> 00:54:57.270
or you actually look at the brain

00:54:57.270 --> 00:54:59.310
under slides in a microscope,

00:54:59.310 --> 00:55:00.900
it looks very, very similar.

00:55:00.900 --> 00:55:03.930
It's not identical, but it
looks very, very similar.

00:55:03.930 --> 00:55:06.930
All the gyri, which are
the bumps on the brain

00:55:06.930 --> 00:55:08.940
that, you know, have
the different contours

00:55:08.940 --> 00:55:10.770
and the valleys that we call sulci,

00:55:10.770 --> 00:55:12.540
those all look basically the same.

00:55:12.540 --> 00:55:17.250
Like, there is a mirror anatomy
on the left and right side,

00:55:17.250 --> 00:55:19.380
and so it's not been so clear

00:55:19.380 --> 00:55:22.240
what's so special actually
about the left side

00:55:24.055 --> 00:55:25.170
to house language.

00:55:25.170 --> 00:55:26.337
But what we do know,

00:55:26.337 --> 00:55:29.460
and this is what we use
all the time in assessing

00:55:29.460 --> 00:55:32.460
and figuring out, you
know, this before surgery,

00:55:32.460 --> 00:55:34.200
is if you're right-handed,

00:55:34.200 --> 00:55:37.020
99% of the time, the
language is going to be

00:55:37.020 --> 00:55:38.700
on the left side of the brain.

00:55:38.700 --> 00:55:40.710
- Is handedness genetic in any way?

00:55:40.710 --> 00:55:42.030
I mean, when I grew up,
- Yes.

00:55:42.030 --> 00:55:43.650
- a pen or a pencil or crayon

00:55:43.650 --> 00:55:46.770
was placed into my hand
presumably, or I started using...

00:55:46.770 --> 00:55:48.240
My father was left-handed,

00:55:48.240 --> 00:55:50.610
and then where he grew
up in South America,

00:55:50.610 --> 00:55:54.750
they forced him to force
himself to become right-handed.

00:55:54.750 --> 00:55:57.240
They actually used to restrict
the movement of his left hand

00:55:57.240 --> 00:55:59.400
so he was forced to write...

00:55:59.400 --> 00:56:02.550
And then you have hook
lefties and hook righties.

00:56:02.550 --> 00:56:04.770
And I know this is a deep dive

00:56:04.770 --> 00:56:05.700
and we probably don't want to go

00:56:05.700 --> 00:56:07.530
into every derivation of this,

00:56:07.530 --> 00:56:10.290
but so for somebody who's left-handed,

00:56:10.290 --> 00:56:12.570
naturally just starts
writing with the left hand,

00:56:12.570 --> 00:56:16.410
there's some genetic predisposition
to being left-handed?

00:56:16.410 --> 00:56:18.630
- Absolutely. No question about it.

00:56:18.630 --> 00:56:23.630
Handedness is not entirely
but strongly genetic.

00:56:23.730 --> 00:56:27.480
So there is something
that ties all of this,

00:56:27.480 --> 00:56:29.370
and what does handedness,
for example, have to do

00:56:29.370 --> 00:56:32.760
with the part of your brain
that controls language?

00:56:32.760 --> 00:56:35.790
Well, it turns out that the
parts that control the hand

00:56:35.790 --> 00:56:37.170
are very close to the areas

00:56:37.170 --> 00:56:39.990
that really are responsible
for the vocal tract.

00:56:39.990 --> 00:56:41.850
Again, part of the motor cortex

00:56:41.850 --> 00:56:45.000
and part of this brain area
called the precentral gyrus.

00:56:45.000 --> 00:56:46.680
And there are some theories

00:56:46.680 --> 00:56:48.573
that, because of their proximity,

00:56:49.470 --> 00:56:50.400
that these parts of the brain

00:56:50.400 --> 00:56:53.460
might develop together early in utero

00:56:53.460 --> 00:56:56.130
and they might have a head start
compared to the right side,

00:56:56.130 --> 00:56:57.870
and because they have a head start

00:56:57.870 --> 00:56:59.910
that things solidify there.

00:56:59.910 --> 00:57:02.940
This is one theory of why this happens.

00:57:02.940 --> 00:57:05.120
In people who are left-handed,

00:57:05.120 --> 00:57:07.980
it still turns out that
the vast majority of people

00:57:07.980 --> 00:57:09.630
have language on the left side,

00:57:09.630 --> 00:57:13.410
but it's not 99%, it's more like 70%.

00:57:13.410 --> 00:57:15.420
So if you're left-handed,

00:57:15.420 --> 00:57:18.750
it's still more likely that
the language part of your brain

00:57:18.750 --> 00:57:20.910
is going to be on the left side,

00:57:20.910 --> 00:57:24.600
but there's going to be a greater
proportion, maybe 20, 30%,

00:57:24.600 --> 00:57:29.553
where it's either in both
hemispheres or on the right side.

00:57:30.780 --> 00:57:34.110
And just to make this a
little bit more interesting

00:57:34.110 --> 00:57:38.970
is that when people have
strokes on the left side,

00:57:38.970 --> 00:57:42.390
and if they are lucky enough
to recover from those strokes,

00:57:42.390 --> 00:57:44.490
sometimes that involves reorganization,

00:57:44.490 --> 00:57:47.010
this term that we call plasticity earlier,

00:57:47.010 --> 00:57:49.830
where the areas around where the stroke

00:57:49.830 --> 00:57:50.910
take on that new function

00:57:50.910 --> 00:57:53.040
in a way that they didn't have before.

00:57:53.040 --> 00:57:56.520
That can certainly happen
in the left hemisphere,

00:57:56.520 --> 00:58:00.990
but there are also instances
where the right hemisphere

00:58:00.990 --> 00:58:03.730
can also start to take on
the function of language

00:58:03.730 --> 00:58:08.730
where it was once on left and
then transfers to the right.

00:58:08.790 --> 00:58:12.210
So the thing that I think about a lot

00:58:12.210 --> 00:58:15.870
is that the machinery

00:58:15.870 --> 00:58:18.960
probably exists on both sides,

00:58:18.960 --> 00:58:22.500
but we don't use them
together all the time.

00:58:22.500 --> 00:58:26.340
In fact, we may strongly
bias one side or the other.

00:58:26.340 --> 00:58:29.880
Just like we use our two hands
in very, very different ways,

00:58:29.880 --> 00:58:31.800
it's a little bit the same with the brain.

00:58:31.800 --> 00:58:33.630
Well, it's because of
what we do with the brain

00:58:33.630 --> 00:58:36.510
that actually is why we use
the hands in different ways.

00:58:36.510 --> 00:58:38.250
And the same thing goes for language,

00:58:38.250 --> 00:58:41.250
which is that, again, the
substrates, the organ,

00:58:41.250 --> 00:58:44.700
the language organ, the part
of the brain that process it

00:58:44.700 --> 00:58:47.730
probably has very similar machinery

00:58:47.730 --> 00:58:50.217
on the left side as the right,

00:58:50.217 --> 00:58:53.250
and the right may have
the capability to do it,

00:58:53.250 --> 00:58:55.680
but in real, everyday use,

00:58:55.680 --> 00:58:59.190
the brain specializes one of the sides

00:58:59.190 --> 00:59:03.360
in order for us to use it functionally.

00:59:03.360 --> 00:59:04.770
That's a theory.

00:59:04.770 --> 00:59:06.120
- You're bilingual, correct?

00:59:06.120 --> 00:59:06.953
- Yeah.

00:59:06.953 --> 00:59:08.220
- [Andrew] You speak English and Chinese?

00:59:08.220 --> 00:59:09.750
- Yeah.

00:59:09.750 --> 00:59:13.290
- For people that are bilingual
and that learn two or more,

00:59:13.290 --> 00:59:14.400
well, bilingual is two, obviously,

00:59:14.400 --> 00:59:16.560
but learn both languages

00:59:16.560 --> 00:59:20.940
or let's say more languages
from an early time in life,

00:59:20.940 --> 00:59:23.820
do they use the same brain
area to generate that language?

00:59:23.820 --> 00:59:27.750
Or perhaps they use the
left side to speak English

00:59:27.750 --> 00:59:29.340
and the right side to speak Chinese?

00:59:29.340 --> 00:59:31.953
Do we know anything about
bilingualism in the brain?

00:59:32.997 --> 00:59:33.840
- Well, I think we know a lot

00:59:33.840 --> 00:59:35.670
about bilingualism in the brain.

00:59:35.670 --> 00:59:38.880
The answers are still out
there, the final answers on it,

00:59:38.880 --> 00:59:42.180
and part of the answer is yes, absolutely,

00:59:42.180 --> 00:59:46.083
we use some parts of the
brain very similarly.

00:59:46.980 --> 00:59:48.630
We actually have a study
in the lab right now

00:59:48.630 --> 00:59:49.650
where we're looking at this

00:59:49.650 --> 00:59:53.340
where people who speak
one language or another

00:59:53.340 --> 00:59:55.230
or are bilingual,

00:59:55.230 --> 00:59:59.340
and we're looking at how the
brain activity patterns occur

00:59:59.340 --> 01:00:01.110
when they're hearing one
language versus the other.

01:00:01.110 --> 01:00:03.600
And what's striking to see, actually,

01:00:03.600 --> 01:00:06.570
is how overlapping they really can be.

01:00:06.570 --> 01:00:09.720
Even though the person may have no idea

01:00:09.720 --> 01:00:12.240
of the language that they're hearing,

01:00:12.240 --> 01:00:15.210
the English part of the brain
is still processing that

01:00:15.210 --> 01:00:16.950
and maybe trying to interpret it

01:00:16.950 --> 01:00:20.040
through an English lens, for example.

01:00:20.040 --> 01:00:23.370
So the short answer is
that with bilingualism,

01:00:23.370 --> 01:00:25.928
there are shared circuitry,

01:00:25.928 --> 01:00:28.710
there's this shared machinery in the brain

01:00:28.710 --> 01:00:33.003
that allows us to process
both, but it's not identical.

01:00:34.320 --> 01:00:35.880
It's the same part of the brain,

01:00:35.880 --> 01:00:38.220
but what it's doing with the signals

01:00:38.220 --> 01:00:40.320
can be very, very different.

01:00:40.320 --> 01:00:41.940
And what I mean by that precisely

01:00:41.940 --> 01:00:46.440
is not the instantaneous detecting
of one sound to the next,

01:00:46.440 --> 01:00:51.440
but the memory of the sequences
of those particular sounds

01:00:51.600 --> 01:00:54.360
that give rise to things
like words and meaning,

01:00:54.360 --> 01:00:58.380
that can be highly variable
from one individual to the next,

01:00:58.380 --> 01:01:01.380
and those neurons are very, very sensitive

01:01:01.380 --> 01:01:03.390
to the sequences of the sounds,

01:01:03.390 --> 01:01:05.580
even though the sounds themselves

01:01:05.580 --> 01:01:07.920
might have some overlap between languages.

01:01:07.920 --> 01:01:09.180
- Fascinating.

01:01:09.180 --> 01:01:11.190
Okay, so we've talked about brain areas

01:01:11.190 --> 01:01:13.830
and a little bit about lateralization.

01:01:13.830 --> 01:01:15.090
I want to get back to the hands

01:01:15.090 --> 01:01:17.040
and some things related to
emotion in a little bit.

01:01:17.040 --> 01:01:20.790
But maybe now we could
go into those brain areas

01:01:20.790 --> 01:01:24.090
and start to ask the question,

01:01:24.090 --> 01:01:27.390
what exactly is represented
or mapped there?

01:01:27.390 --> 01:01:29.910
And for people who perhaps aren't familiar

01:01:29.910 --> 01:01:33.120
with brain mapping and
representation and receptive fields,

01:01:33.120 --> 01:01:38.040
perhaps the simplest analogy
might be the visual system

01:01:38.040 --> 01:01:40.560
where I look at your face,
I know you, I recognize you,

01:01:40.560 --> 01:01:41.790
and certainly there are brain areas

01:01:41.790 --> 01:01:45.390
that are responsible for face recognition.

01:01:45.390 --> 01:01:48.990
But the fact that I know
that that's your face,

01:01:48.990 --> 01:01:51.570
and for those listening, I'm
looking into Eddie's face,

01:01:51.570 --> 01:01:53.220
the fact that I know that
that's your face at all

01:01:53.220 --> 01:01:56.610
is because we are well
aware that there are cells

01:01:56.610 --> 01:01:59.550
that represent edges and that
represent dark and light,

01:01:59.550 --> 01:02:00.570
and those all combine

01:02:00.570 --> 01:02:02.370
in what we call a hierarchical structure.

01:02:02.370 --> 01:02:05.250
They sort of build up from basic elements

01:02:05.250 --> 01:02:06.510
as simple as little dots,

01:02:06.510 --> 01:02:08.400
but then lines and things
that move, et cetera,

01:02:08.400 --> 01:02:11.313
to give a coherent
representation of the face.

01:02:12.540 --> 01:02:13.710
When I think about language,

01:02:13.710 --> 01:02:15.630
I think about words and just talking.

01:02:15.630 --> 01:02:17.190
If I sit down and do a long podcast

01:02:17.190 --> 01:02:18.510
or I think about asking you a question,

01:02:18.510 --> 01:02:21.930
I don't even think about the
words I want to say very much.

01:02:21.930 --> 01:02:23.310
I mean, I have to think
about them a little bit,

01:02:23.310 --> 01:02:24.390
one would hope,

01:02:24.390 --> 01:02:26.610
but I don't think about
individual syllables

01:02:26.610 --> 01:02:30.180
unless I'm trying to, you
know, accent something

01:02:30.180 --> 01:02:34.290
or it's a word that I have a
particular difficulty saying

01:02:34.290 --> 01:02:36.870
or I want to change
the cadence, et cetera.

01:02:36.870 --> 01:02:39.210
So what's represented in the neurons,

01:02:39.210 --> 01:02:40.410
the nerve cells in these areas?

01:02:40.410 --> 01:02:43.020
Are they representing vowels, consonants?

01:02:43.020 --> 01:02:44.520
And how do things like inflection...

01:02:44.520 --> 01:02:47.790
Like I occasionally will
poke fun at upspeak,

01:02:47.790 --> 01:02:52.790
but there's, I think, a healthy,
a normal version of upspeak

01:02:52.950 --> 01:02:54.240
where somebody's asking a question,

01:02:54.240 --> 01:02:56.520
like, for instance, what is that?

01:02:56.520 --> 01:02:58.710
That's an appropriate use of upspeak

01:02:58.710 --> 01:03:01.890
as opposed to saying something
that is not a question

01:03:01.890 --> 01:03:03.750
and putting a lilt at
the end of the sentence,

01:03:03.750 --> 01:03:05.190
then we call that upspeak,

01:03:05.190 --> 01:03:08.910
which doesn't fit with
what the person is saying.

01:03:08.910 --> 01:03:11.940
So what in the world is
contained in these brain areas,

01:03:11.940 --> 01:03:13.980
what is represented,

01:03:13.980 --> 01:03:17.130
to me, is perhaps one of the
most interesting questions,

01:03:17.130 --> 01:03:19.440
and I know this lands
square in your wheelhouse.

01:03:19.440 --> 01:03:22.440
- Sure, let's get into this, Andrew,

01:03:22.440 --> 01:03:25.320
because this is one of
the most exciting stuff

01:03:25.320 --> 01:03:27.210
that's happening right
now is understanding

01:03:27.210 --> 01:03:30.780
how the brain processes
these exact questions.

01:03:30.780 --> 01:03:31.630
And you asked me earlier,

01:03:31.630 --> 01:03:34.950
you know, what is difference
between speech and language?

01:03:34.950 --> 01:03:39.810
Speech corresponds to
the communication signal.

01:03:39.810 --> 01:03:43.140
It corresponds to me moving
my mouth and my vocal tract

01:03:43.140 --> 01:03:44.850
to generate words,

01:03:44.850 --> 01:03:48.450
and you're hearing these
as an auditory signal.

01:03:48.450 --> 01:03:52.290
Language is something much broader.

01:03:52.290 --> 01:03:55.350
So it refers to what you're extracting

01:03:55.350 --> 01:03:56.730
from the words that I'm saying,

01:03:56.730 --> 01:03:57.900
we call that pragmatics

01:03:57.900 --> 01:04:00.510
and sort of are you getting
the gist of what I'm saying?

01:04:00.510 --> 01:04:02.820
There's another aspect of
it that we call semantics.

01:04:02.820 --> 01:04:04.830
Do you understand the meaning

01:04:04.830 --> 01:04:08.250
of these words and the sentences?

01:04:08.250 --> 01:04:09.900
There's another part that we call syntax,

01:04:09.900 --> 01:04:12.480
which refers to how
the words are assembled

01:04:12.480 --> 01:04:14.070
in a grammatical form.

01:04:14.070 --> 01:04:17.130
So those are all really
critical parts of language,

01:04:17.130 --> 01:04:20.310
and speech is just one form of language.

01:04:20.310 --> 01:04:24.120
There's many other forms
like sign language, reading.

01:04:24.120 --> 01:04:27.090
Those are all important
modalities for reading.

01:04:27.090 --> 01:04:31.620
Our research really focuses on this area

01:04:31.620 --> 01:04:32.640
that we're calling speech,

01:04:32.640 --> 01:04:36.990
again, the production of this audio signal

01:04:36.990 --> 01:04:41.190
which you can't see but your
microphones are picking up.

01:04:41.190 --> 01:04:43.890
There are these vibrations in the air

01:04:43.890 --> 01:04:45.780
that are created by my vocal tract

01:04:45.780 --> 01:04:47.580
that are picked up by the microphone

01:04:48.540 --> 01:04:49.800
in the case of this recording,

01:04:49.800 --> 01:04:53.070
but also picked up by
the sensors in your ear.

01:04:53.070 --> 01:04:56.910
The very tiny vibrations in
your ear are picking that up

01:04:56.910 --> 01:05:00.930
and translating that
into electrical activity.

01:05:00.930 --> 01:05:04.200
And what the ear does at the periphery

01:05:04.200 --> 01:05:09.200
is translates all sounds
into different frequencies.

01:05:09.390 --> 01:05:14.390
So its main thing to do
is to take a speech signal

01:05:14.520 --> 01:05:17.670
or any other kind of
sound and decompose it,

01:05:17.670 --> 01:05:22.670
meaning separate that sound
into different kind of signals.

01:05:23.310 --> 01:05:25.470
And in the case of
hearing, what it's doing

01:05:25.470 --> 01:05:30.470
is separating it out into
low, middle, high frequencies

01:05:30.720 --> 01:05:33.630
at a very, very high resolution.

01:05:33.630 --> 01:05:35.100
It's doing it very quickly,

01:05:35.100 --> 01:05:36.360
and it's doing it in a really fine way

01:05:36.360 --> 01:05:38.730
to separate all of those different sounds.

01:05:38.730 --> 01:05:40.680
So if you look at the periphery

01:05:40.680 --> 01:05:43.650
near the nerve that goes to your ear,

01:05:43.650 --> 01:05:44.670
those nerve fibers,

01:05:44.670 --> 01:05:47.490
some of them are tuned to low frequencies,

01:05:47.490 --> 01:05:49.470
some of them are tuned
to high frequencies,

01:05:49.470 --> 01:05:51.750
some of them are tuned to
the middle frequencies,

01:05:51.750 --> 01:05:53.730
and that is what your ear is doing.

01:05:53.730 --> 01:05:54.840
It's taking these words

01:05:54.840 --> 01:05:58.080
and splitting them up into
different frequencies.

01:05:58.080 --> 01:05:59.160
- And for those of you out there

01:05:59.160 --> 01:06:00.540
that aren't familiar with
thinking about things

01:06:00.540 --> 01:06:02.223
in the so-called frequency space,

01:06:03.420 --> 01:06:05.340
bass tones would be lower frequencies

01:06:05.340 --> 01:06:07.590
and high-pitched tones
would be higher frequencies,

01:06:07.590 --> 01:06:09.810
just to make sure
everyone's on the same page.

01:06:09.810 --> 01:06:12.480
So the sound of my voice,
the sound of your voice,

01:06:12.480 --> 01:06:13.470
or any sound in the environment

01:06:13.470 --> 01:06:15.660
is being broken down
into these frequencies.

01:06:15.660 --> 01:06:17.040
Are they being broken down

01:06:17.040 --> 01:06:20.040
into very narrow channels of frequency,

01:06:20.040 --> 01:06:23.520
or are they, I want to
avoid nomenclature here,

01:06:23.520 --> 01:06:27.240
or are they being binned as
fairly broad frequencies?

01:06:27.240 --> 01:06:29.310
'Cause we know low, medium, and high,

01:06:29.310 --> 01:06:31.860
but, for instance, I can detect

01:06:31.860 --> 01:06:33.690
whether or not something's approaching me

01:06:33.690 --> 01:06:35.070
or moving away from me

01:06:35.070 --> 01:06:38.197
depending on whether
or not it sweeps louder

01:06:38.197 --> 01:06:39.270
[imitates sound approaching]

01:06:39.270 --> 01:06:42.120
or [imitates sound receding],
right, towards or away.

01:06:42.120 --> 01:06:43.980
It's subtle, and of course it's combined

01:06:43.980 --> 01:06:46.050
with what I see and my own movement,

01:06:46.050 --> 01:06:50.340
But how finely sliced

01:06:50.340 --> 01:06:53.160
is our perception of the auditory world?

01:06:53.160 --> 01:06:55.830
- Oh, extraordinarily precise.

01:06:55.830 --> 01:07:00.090
I mean, we take these millisecond cues,

01:07:00.090 --> 01:07:01.530
the millisecond differences

01:07:01.530 --> 01:07:03.780
between the sound coming to one ear,

01:07:03.780 --> 01:07:06.540
let's say your right ear versus your left,

01:07:06.540 --> 01:07:10.110
to understand what direction
that sound came from.

01:07:10.110 --> 01:07:12.570
Those are only millisecond differences,

01:07:12.570 --> 01:07:15.060
and that's how precise this works.

01:07:15.060 --> 01:07:16.710
But on the other hand,

01:07:16.710 --> 01:07:18.900
it does a lot of computation on this

01:07:18.900 --> 01:07:21.507
it does a lot of analysis as you go up,

01:07:21.507 --> 01:07:25.770
and a lot of our work is
focused on the part of the brain

01:07:25.770 --> 01:07:26.850
that we call the cortex.

01:07:26.850 --> 01:07:31.850
The cortex is the outermost
part of brain where we believe

01:07:32.220 --> 01:07:36.600
that sounds are actually
converted into words and language.

01:07:36.600 --> 01:07:38.250
So there's this transformation

01:07:38.250 --> 01:07:42.120
where, at the ear, words are decomposed

01:07:42.120 --> 01:07:46.770
and turned into these
elemental frequency channels,

01:07:46.770 --> 01:07:49.623
and then as it goes up
through the auditory system,

01:07:50.490 --> 01:07:52.320
hits the cortex.

01:07:52.320 --> 01:07:53.760
There are some things
that happen obviously

01:07:53.760 --> 01:07:55.530
before it gets to the cortex,

01:07:55.530 --> 01:07:56.940
but when it gets to cortex,

01:07:56.940 --> 01:07:59.280
there's something special going on,

01:07:59.280 --> 01:08:00.900
which is that that part of the brain

01:08:00.900 --> 01:08:04.320
is looking for specific sounds.

01:08:04.320 --> 01:08:06.210
And specifically what I mean by that

01:08:06.210 --> 01:08:09.330
is the sounds of human language,

01:08:09.330 --> 01:08:12.273
so the ones that are the
different consonants and vowels

01:08:12.273 --> 01:08:14.010
in a different language.

01:08:14.010 --> 01:08:16.620
One of the ways that we have studied this

01:08:16.620 --> 01:08:21.000
is looking in patients who have epilepsy.

01:08:21.000 --> 01:08:22.410
And in a lot of these cases

01:08:22.410 --> 01:08:25.470
where the MRI looks completely normal,

01:08:25.470 --> 01:08:29.643
we have to put electrodes
surgically on a part of the brain.

01:08:30.720 --> 01:08:32.460
The temporal lobe is a
very, very common place,

01:08:32.460 --> 01:08:33.600
so we've done a lot of our work

01:08:33.600 --> 01:08:37.830
looking at how the temporal
lobe processes speech sounds

01:08:37.830 --> 01:08:42.270
because we're looking for
where the seizures start,

01:08:42.270 --> 01:08:44.430
but then we're also doing brain mapping

01:08:44.430 --> 01:08:47.070
for language and speech so
we can protect those areas.

01:08:47.070 --> 01:08:49.080
We want to identify the
areas that we want to remove

01:08:49.080 --> 01:08:50.520
to cure someone's seizures,

01:08:50.520 --> 01:08:52.170
but we also want to figure out the areas

01:08:52.170 --> 01:08:53.910
that are important for speech and language

01:08:53.910 --> 01:08:56.100
to protect those so
that we can do a surgery

01:08:56.100 --> 01:08:58.380
that's effective and safe.

01:08:58.380 --> 01:09:01.440
And so in our research,

01:09:01.440 --> 01:09:04.590
and why it's become a
really important addition

01:09:04.590 --> 01:09:05.760
to our knowledge

01:09:05.760 --> 01:09:09.240
is that we have electrodes
directly recording

01:09:09.240 --> 01:09:12.090
from the human brain surface.

01:09:12.090 --> 01:09:14.460
A lot of the technology
we work with right now

01:09:14.460 --> 01:09:17.730
is recording on the order of millimeters,

01:09:17.730 --> 01:09:22.170
and they can record
millisecond time resolution

01:09:22.170 --> 01:09:24.060
of neural activity,

01:09:24.060 --> 01:09:29.060
and what we see is extraordinary
patterns of activity

01:09:29.790 --> 01:09:32.850
when people hear words and sentences.

01:09:32.850 --> 01:09:34.470
If you look at that part of the brain

01:09:34.470 --> 01:09:35.540
that we call Wernicke's area

01:09:35.540 --> 01:09:37.680
in this part of the temporal lobe,

01:09:37.680 --> 01:09:42.680
this whole area lights up
when you hear words or speech.

01:09:43.050 --> 01:09:44.730
And it's not in a way

01:09:44.730 --> 01:09:48.090
that is like a general
light bulb warming up

01:09:48.090 --> 01:09:50.730
and it's generally lit up,

01:09:50.730 --> 01:09:51.630
but what you actually see

01:09:51.630 --> 01:09:54.300
is something much, much more complicated,

01:09:54.300 --> 01:09:56.580
which is a pattern of activity,

01:09:56.580 --> 01:09:58.920
and what we've done in the last 10 years

01:09:58.920 --> 01:10:02.910
is try to understand what
does that pattern come from?

01:10:02.910 --> 01:10:05.370
And if we were to look
at each individual site

01:10:05.370 --> 01:10:08.823
from that part of the
brain, what would we see?

01:10:09.750 --> 01:10:14.360
What parts of words are being
coded by electrical activity

01:10:14.360 --> 01:10:15.300
in those parts of the brain?

01:10:15.300 --> 01:10:18.390
Remember, the cortex is
using electrical activity

01:10:18.390 --> 01:10:22.410
to transmit information and do analysis,

01:10:22.410 --> 01:10:24.450
and what we're doing
is we're eavesdropping

01:10:24.450 --> 01:10:27.930
on this part of the brain
as it's processing speech

01:10:27.930 --> 01:10:31.050
to try to understand what
each individual site is doing.

01:10:31.050 --> 01:10:32.880
- And what are those sites doing,

01:10:32.880 --> 01:10:34.260
or could you give us some examples

01:10:34.260 --> 01:10:35.220
of what those sites are doing?

01:10:35.220 --> 01:10:39.890
So, for instance, are they
sites that are specific for,

01:10:39.890 --> 01:10:43.590
or we could say even listening
for consonants or for vowels

01:10:43.590 --> 01:10:47.703
or for inflection or for emotionality?

01:10:48.930 --> 01:10:49.830
What's in there?

01:10:49.830 --> 01:10:50.663
- [Eddie] Okay, well-

01:10:50.663 --> 01:10:53.700
- What makes these, what
makes these cells fire?

01:10:53.700 --> 01:10:55.440
- Yeah, what gets them excited?

01:10:55.440 --> 01:10:56.850
- Yeah.
- What gets them going

01:10:56.850 --> 01:10:58.560
is hearing speech.

01:10:58.560 --> 01:11:02.850
In particular, there are some
of these really focal sites,

01:11:02.850 --> 01:11:04.620
again, just on the order of millimeter

01:11:04.620 --> 01:11:07.650
or, at some level, single neurons

01:11:07.650 --> 01:11:12.300
that are tuned to consonants,
some are tuned to vowels,

01:11:12.300 --> 01:11:16.260
some are tuned to particular
features of consonants.

01:11:16.260 --> 01:11:21.260
What I mean by that are different
categories of consonants.

01:11:21.600 --> 01:11:22.980
There's a class of consonants

01:11:22.980 --> 01:11:24.593
that we call plosive consonants.

01:11:24.593 --> 01:11:26.970
This is a little bit of linguistic jargon,

01:11:26.970 --> 01:11:28.380
but I'm going to make
a point here with that

01:11:28.380 --> 01:11:31.350
is that certain classes of
sounds, when you make them,

01:11:31.350 --> 01:11:34.290
it requires you to actually
close your mouth temporarily.

01:11:34.290 --> 01:11:37.560
- Hmm. Now I'm going to
be thinking about this.

01:11:37.560 --> 01:11:39.298
So plosive, like plosive,

01:11:39.298 --> 01:11:41.700
like saying the word
plosive requires that.

01:11:41.700 --> 01:11:44.070
- Exactly, so what's cool about that

01:11:44.070 --> 01:11:46.560
is that we actually have no idea

01:11:46.560 --> 01:11:49.260
what's going on in our
mouth when we speak.

01:11:49.260 --> 01:11:50.520
We really have no idea.

01:11:50.520 --> 01:11:52.183
- Some people definitely have no idea.

01:11:52.183 --> 01:11:53.430
[Andrew laughs]
[Eddie laughs]

01:11:53.430 --> 01:11:54.540
- Well, not just like in terms

01:11:54.540 --> 01:11:56.070
of what you're saying sometimes,

01:11:56.070 --> 01:11:59.550
but actually like how
you're actually moving,

01:11:59.550 --> 01:12:01.080
you know, the different
parts of vocal tract.

01:12:01.080 --> 01:12:05.910
And I have a feeling if we
actually required understanding,

01:12:05.910 --> 01:12:08.550
we would never be able to
speak 'cause it's so complex.

01:12:08.550 --> 01:12:09.750
It's such a complex feat.

01:12:09.750 --> 01:12:12.810
Some people would say it's
the most complex motor thing

01:12:12.810 --> 01:12:15.420
that we do as a species is just speaking.

01:12:15.420 --> 01:12:18.810
Not, you know, the extreme
feats of acrobatics

01:12:18.810 --> 01:12:21.480
or athleticism but speaking.

01:12:21.480 --> 01:12:24.870
- And especially when one
observes, you know, opera

01:12:24.870 --> 01:12:28.560
or people who, you
know, freestyle rappers.

01:12:28.560 --> 01:12:32.250
And of course it's not just
the lips. It's the tongue.

01:12:32.250 --> 01:12:35.220
And you've mentioned two other structures

01:12:35.220 --> 01:12:38.136
Pharynx and larynx are the main ones.

01:12:38.136 --> 01:12:41.520
Can you tell us, just educate
us at a superficial level

01:12:41.520 --> 01:12:44.767
what the pharynx and
larynx do differentially?

01:12:44.767 --> 01:12:46.350
'Cause I think most people aren't going to

01:12:46.350 --> 01:12:47.670
be familiar with that.
- Okay, sure.

01:12:47.670 --> 01:12:50.520
So I'll talk primarily

01:12:50.520 --> 01:12:53.430
about the larynx here for a second,

01:12:53.430 --> 01:12:56.403
which is that if you think
about when we're speaking,

01:12:57.570 --> 01:13:00.810
really, what we're doing is
we're shaping the breath.

01:13:00.810 --> 01:13:02.430
So even before you get to the larynx,

01:13:02.430 --> 01:13:04.863
you got to start with the expiration.

01:13:06.120 --> 01:13:11.120
So we fill up our lungs and
then we push the air out.

01:13:11.550 --> 01:13:14.070
That's a normal part of breathing.

01:13:14.070 --> 01:13:16.970
And what is really amazing
about speech and language

01:13:16.970 --> 01:13:19.590
is that we evolved to take advantage

01:13:19.590 --> 01:13:23.490
of that normal physiologic
thing, add a larynx,

01:13:23.490 --> 01:13:26.490
and what the larynx does is
that when you're exhaling,

01:13:26.490 --> 01:13:28.830
it brings the vocal folds together.

01:13:28.830 --> 01:13:30.930
Some people call them vocal cords.

01:13:30.930 --> 01:13:33.450
They're not really cords.
They're really vocal folds.

01:13:33.450 --> 01:13:35.850
They're two pieces of
tissue that come together,

01:13:35.850 --> 01:13:37.680
and a muscle brings them together.

01:13:37.680 --> 01:13:38.513
And then what happens

01:13:38.513 --> 01:13:41.010
is when the air comes
through the vocal folds

01:13:41.010 --> 01:13:41.843
when they're together,

01:13:41.843 --> 01:13:45.324
they vibrate at really high frequencies,

01:13:45.324 --> 01:13:47.400
like 100 to 200 hertz.

01:13:47.400 --> 01:13:50.008
Yours is probably about 100 hertz.

01:13:50.008 --> 01:13:51.480
The average-
- Whereas yours is 200.

01:13:51.480 --> 01:13:52.471
[Andrew laughs]

01:13:52.471 --> 01:13:55.980
- No, no. Most male voices
are around 100, okay?

01:13:55.980 --> 01:13:58.679
And then the average female
voice around 200 hertz.

01:13:58.679 --> 01:14:00.690
- Well, and as you know, I've
always had the same voice.

01:14:00.690 --> 01:14:02.130
- Yes, yes, the same-
- This was a point of shame

01:14:02.130 --> 01:14:03.240
when I was a kid.

01:14:03.240 --> 01:14:06.150
Folks, my voice never changed.
I always had the same voice.

01:14:06.150 --> 01:14:07.770
This is a discussion for another time.

01:14:07.770 --> 01:14:09.810
- Yeah, well, it's a great voice,

01:14:09.810 --> 01:14:11.700
you know, a great baritone voice,

01:14:11.700 --> 01:14:15.900
but I know in your voice,
it's a low-frequency voice.

01:14:15.900 --> 01:14:18.060
And the reason why men and women

01:14:18.060 --> 01:14:21.780
generally have different voice qualities

01:14:21.780 --> 01:14:24.750
is it has to do with
the size of the larynx

01:14:24.750 --> 01:14:26.340
and the shape of it, okay?

01:14:26.340 --> 01:14:30.720
So in general, men have a
larger voice box or larynx,

01:14:30.720 --> 01:14:33.450
and the vibrating frequency,
the resonance frequency

01:14:33.450 --> 01:14:36.780
of the vocal folds when
the air comes through them

01:14:36.780 --> 01:14:40.260
is about 100 hertz for men
and about 200 for women.

01:14:40.260 --> 01:14:43.020
So what happens is,

01:14:43.020 --> 01:14:46.710
okay, so you take a breath in,

01:14:46.710 --> 01:14:49.800
and then as the air is coming out,

01:14:49.800 --> 01:14:52.470
the vocal folds come together
and the air goes through.

01:14:52.470 --> 01:14:56.583
That creates the sound of the
voice that we call voicing,

01:14:57.480 --> 01:14:59.880
and that's the energy of your voice.

01:14:59.880 --> 01:15:02.670
It's not just your voice characteristic,

01:15:02.670 --> 01:15:05.070
it's the energy of your voice.

01:15:05.070 --> 01:15:08.943
It's coming from the
larynx there, it's a noise.

01:15:09.900 --> 01:15:12.990
And then it's the source of the voice.

01:15:12.990 --> 01:15:15.570
And then what happens is that energy,

01:15:15.570 --> 01:15:19.650
that sound goes up through
the parts of the vocal tract,

01:15:19.650 --> 01:15:23.820
like the pharynx into the oral cavity,

01:15:23.820 --> 01:15:26.850
which is your mouth and
your tongue and your lips.

01:15:26.850 --> 01:15:28.920
And what those things are doing

01:15:28.920 --> 01:15:33.240
is that they're shaping
the air in particular ways

01:15:33.240 --> 01:15:35.673
that create consonants and vowels.

01:15:36.930 --> 01:15:40.110
So that's what I mean
by shaping the breath.

01:15:40.110 --> 01:15:43.110
It just starts with this exhalation.

01:15:43.110 --> 01:15:45.630
You generate the voice in the larynx,

01:15:45.630 --> 01:15:49.050
and then everything above
the larynx is moving around,

01:15:49.050 --> 01:15:51.330
just like the way my
mouth is doing right now,

01:15:51.330 --> 01:15:54.880
to shape that air into particular patterns

01:15:55.931 --> 01:15:58.053
that you can hear as words.

01:15:59.190 --> 01:16:04.190
- Fascinating, and
immediately makes me wonder

01:16:04.200 --> 01:16:09.000
about more primitive or
non-learned vocalizations

01:16:09.000 --> 01:16:11.070
like crying or laughter.

01:16:11.070 --> 01:16:14.730
Babies will cry, babies
will show laughter.

01:16:14.730 --> 01:16:19.200
Are those sorts of vocalizations

01:16:19.200 --> 01:16:22.350
produced by the language
areas like Wernicke's,

01:16:22.350 --> 01:16:25.530
or do they have their own
unique neural structures?

01:16:25.530 --> 01:16:26.970
- Yeah, interesting question.

01:16:26.970 --> 01:16:29.973
So we call those vocalizations.

01:16:31.170 --> 01:16:33.630
A vocalization is basically where someone

01:16:33.630 --> 01:16:37.803
can create a sound, like a cry or a moan,

01:16:38.880 --> 01:16:40.320
that kind of sound,

01:16:40.320 --> 01:16:44.850
and it also involves
the exhalation of air.

01:16:44.850 --> 01:16:48.210
It also involves some
phonation at the level larynx

01:16:48.210 --> 01:16:50.220
where the vocal folds come together

01:16:50.220 --> 01:16:51.993
to create that audible sound.

01:16:53.220 --> 01:16:56.340
But it turns out that those
are actually different areas,

01:16:56.340 --> 01:17:00.360
so people who have injuries in
the speech and language areas

01:17:00.360 --> 01:17:03.960
oftentimes can still moan,
they can still vocalize,

01:17:03.960 --> 01:17:05.880
and it is a different part of the brain,

01:17:05.880 --> 01:17:09.810
I would say an area that
even nonhuman primates have

01:17:09.810 --> 01:17:12.900
that can be specialized,
you know, for vocalization.

01:17:12.900 --> 01:17:15.420
It's a different form of communication

01:17:15.420 --> 01:17:17.700
than words, for example.

01:17:17.700 --> 01:17:20.070
- The intricacy of these
circuits in the brain

01:17:20.070 --> 01:17:25.070
and their connections to the
pharynx and larynx is just,

01:17:25.170 --> 01:17:26.580
it's almost overwhelming

01:17:26.580 --> 01:17:29.400
in terms of thinking about just
how complicated it must be,

01:17:29.400 --> 01:17:31.920
and yet some general
features and principles

01:17:31.920 --> 01:17:33.480
are starting to emerge from your work

01:17:33.480 --> 01:17:34.953
and from the work of others.

01:17:36.330 --> 01:17:38.070
If we think about that work

01:17:38.070 --> 01:17:40.803
and we think about, for
instance, Wernicke's area,

01:17:42.060 --> 01:17:47.060
if I were to record from
neurons in Wernicke's area

01:17:47.130 --> 01:17:48.240
at different locations,

01:17:48.240 --> 01:17:52.710
would I find that there's any
kind of systematic layout?

01:17:52.710 --> 01:17:55.650
For instance, in terms of, you
talked about sound frequency,

01:17:55.650 --> 01:17:56.730
we know that low frequencies

01:17:56.730 --> 01:17:58.380
are represented at one end of a structure

01:17:58.380 --> 01:17:59.550
and high frequencies at the other.

01:17:59.550 --> 01:18:02.190
This is true actually, at
least from my earlier training,

01:18:02.190 --> 01:18:04.740
within the ear itself, within the cochlea,

01:18:04.740 --> 01:18:07.560
the early work of von Bekesy
and from cadavers, right?

01:18:07.560 --> 01:18:09.420
They actually figured
this out from dead people,

01:18:09.420 --> 01:18:10.620
which is incredible.

01:18:10.620 --> 01:18:12.993
A fascinating literature
people should look up.

01:18:15.030 --> 01:18:15.960
And in the visual system,

01:18:15.960 --> 01:18:20.960
we know that, for instance,
you know, visual position,

01:18:20.970 --> 01:18:23.370
where things are is mapped systematically.

01:18:23.370 --> 01:18:24.203
In other words,

01:18:24.203 --> 01:18:26.250
neurons that sit next to
each other in the brain

01:18:26.250 --> 01:18:28.650
represent portions of visual space

01:18:28.650 --> 01:18:31.080
that are next to each
other in the real world.

01:18:31.080 --> 01:18:33.930
What is the organization of language

01:18:33.930 --> 01:18:36.180
in areas like Wernicke's and Broca's?

01:18:36.180 --> 01:18:39.963
For instance, I think of
the vowels, A, E, I, O, U,

01:18:40.890 --> 01:18:43.050
as kind of a coherent unit,

01:18:43.050 --> 01:18:46.590
but do I find the A neurons
are next to the E neurons

01:18:46.590 --> 01:18:50.700
or next to the A, E, I, O, U.

01:18:50.700 --> 01:18:55.050
Is that vowel representation
also laid out in order,

01:18:55.050 --> 01:18:57.990
or is it kind of salt
and pepper, is it random?

01:18:57.990 --> 01:19:00.660
- That's been one of the,
like, most important questions

01:19:00.660 --> 01:19:03.450
we've been trying to
answer for the past decade.

01:19:03.450 --> 01:19:06.060
So there is a part of the brain

01:19:06.060 --> 01:19:08.280
that we call the primary auditory cortex,

01:19:08.280 --> 01:19:10.230
and the primary auditory cortex

01:19:10.230 --> 01:19:11.430
is deep in the temporal lobe.

01:19:11.430 --> 01:19:15.240
And if you looked at
that part of the brain,

01:19:15.240 --> 01:19:18.420
there is a map of different
sound frequencies.

01:19:18.420 --> 01:19:21.720
So if you look at the front of
that primary auditory cortex,

01:19:21.720 --> 01:19:23.460
you'll find low-frequency sounds,

01:19:23.460 --> 01:19:28.350
and then as you march
backwards in that cortex,

01:19:28.350 --> 01:19:31.020
it goes from low to medium
to high frequencies.

01:19:31.020 --> 01:19:34.920
It's organized in this
really nice and orderly way.

01:19:34.920 --> 01:19:36.630
And it turns out there's not just one.

01:19:36.630 --> 01:19:40.950
There's, like, mirrors of
that tone frequency map

01:19:40.950 --> 01:19:43.680
in the primary auditory cortex.

01:19:43.680 --> 01:19:46.830
The areas that are really
important for speech

01:19:46.830 --> 01:19:48.570
are on the side of that.

01:19:48.570 --> 01:19:51.900
And we now think that speech

01:19:51.900 --> 01:19:53.940
can go straight to the speech cortex

01:19:53.940 --> 01:19:57.063
without having to go through
the primary auditory cortex,

01:19:57.063 --> 01:20:02.063
that it has its own pathway to
get to the part of the brain

01:20:02.370 --> 01:20:04.680
that processes speech.

01:20:04.680 --> 01:20:09.330
And when we've looked at that
question about is there a map,

01:20:09.330 --> 01:20:12.873
the short answer is yes, there is a map,

01:20:14.010 --> 01:20:18.174
but it is not structured
universally across all people

01:20:18.174 --> 01:20:21.120
in a way that we can
clearly see right now.

01:20:21.120 --> 01:20:23.670
It is like a salt and pepper map

01:20:23.670 --> 01:20:25.920
of the different features in speech.

01:20:25.920 --> 01:20:27.690
So before, we talked about these sounds

01:20:27.690 --> 01:20:29.430
that are called plosives.

01:20:29.430 --> 01:20:32.130
You make a plosive when the mouth

01:20:32.130 --> 01:20:35.070
or something in the oral
cavity closes temporarily,

01:20:35.070 --> 01:20:37.470
and when it opens,

01:20:37.470 --> 01:20:41.220
that creates that fast plosive sound.

01:20:41.220 --> 01:20:43.720
So when you say dad

01:20:45.000 --> 01:20:50.000
or, you know, like the B in ball,

01:20:50.400 --> 01:20:51.390
that kind of thing,

01:20:51.390 --> 01:20:54.090
you will notice that
your lips actually close,

01:20:54.090 --> 01:20:55.350
and then it's the release of that

01:20:55.350 --> 01:20:57.960
that creates that particular sounds, okay?

01:20:57.960 --> 01:20:59.760
So those are the sounds
that we call plosive.

01:20:59.760 --> 01:21:02.433
Those are like ba da ga, pa ta ka,

01:21:04.410 --> 01:21:05.673
Those are a certain class of consonants

01:21:05.673 --> 01:21:08.190
that we call plosive sounds.

01:21:08.190 --> 01:21:09.870
There is another class of sounds

01:21:09.870 --> 01:21:13.050
that we call fricatives in linguistics.

01:21:13.050 --> 01:21:16.470
Fricatives are created by turbulence

01:21:16.470 --> 01:21:20.523
in the airstream as it
comes out through the mouth,

01:21:21.652 --> 01:21:24.600
and the way that we make that turbulence

01:21:24.600 --> 01:21:27.510
is getting the mouth and the lips to close

01:21:27.510 --> 01:21:29.310
almost until they're completely shut

01:21:30.720 --> 01:21:33.780
or putting the tongue to near the teeth

01:21:33.780 --> 01:21:36.300
to almost get it completely shut

01:21:36.300 --> 01:21:38.340
but just have a narrow aperture,

01:21:38.340 --> 01:21:40.590
that creates a turbulence in the airflow

01:21:40.590 --> 01:21:42.210
that we perceive as a
high-frequency sound.

01:21:42.210 --> 01:21:45.123
So those are the sounds like sha and tha,

01:21:46.200 --> 01:21:47.040
those kind of things.

01:21:47.040 --> 01:21:48.810
Those are, if you look at the frequencies,

01:21:48.810 --> 01:21:49.950
they're higher frequencies,

01:21:49.950 --> 01:21:52.950
and those are created
by specific movements

01:21:52.950 --> 01:21:55.230
that you constrict the
airflow to create turbulence,

01:21:55.230 --> 01:21:59.853
and we hear it as sha, sa, tha.

01:22:00.810 --> 01:22:02.280
- So if I say that.

01:22:02.280 --> 01:22:03.120
- [Eddie] Exactly.

01:22:03.120 --> 01:22:07.260
- And as opposed to a plosive
where I'd say explosive.

01:22:07.260 --> 01:22:08.093
- [Eddie] Right.

01:22:08.093 --> 01:22:09.180
- Of course, I'm emphasizing here.

01:22:09.180 --> 01:22:11.880
Well, this explains something
and solves a mystery,

01:22:11.880 --> 01:22:14.070
which is recently I've
been fascinated by the work

01:22:14.070 --> 01:22:18.690
of a physician scientist back east,

01:22:18.690 --> 01:22:20.460
Dr. Shanna Swan, who's done a lot of work

01:22:20.460 --> 01:22:23.340
on things that are contained
in pesticides and foods

01:22:23.340 --> 01:22:25.380
that are changing hormone levels,

01:22:25.380 --> 01:22:28.890
and she refers to phthalates,
which is spelled...

01:22:28.890 --> 01:22:31.140
So it's both a plosive and a tha,

01:22:31.140 --> 01:22:32.130
so it's combining the two,

01:22:32.130 --> 01:22:33.930
and it's one of the most difficult words

01:22:33.930 --> 01:22:35.700
in the English language to pronounce,

01:22:35.700 --> 01:22:36.930
second only perhaps

01:22:36.930 --> 01:22:39.547
to the correct pronunciation
of ophthalmology.

01:22:39.547 --> 01:22:41.280
[Eddie laughs]
[Andrew laughs]

01:22:41.280 --> 01:22:43.980
So it's a combination of a plosive

01:22:43.980 --> 01:22:45.960
and one of these tha sounds,

01:22:45.960 --> 01:22:47.580
and that's probably why it's difficult.

01:22:47.580 --> 01:22:48.540
- That's exactly right.

01:22:48.540 --> 01:22:50.760
In fact, we have a term for that.

01:22:50.760 --> 01:22:52.260
That's called a consonant cluster.

01:22:52.260 --> 01:22:55.410
So sometimes syllables will
just have one consonant,

01:22:55.410 --> 01:22:58.680
but when we start stacking
certain syllables in a sequence,

01:22:58.680 --> 01:23:02.340
and there's rules that actually
govern which consonants

01:23:02.340 --> 01:23:05.163
can be in a particular
sequence for a given language,

01:23:06.510 --> 01:23:07.830
that makes it more complicated.

01:23:07.830 --> 01:23:08.850
And certain languages

01:23:08.850 --> 01:23:12.270
have a lot more constant
clusters than others.

01:23:12.270 --> 01:23:13.860
- For instance-
- So for instance,

01:23:13.860 --> 01:23:16.740
Russian, for example, has a
lot of consonant clusters.

01:23:16.740 --> 01:23:18.480
English has a lot of them.

01:23:18.480 --> 01:23:22.260
There are other languages
that have very, very few.

01:23:22.260 --> 01:23:24.300
For example, Hawaiian.

01:23:24.300 --> 01:23:25.380
Hawaiian has an inventory

01:23:25.380 --> 01:23:28.380
of about 12 to 14 different phonemes,

01:23:28.380 --> 01:23:30.630
14 different consonants and vowels.

01:23:30.630 --> 01:23:31.710
English, on contrast,

01:23:31.710 --> 01:23:35.250
has about 40 different
consonants and vowels.

01:23:35.250 --> 01:23:37.860
So languages have different inventories.

01:23:37.860 --> 01:23:39.870
They can overlap for sure,

01:23:39.870 --> 01:23:43.860
but different languages use
different sound elements,

01:23:43.860 --> 01:23:45.570
combine and recombine those elements

01:23:45.570 --> 01:23:48.270
to give rise to different
words and meanings.

01:23:48.270 --> 01:23:51.510
- Can we say that there is
a most complicated language

01:23:51.510 --> 01:23:53.160
out there, or among the most complicated?

01:23:53.160 --> 01:23:54.210
Would it be Russian?

01:23:54.210 --> 01:23:55.530
- It's definitely high up there.

01:23:55.530 --> 01:23:59.160
English is up there, too,
actually. Yeah, German as well.

01:23:59.160 --> 01:24:00.930
- And in terms of learning
multiple languages

01:24:00.930 --> 01:24:01.763
during development,

01:24:01.763 --> 01:24:03.060
my understanding is that if one

01:24:03.060 --> 01:24:05.940
wants to become bilingual or trilingual,

01:24:05.940 --> 01:24:08.520
best to learn those
languages simultaneously

01:24:08.520 --> 01:24:11.370
during development, ideally before age 12,

01:24:11.370 --> 01:24:14.580
if one hopes to not have an
accent in speaking them later.

01:24:14.580 --> 01:24:16.440
Is that correct, or do
you want to revise that?

01:24:16.440 --> 01:24:20.190
- Well, basically, the earlier,

01:24:20.190 --> 01:24:22.590
and the earlier is better,

01:24:22.590 --> 01:24:25.773
the more intense it is and
the more immersive it is,

01:24:26.790 --> 01:24:29.850
the longer, you know, that
you can be exposed to that

01:24:29.850 --> 01:24:30.683
is really important.

01:24:30.683 --> 01:24:32.430
A lot of people can
get exposed to it early

01:24:32.430 --> 01:24:33.480
and basically lose it.

01:24:33.480 --> 01:24:35.190
Even though it's, quote, unquote,

01:24:35.190 --> 01:24:36.720
during that sensitive period,

01:24:36.720 --> 01:24:40.170
unless it's maintained, it
can be very easily lost.

01:24:40.170 --> 01:24:42.840
Then I think another aspect
of it that's very interesting

01:24:42.840 --> 01:24:47.310
is some of the social
requirements for it too.

01:24:47.310 --> 01:24:51.040
It's pretty clear that
you can only go so far

01:24:51.960 --> 01:24:54.690
just listening to these
sounds from a tape recording

01:24:54.690 --> 01:24:56.250
or something like that.

01:24:56.250 --> 01:25:00.210
There's something extra
about real human interactions

01:25:00.210 --> 01:25:02.370
that activates the brain's sensitivity

01:25:02.370 --> 01:25:03.717
to different speech sounds

01:25:03.717 --> 01:25:05.820
and allows us to become
specialized for them

01:25:05.820 --> 01:25:07.860
for a given language.

01:25:07.860 --> 01:25:10.230
- So returning to what's mapped,

01:25:10.230 --> 01:25:13.020
what the representations are in the brain,

01:25:13.020 --> 01:25:15.210
I'm starting to get a picture now

01:25:15.210 --> 01:25:17.793
based on these plosives
and these tha sounds.

01:25:18.810 --> 01:25:22.650
And what I find so interesting
and logical about that

01:25:22.650 --> 01:25:24.777
is it maps to the motor structures

01:25:24.777 --> 01:25:26.880
and the actual
pronunciation of the sounds,

01:25:26.880 --> 01:25:31.290
not necessarily to the meaning
of the individual words.

01:25:31.290 --> 01:25:32.640
Now, of course, it's
related to the meaning

01:25:32.640 --> 01:25:34.290
of the individual words,

01:25:34.290 --> 01:25:36.810
but it makes good sense to me

01:25:36.810 --> 01:25:39.543
why something as complex as language,

01:25:40.440 --> 01:25:42.660
both to understand and to generate,

01:25:42.660 --> 01:25:46.380
would map to something that
is essentially motor in design

01:25:46.380 --> 01:25:50.340
because, as you point out, I
have to generate these sounds

01:25:50.340 --> 01:25:53.280
and I have to hear them
generated from others.

01:25:53.280 --> 01:25:56.910
However, there's reading
and there's writing,

01:25:56.910 --> 01:25:59.790
and writing is certainly motor,

01:25:59.790 --> 01:26:01.620
reading involves some motor commands

01:26:01.620 --> 01:26:04.440
of the eyes and et cetera.

01:26:04.440 --> 01:26:07.170
Where do reading and writing
come into this picture?

01:26:07.170 --> 01:26:10.980
Are they in parallel with, as
we would say in neuroscience,

01:26:10.980 --> 01:26:13.980
or are they embedded
within the same structures?

01:26:13.980 --> 01:26:18.510
Are they part of the same
series of computations?

01:26:18.510 --> 01:26:23.510
- Yeah, so to address the first part

01:26:24.150 --> 01:26:26.010
is that we've got this map

01:26:26.010 --> 01:26:29.010
of these different parts
of consonants and vowels,

01:26:29.010 --> 01:26:33.450
and when we look at how they lay out

01:26:33.450 --> 01:26:36.210
in this part of the brain
that we call Wernicke's area,

01:26:36.210 --> 01:26:39.240
we've spent a lot of time
really just dissecting this

01:26:39.240 --> 01:26:40.923
millimeter by millimeter.

01:26:41.850 --> 01:26:43.830
The term that you used is very apropos.

01:26:43.830 --> 01:26:46.140
It's salt and pepper. It's not random.

01:26:46.140 --> 01:26:48.210
There is this kind of selectivity

01:26:48.210 --> 01:26:50.010
to these individual speech sounds.

01:26:50.010 --> 01:26:52.140
And one point I want to
make about it is this,

01:26:52.140 --> 01:26:54.960
is that in English, for example,

01:26:54.960 --> 01:26:56.852
there are about 40 different phonemes.

01:26:56.852 --> 01:26:58.530
Phonemes are just consonants or vowels

01:26:58.530 --> 01:27:01.290
or individual speech segments.

01:27:01.290 --> 01:27:05.730
But these articulatory
features that you refer to,

01:27:05.730 --> 01:27:09.030
for example, the characteristic sounds

01:27:09.030 --> 01:27:12.153
that are generated by specific
movements in the mouth,

01:27:13.590 --> 01:27:15.120
you can more or less reduce that

01:27:15.120 --> 01:27:18.630
to about 12 different features.

01:27:18.630 --> 01:27:21.600
Okay, these are specific
movements of the tongue,

01:27:21.600 --> 01:27:23.613
the jaw, the lips, the larynx.

01:27:24.540 --> 01:27:27.480
There are about 12 of these movements,

01:27:27.480 --> 01:27:29.940
and just like you said, Andrew,

01:27:29.940 --> 01:27:32.073
by themselves, they have no meaning.

01:27:33.030 --> 01:27:34.920
They're just movements.

01:27:34.920 --> 01:27:36.233
But what's incredible about it

01:27:36.233 --> 01:27:38.520
is that you take these 12 movements

01:27:38.520 --> 01:27:41.010
and you put them in combinations

01:27:41.010 --> 01:27:42.960
and you start putting them in sequence.

01:27:44.910 --> 01:27:47.880
We as humans use those 12 set features

01:27:47.880 --> 01:27:50.550
to generate all words.

01:27:50.550 --> 01:27:51.840
And because we can generate

01:27:51.840 --> 01:27:53.610
nearly an infinite number of words

01:27:53.610 --> 01:27:57.030
with that code of just 12 features,

01:27:57.030 --> 01:27:58.320
we have something

01:27:58.320 --> 01:28:01.650
that generates essentially
all possible meaning

01:28:01.650 --> 01:28:04.860
because that's what we do as
humans, we generate meanings,

01:28:04.860 --> 01:28:07.080
I'm trying to communicate
one idea to another,

01:28:07.080 --> 01:28:09.390
which, to me, is extraordinary.

01:28:09.390 --> 01:28:11.880
A parallel would be, for example, DNA.

01:28:11.880 --> 01:28:14.550
There's four base pairs in DNA,

01:28:14.550 --> 01:28:18.270
but with those four base
pairs in a specific sequence

01:28:18.270 --> 01:28:21.210
can generate an entire code for life.

01:28:21.210 --> 01:28:22.380
And speech is the same way.

01:28:22.380 --> 01:28:25.050
It's like you've got
these fundamental elements

01:28:25.050 --> 01:28:26.580
that, by themselves, have no meaning,

01:28:26.580 --> 01:28:27.810
but when you put them together

01:28:27.810 --> 01:28:29.823
give rise to every possible meaning.

01:28:31.380 --> 01:28:35.010
So with regard to your second point

01:28:35.010 --> 01:28:38.223
about reading and writing,
it's a fascinating question.

01:28:39.120 --> 01:28:43.503
Speech and language is part
of who we are as humans.

01:28:44.910 --> 01:28:47.250
That's part of how we evolved,

01:28:47.250 --> 01:28:50.190
and it's hardwired

01:28:50.190 --> 01:28:53.643
and, you know, molded by experience.

01:28:54.510 --> 01:28:57.513
Reading and writing are a human invention.

01:28:59.970 --> 01:29:01.710
It's something that was added on

01:29:01.710 --> 01:29:03.780
to the architecture of the brain.

01:29:03.780 --> 01:29:05.940
And because reading and writing

01:29:05.940 --> 01:29:09.300
are fairly recent in human evolution,

01:29:09.300 --> 01:29:10.830
it's essentially too quick

01:29:10.830 --> 01:29:13.200
for anything to, like,
have a dramatic change

01:29:13.200 --> 01:29:15.630
in, let's say, a new brain area

01:29:15.630 --> 01:29:17.010
or some kind of specialization.

01:29:17.010 --> 01:29:21.270
Instead, what happens is that
whenever any kind of behavior

01:29:21.270 --> 01:29:26.270
becomes ultra specialized in
any of us or any organism,

01:29:26.370 --> 01:29:29.550
we can sort of take some areas

01:29:29.550 --> 01:29:32.850
that are normally involved
with vision, for example,

01:29:32.850 --> 01:29:35.850
and specialize it for
the purpose of reading.

01:29:35.850 --> 01:29:38.550
So all of us have a part of our brain

01:29:38.550 --> 01:29:39.720
in the back of the temporal lobe

01:29:39.720 --> 01:29:43.680
that interfaces with the
occipital visual cortex

01:29:43.680 --> 01:29:45.990
that we call a visual word form area.

01:29:45.990 --> 01:29:50.460
There's actually a part of the
brain that is very sensitive

01:29:50.460 --> 01:29:55.200
to seeing words, like,
either typed or handwritten.

01:29:55.200 --> 01:29:57.960
There's a part of the brain
that also is sensitive

01:29:57.960 --> 01:30:00.750
to seeing things like faces.

01:30:00.750 --> 01:30:02.850
So these are things
that are all conditioned

01:30:02.850 --> 01:30:06.603
on what's important, you know, to survive.

01:30:08.070 --> 01:30:10.500
So reading and writing are an invention,

01:30:10.500 --> 01:30:12.960
and there are things that have mapped

01:30:12.960 --> 01:30:17.010
to functions that the brain already has.

01:30:17.010 --> 01:30:19.800
And one of the really important things

01:30:19.800 --> 01:30:21.000
about reading and writing

01:30:21.000 --> 01:30:25.530
is that when we learn to read and write,

01:30:25.530 --> 01:30:27.300
especially with the reading part,

01:30:27.300 --> 01:30:29.160
it maps to the part of the brain

01:30:29.160 --> 01:30:30.390
that we've been talking about,

01:30:30.390 --> 01:30:33.123
which is the part that's
processing speech sounds.

01:30:34.290 --> 01:30:36.030
So some of us kind of think about it,

01:30:36.030 --> 01:30:37.020
these are two different things.

01:30:37.020 --> 01:30:40.170
One is hearing sounds through your ears,

01:30:40.170 --> 01:30:41.130
the other is reading

01:30:41.130 --> 01:30:44.280
where you're actually seeing
things through your eyes

01:30:44.280 --> 01:30:46.110
and then getting into the language system.

01:30:46.110 --> 01:30:50.790
Well, it turns out that
the auditory speech cortex

01:30:50.790 --> 01:30:54.720
is the primal and
primitive fundamental area

01:30:54.720 --> 01:30:55.827
that's really important for speech,

01:30:55.827 --> 01:30:57.930
and what happens with the reading

01:30:57.930 --> 01:31:01.680
is once it gets through
that visual cortex,

01:31:01.680 --> 01:31:05.220
it's going to try to map
those reading signals

01:31:05.220 --> 01:31:06.120
to the part of the brain

01:31:06.120 --> 01:31:08.820
that's trying to make sense of sounds,

01:31:08.820 --> 01:31:12.570
the sounds of words,
what we call phonology.

01:31:12.570 --> 01:31:14.280
Now, why is this important?

01:31:14.280 --> 01:31:18.723
It has a lot of relevance
to how we learn to write.

01:31:19.980 --> 01:31:23.760
And in some kids with dyslexia...

01:31:23.760 --> 01:31:27.030
Dyslexia is a neurological condition

01:31:27.030 --> 01:31:30.840
where a child, in some cases, an adult,

01:31:30.840 --> 01:31:34.650
has trouble reading, for example.

01:31:34.650 --> 01:31:37.320
And in many of those cases,

01:31:37.320 --> 01:31:41.940
it's because that mapping
between how we see the words

01:31:41.940 --> 01:31:45.840
to the way that the brain
processes the sounds

01:31:45.840 --> 01:31:47.220
is something different,

01:31:47.220 --> 01:31:48.390
it's a little bit different

01:31:48.390 --> 01:31:51.810
than people who can read really well.

01:31:51.810 --> 01:31:53.970
So when you're reading, a lot of times,

01:31:53.970 --> 01:31:56.280
you're actually activating
the part of the brain

01:31:56.280 --> 01:31:58.893
that is processing the
words that you hear.

01:32:00.600 --> 01:32:04.263
- What is the current
treatment for dyslexia?

01:32:06.060 --> 01:32:07.920
I've heard that it's a deficit

01:32:07.920 --> 01:32:10.260
in some of the motion processing systems

01:32:10.260 --> 01:32:11.880
of the visual system.

01:32:11.880 --> 01:32:13.410
You know, people, their eyes are jumping

01:32:13.410 --> 01:32:15.570
as opposed to more linear reading across,

01:32:15.570 --> 01:32:17.760
or I suppose if it were
Chinese it would be...

01:32:17.760 --> 01:32:18.930
You know, I don't want to presume

01:32:18.930 --> 01:32:20.220
people are always reading English.

01:32:20.220 --> 01:32:21.330
Or I suppose if it's Hebrew,

01:32:21.330 --> 01:32:24.453
they're going from the
opposite side of the page.

01:32:25.710 --> 01:32:27.750
What can be done for dyslexia?

01:32:27.750 --> 01:32:31.080
And do any of the modern
treatments for dyslexia

01:32:31.080 --> 01:32:34.800
involve changing things
from the speech side

01:32:34.800 --> 01:32:37.020
as opposed to just the,
quote, unquote, reading side,

01:32:37.020 --> 01:32:39.330
given that speech and
reading are interconnected?

01:32:39.330 --> 01:32:40.350
- Yeah, absolutely.

01:32:40.350 --> 01:32:42.420
So, again, I think in the beginning,

01:32:42.420 --> 01:32:43.410
people might have thought

01:32:43.410 --> 01:32:46.380
this was purely a visual abstraction

01:32:46.380 --> 01:32:48.750
or something really just
about the visual system,

01:32:48.750 --> 01:32:51.000
but there's been more recognition

01:32:51.000 --> 01:32:53.880
that it could be both
or it could be either,

01:32:53.880 --> 01:32:56.193
depending on the particular instance.

01:32:57.210 --> 01:33:00.930
It's very clear that there
are many kids with dyslexia

01:33:00.930 --> 01:33:05.310
where the problem is a problem
of a phonological awareness.

01:33:05.310 --> 01:33:09.720
So, you know, it can
be very hard to detect

01:33:09.720 --> 01:33:12.180
because they may understand
the words that you were saying,

01:33:12.180 --> 01:33:16.080
but because the brain is so
good at pattern recognition,

01:33:16.080 --> 01:33:18.270
sometimes even if the
individual speech sounds

01:33:18.270 --> 01:33:20.820
are not crystal clear,
it can compensate that,

01:33:20.820 --> 01:33:24.480
so that you can have an
individual who can hear the words

01:33:24.480 --> 01:33:28.620
but not be able to essentially hear them

01:33:28.620 --> 01:33:30.687
when they're reading those same words.

01:33:30.687 --> 01:33:33.300
And so what can happen with that

01:33:33.300 --> 01:33:35.790
is that you can have this disconnection

01:33:35.790 --> 01:33:38.190
between what they're seeing

01:33:38.190 --> 01:33:40.560
and what they need in
order to hear it as words

01:33:40.560 --> 01:33:42.450
and process it as language.

01:33:42.450 --> 01:33:45.640
And so skilled readers

01:33:46.650 --> 01:33:48.600
usually need that route first.

01:33:48.600 --> 01:33:51.450
They've got to map the vision to the sound

01:33:51.450 --> 01:33:53.520
in order to get that
sort of like foundation.

01:33:53.520 --> 01:33:57.300
But then over time, the reading
has its direct connection

01:33:57.300 --> 01:33:59.190
to the language parts of the brain,

01:33:59.190 --> 01:34:03.080
and we don't necessarily
always need to map to sounds.

01:34:03.080 --> 01:34:06.120
You know, you can basically
develop a parallel route,

01:34:06.120 --> 01:34:10.200
and we, as readers, actually
use both all the time.

01:34:10.200 --> 01:34:11.970
So for example, if it's a new word

01:34:11.970 --> 01:34:13.650
that you've never seen before,

01:34:13.650 --> 01:34:16.650
sometimes you try to, like,
pronounce it in your mind,

01:34:16.650 --> 01:34:19.620
you know, and try to
hear what that word is.

01:34:19.620 --> 01:34:21.210
Even though you're not actually saying it,

01:34:21.210 --> 01:34:22.320
you're trying to just generate

01:34:22.320 --> 01:34:25.047
what those sounds might be like.

01:34:25.047 --> 01:34:28.470
And that's the part where
we're kind of relying

01:34:28.470 --> 01:34:31.080
on how we learn to read
in the first place,

01:34:31.080 --> 01:34:33.600
which is mapping those word images

01:34:33.600 --> 01:34:36.390
to the sounds that, you
know, go along with them.

01:34:36.390 --> 01:34:39.450
But in other times, if you're
a really proficient reader,

01:34:39.450 --> 01:34:40.560
you're just seeing the words

01:34:40.560 --> 01:34:42.450
and you can map them directly to meaning

01:34:42.450 --> 01:34:47.040
without having to go through that process.

01:34:47.040 --> 01:34:49.440
- Yeah, I'm a big fan of
listening to audiobooks,

01:34:49.440 --> 01:34:53.400
and of course I also listen
to podcasts quite a lot,

01:34:53.400 --> 01:34:55.440
but I also am a strong believer,

01:34:55.440 --> 01:34:57.540
based on the research that I've seen,

01:34:57.540 --> 01:34:59.880
that reading books, physical books,

01:34:59.880 --> 01:35:01.020
it could be on a Kindle, I suppose,

01:35:01.020 --> 01:35:04.380
but reading a physical book is useful

01:35:04.380 --> 01:35:09.380
for being able to articulate
well and structure sentences

01:35:09.540 --> 01:35:11.370
and build what are essentially paragraphs,

01:35:11.370 --> 01:35:12.390
which is what I'm required to do

01:35:12.390 --> 01:35:15.003
when I do solo episodes of the podcast.

01:35:16.050 --> 01:35:17.430
I've noticed over the years

01:35:17.430 --> 01:35:21.310
as text messaging has become more popular

01:35:22.230 --> 01:35:26.220
and there's essentially
an erosion of punctuation

01:35:26.220 --> 01:35:27.960
or the need to have complete sentences,

01:35:27.960 --> 01:35:31.470
and now that's sort of
transferred to email as well.

01:35:31.470 --> 01:35:32.640
It's become acceptable

01:35:32.640 --> 01:35:36.333
to just say, you know,
fragmented sentences in email.

01:35:38.400 --> 01:35:40.890
It seems likely that
it's starting to impact

01:35:40.890 --> 01:35:42.930
the way that people speak as well.

01:35:42.930 --> 01:35:45.120
And I don't think this has
anything to do with intelligence

01:35:45.120 --> 01:35:47.040
or education level,

01:35:47.040 --> 01:35:50.280
but are you aware of any evidence

01:35:50.280 --> 01:35:53.820
that how we read and what we read

01:35:53.820 --> 01:35:56.700
and whether or not we consume information

01:35:56.700 --> 01:36:00.270
purely through reading or
mainly through auditory sources,

01:36:00.270 --> 01:36:01.710
does it change the way that we speak?

01:36:01.710 --> 01:36:04.470
Because, after all,
Wernicke's and Broca's area

01:36:04.470 --> 01:36:07.260
and the other auditory and
speech production areas

01:36:07.260 --> 01:36:09.897
are heavily intermeshed,

01:36:09.897 --> 01:36:13.977
and so it would make perfect
sense to me that what we hear

01:36:13.977 --> 01:36:16.470
and the patterns of sound that
are being communicated to us

01:36:16.470 --> 01:36:18.960
would also change the way that we speak.

01:36:18.960 --> 01:36:22.743
- Yeah, that's a really fascinating point.

01:36:23.580 --> 01:36:24.630
There is this idea

01:36:24.630 --> 01:36:27.390
that there's, like, this
proper way to speak,

01:36:27.390 --> 01:36:30.063
like that there's the
right way, for example,

01:36:31.110 --> 01:36:32.700
what are the appropriate, you know...

01:36:32.700 --> 01:36:37.477
Like, for example, in school,
you're oftentimes told, like,

01:36:37.477 --> 01:36:38.520
"You should say it like this,

01:36:38.520 --> 01:36:40.170
not say it like that," you know?

01:36:40.170 --> 01:36:42.060
And every language kind of has that.

01:36:42.060 --> 01:36:45.240
It turns out that that's really unnatural.

01:36:45.240 --> 01:36:49.440
Languages, and speech in
particular, change over time,

01:36:49.440 --> 01:36:54.180
it evolves, and it can
happen very quickly.

01:36:54.180 --> 01:36:56.607
You know, the things that we
call dialects, for example,

01:36:56.607 --> 01:36:57.867
are just different ways of speaking,

01:36:57.867 --> 01:36:59.910
and someone can just be in one environment

01:36:59.910 --> 01:37:02.310
and change from one dialect to another,

01:37:02.310 --> 01:37:06.600
and some people, it
kind of is really fixed.

01:37:06.600 --> 01:37:11.040
And there is this idea that,
you know, like in school

01:37:11.040 --> 01:37:13.320
that we're told that
there's this right way,

01:37:13.320 --> 01:37:17.010
but in reality, that's not true.

01:37:17.010 --> 01:37:19.140
Like, language change and speech change

01:37:19.140 --> 01:37:23.130
is completely normal and
happens all the time,

01:37:23.130 --> 01:37:25.110
and it can be really dramatic.

01:37:25.110 --> 01:37:27.330
Like, certain cultures and communities,

01:37:27.330 --> 01:37:29.160
if they are isolated,

01:37:29.160 --> 01:37:30.570
they can develop a whole new language,

01:37:30.570 --> 01:37:33.330
a whole new set of words, for example,

01:37:33.330 --> 01:37:37.050
and new ways and dialects that
are independent from people

01:37:37.050 --> 01:37:41.010
to the point where it's
unintelligible even to others.

01:37:41.010 --> 01:37:45.100
And so the basic idea is that sound change

01:37:46.140 --> 01:37:48.840
is part of the way it works,

01:37:48.840 --> 01:37:52.743
and the brain is very sensitive
to those kind of changes.

01:37:53.580 --> 01:37:55.430
- Speaking of learning new languages,

01:37:56.460 --> 01:37:58.920
I'm assuming it's possible
to learn new languages

01:37:58.920 --> 01:38:01.020
throughout the lifespan, correct?

01:38:01.020 --> 01:38:02.010
- [Eddie] Yeah.

01:38:02.010 --> 01:38:05.340
- I've also heard these
kind of fantastical stories

01:38:05.340 --> 01:38:07.350
of somebody has a stroke

01:38:07.350 --> 01:38:12.180
and then suddenly, spontaneously,
can speak French fluently,

01:38:12.180 --> 01:38:14.910
whereas prior to the
stroke, they could not.

01:38:14.910 --> 01:38:17.402
Is there any merit to
those stories whatsoever?

01:38:17.402 --> 01:38:18.235
[Eddie laughs]

01:38:18.235 --> 01:38:20.310
I find it very hard to believe

01:38:20.310 --> 01:38:22.770
that there was a complete
map representation

01:38:22.770 --> 01:38:24.270
of a language in somebody's brain

01:38:24.270 --> 01:38:26.760
that they were completely unaware of,

01:38:26.760 --> 01:38:30.630
and then because of
damage to a brain area,

01:38:30.630 --> 01:38:34.290
that capacity to speak that
language was somehow unveiled.

01:38:34.290 --> 01:38:36.870
It just seems too wild,

01:38:36.870 --> 01:38:38.430
and I don't want to say good to be true

01:38:38.430 --> 01:38:40.020
because nobody wants a stroke,

01:38:40.020 --> 01:38:44.040
but it just seems out
outrageously implausible.

01:38:44.040 --> 01:38:45.660
- Well, there are aspects of that

01:38:45.660 --> 01:38:47.400
that certainly are implausible.

01:38:47.400 --> 01:38:51.450
So I don't know of any true
case that I've ever seen

01:38:51.450 --> 01:38:54.300
or experienced myself or even read about

01:38:54.300 --> 01:38:56.910
where, for example, there
was an injury to the brain

01:38:56.910 --> 01:39:00.033
that resulted in loss of,

01:39:01.320 --> 01:39:03.210
well, essentially a gain of function,

01:39:03.210 --> 01:39:05.970
meaning, like, just all of a sudden

01:39:05.970 --> 01:39:08.400
started speaking another language.

01:39:08.400 --> 01:39:09.660
So for example, if you had a stroke

01:39:09.660 --> 01:39:11.250
and you never spoke French,

01:39:11.250 --> 01:39:12.210
and then you had it

01:39:12.210 --> 01:39:14.310
and then all of a sudden you're speaking,

01:39:14.310 --> 01:39:16.860
that, I've never heard of and never seen.

01:39:16.860 --> 01:39:21.780
However, there is a condition
that is well acknowledged

01:39:21.780 --> 01:39:23.040
and I have seen one case of this

01:39:23.040 --> 01:39:25.710
called a foreign accent syndrome,

01:39:25.710 --> 01:39:29.580
which is peculiar because there are people

01:39:29.580 --> 01:39:31.830
who have an injury to
the part of the brain

01:39:31.830 --> 01:39:34.620
where it sounds like they're starting

01:39:34.620 --> 01:39:36.120
to speak this other language,

01:39:36.120 --> 01:39:37.940
but they're not actually
speaking the language,

01:39:37.940 --> 01:39:40.620
it just sounds like it.

01:39:40.620 --> 01:39:43.650
And this goes back to what
we were talking about earlier

01:39:43.650 --> 01:39:46.470
about these areas that
are really important

01:39:46.470 --> 01:39:49.800
for speech control of the vocal tract,

01:39:49.800 --> 01:39:51.603
this area in the precentral gyrus.

01:39:52.590 --> 01:39:54.300
People have documented

01:39:54.300 --> 01:39:57.210
where, you know, patients
have had strokes there,

01:39:57.210 --> 01:40:01.740
and after that, it sounds
like they're speaking Spanish

01:40:01.740 --> 01:40:02.820
as opposed to English

01:40:02.820 --> 01:40:05.910
or it sounds like they have
the intonational properties

01:40:05.910 --> 01:40:08.340
of French or Russian

01:40:08.340 --> 01:40:11.610
as compared to their
original native language.

01:40:11.610 --> 01:40:13.140
They're not learning all the rest of it,

01:40:13.140 --> 01:40:16.650
like the meaning and
the grammar, et cetera,

01:40:16.650 --> 01:40:20.430
but they're adopting
some of the phonology,

01:40:20.430 --> 01:40:23.520
and part of that is just
because it's not working

01:40:23.520 --> 01:40:25.590
the way it normally does.

01:40:25.590 --> 01:40:26.423
So there is something

01:40:26.423 --> 01:40:28.380
actually called a foreign accent syndrome

01:40:28.380 --> 01:40:30.183
that people can have after a stroke.

01:40:31.800 --> 01:40:34.740
- Interesting. I'm curious
about auditory memory.

01:40:34.740 --> 01:40:37.980
When I was a kid, I used
to get into bed at night,

01:40:37.980 --> 01:40:42.090
and I'd close my eyes and I
would replay conversations

01:40:42.090 --> 01:40:44.760
that I had heard during
the day or people's voices.

01:40:44.760 --> 01:40:47.070
I actually can remember calling your house

01:40:47.070 --> 01:40:48.390
when we were young kids,

01:40:48.390 --> 01:40:50.100
and because I don't speak any Chinese

01:40:50.100 --> 01:40:51.780
but I'd have to ask for you,

01:40:51.780 --> 01:40:54.668
I'd say, I think it was,
Eddie [speaking Chinese].

01:40:54.668 --> 01:40:55.501
- Yeah.

01:40:55.501 --> 01:40:58.120
- Yeah, and then whoever
answered the phone

01:40:59.284 --> 01:41:00.720
would go get you, and then
I'd say [speaking Chinese],

01:41:00.720 --> 01:41:02.580
which I believe means thank you, right?

01:41:02.580 --> 01:41:06.060
That's the total of the Chinese
that I speak, by the way,

01:41:06.060 --> 01:41:09.060
but I will never forget that.

01:41:09.060 --> 01:41:10.710
I'll just never forget it, I hope.

01:41:10.710 --> 01:41:12.810
I suppose if I have a stroke
or something of that sort,

01:41:12.810 --> 01:41:13.710
at some point, I'll forget it

01:41:13.710 --> 01:41:15.090
and I won't know that I've forgotten it.

01:41:15.090 --> 01:41:18.360
But in all seriousness, I
remember that to this day.

01:41:18.360 --> 01:41:20.610
I couldn't spell that
out, I wouldn't know how,

01:41:20.610 --> 01:41:21.860
certainly not in Chinese,

01:41:22.867 --> 01:41:24.510
but even a transliteration,

01:41:24.510 --> 01:41:28.893
I couldn't do using English letters.

01:41:30.210 --> 01:41:33.903
Where are memories of sounds stored?

01:41:34.890 --> 01:41:38.070
Because within our days
and across our lives,

01:41:38.070 --> 01:41:42.750
we have an infinite number
of auditory experiences,

01:41:42.750 --> 01:41:46.200
just like we have an infinite
number of visual experiences.

01:41:46.200 --> 01:41:47.033
Where are they stored,

01:41:47.033 --> 01:41:48.780
and what is the structure
of their storage?

01:41:48.780 --> 01:41:51.210
What am I calling upon,

01:41:51.210 --> 01:41:53.400
besides, of course, the motor commands

01:41:53.400 --> 01:41:56.040
that are required to say
what I just said in Chinese,

01:41:56.040 --> 01:41:57.060
which I won't repeat again,

01:41:57.060 --> 01:41:59.450
'cause I somehow managed to
get it right the first time,

01:41:59.450 --> 01:42:00.930
or at least not terribly wrong,

01:42:00.930 --> 01:42:03.600
then I don't want to
botch it the second time.

01:42:03.600 --> 01:42:06.270
Where is that stored,
and how does that work?

01:42:06.270 --> 01:42:08.070
And, more importantly,

01:42:08.070 --> 01:42:10.230
as I speak my native language, English,

01:42:10.230 --> 01:42:12.420
am I pulling from a memory bank?

01:42:12.420 --> 01:42:14.040
Because it doesn't feel like it.

01:42:14.040 --> 01:42:17.130
I'm just telling you what I want to say.

01:42:17.130 --> 01:42:20.850
I'm doing my best to communicate
clearly and succinctly.

01:42:20.850 --> 01:42:22.830
I'm usually not so good
at the succinct part.

01:42:22.830 --> 01:42:27.000
But where is the bank of information?

01:42:27.000 --> 01:42:31.050
On my keyboard on my
computer, I have the letters,

01:42:31.050 --> 01:42:33.780
and I have certain elements
of punctuation and the space.

01:42:33.780 --> 01:42:37.683
What am I pulling from? Am I
pulling from those plosives?

01:42:38.940 --> 01:42:42.390
But if so, how can I do it so quickly?

01:42:42.390 --> 01:42:44.460
Even for people that speak slowly,

01:42:44.460 --> 01:42:47.430
it appears more or less fluid.

01:42:47.430 --> 01:42:51.540
This, to me, is overwhelmingly impressive

01:42:51.540 --> 01:42:53.043
that the brain can do that.

01:42:53.970 --> 01:42:54.970
How does it do that?

01:42:56.460 --> 01:42:58.110
- Well, first of all,

01:42:58.110 --> 01:43:01.590
I am impressed that 35
years later... [laughs]

01:43:01.590 --> 01:43:02.560
- Well, I had to get
ahold of you. [laughs]

01:43:02.560 --> 01:43:06.090
- Yeah, so I am impressed, 35 years later,

01:43:06.090 --> 01:43:09.930
that you can still remember that.

01:43:09.930 --> 01:43:11.460
- But only that.

01:43:11.460 --> 01:43:15.123
- That's fine, but I'm
still very impressed.

01:43:16.110 --> 01:43:18.410
But it clearly was
something important to you.

01:43:19.440 --> 01:43:24.440
So the short answer is that
memory is very distributed.

01:43:24.690 --> 01:43:27.840
So it's almost like the
question that you asked me

01:43:27.840 --> 01:43:31.950
is ill posed 'cause you asked me where?

01:43:31.950 --> 01:43:34.080
Well, it's not one specific area.

01:43:34.080 --> 01:43:36.870
It's actually really distributed.

01:43:36.870 --> 01:43:38.040
It's not just one particular area.

01:43:38.040 --> 01:43:39.870
In fact, I'm fairly certain

01:43:39.870 --> 01:43:41.760
that if we were to injure
that part of the brain

01:43:41.760 --> 01:43:43.140
called the Wernicke's area,

01:43:43.140 --> 01:43:46.440
you may still even have memories of that.

01:43:46.440 --> 01:43:48.900
People can have injuries of Broca's area

01:43:48.900 --> 01:43:50.850
or certainly the precentral gyrus

01:43:50.850 --> 01:43:54.960
and be able to sing "Happy
Birthday," for example,

01:43:54.960 --> 01:43:56.160
when it's embedded in melody

01:43:56.160 --> 01:43:59.760
or highly rehearsed things like counting

01:43:59.760 --> 01:44:03.510
despite not being able to speak,
which is incredible, right?

01:44:03.510 --> 01:44:06.990
It's like you can see a patient,

01:44:06.990 --> 01:44:10.890
for example, who can't really
put together a sentence.

01:44:10.890 --> 01:44:11.877
You ask them, "How are you feeling today?"

01:44:11.877 --> 01:44:14.460
And they can't even utter a word.

01:44:14.460 --> 01:44:16.287
But then you ask them to count sometimes,

01:44:16.287 --> 01:44:19.170
and they'll get up to any number really.

01:44:19.170 --> 01:44:21.690
And so there are some things

01:44:21.690 --> 01:44:24.330
that are really built
into our motor memory

01:44:24.330 --> 01:44:25.440
and it's distributed.

01:44:25.440 --> 01:44:27.510
It's not one particular part of the brain,

01:44:27.510 --> 01:44:29.430
it's actually multiple areas

01:44:29.430 --> 01:44:30.840
where that memory is distributed.

01:44:30.840 --> 01:44:32.610
And thank God that's the way it is

01:44:32.610 --> 01:44:37.610
because it's very rare

01:44:37.830 --> 01:44:40.470
in the kind of surgeries that I do

01:44:40.470 --> 01:44:43.380
where you go in, you remove
a piece of the brain,

01:44:43.380 --> 01:44:47.040
that someone forgets these
kind of long-term memories

01:44:47.040 --> 01:44:51.750
or these long-term motor
skills that they have.

01:44:51.750 --> 01:44:53.760
That's very, very rare.

01:44:53.760 --> 01:44:56.640
It's the number one question
a patient will ask me,

01:44:56.640 --> 01:44:57.810
like, "Am I going to be the same?

01:44:57.810 --> 01:45:01.950
And am I going to remember,
you know, my wife?

01:45:01.950 --> 01:45:04.800
Or am I going to remember,

01:45:04.800 --> 01:45:06.150
you know, these thoughts of my birthday

01:45:06.150 --> 01:45:07.467
when I was 10 years old?"

01:45:08.970 --> 01:45:13.830
And I've never really seen
that kind of severe amnesia

01:45:13.830 --> 01:45:15.840
unless it's a very, very severe injury

01:45:15.840 --> 01:45:19.410
that involves almost the
entire brain, and thank God.

01:45:19.410 --> 01:45:21.420
So a lot of that information

01:45:21.420 --> 01:45:25.353
is really distributed
across the entire brain.

01:45:26.190 --> 01:45:31.170
- Speaking of storage
of and ability to speak,

01:45:31.170 --> 01:45:33.810
you are doing some amazing work

01:45:33.810 --> 01:45:37.260
and have achieved some pretty incredible,

01:45:37.260 --> 01:45:39.570
well-deserved recognition for your work

01:45:39.570 --> 01:45:43.710
in bringing language
out of paralyzed people,

01:45:43.710 --> 01:45:44.970
essentially allowing people

01:45:44.970 --> 01:45:47.910
who are locked into a paralyzed state

01:45:47.910 --> 01:45:51.720
or otherwise unable to articulate speech

01:45:51.720 --> 01:45:54.360
using brain-machine interfaces,

01:45:54.360 --> 01:45:57.360
essentially translating
the neural activity

01:45:57.360 --> 01:46:00.810
of areas of the brain
that would produce speech

01:46:00.810 --> 01:46:03.120
into hardware,

01:46:03.120 --> 01:46:07.260
wires and things of that sort,

01:46:07.260 --> 01:46:09.540
artificial, non-biological tools

01:46:09.540 --> 01:46:12.123
in order to allow paralyzed
people to communicate.

01:46:13.290 --> 01:46:14.220
We will provide a link

01:46:14.220 --> 01:46:16.647
to some of the popular
press coverage of that work

01:46:16.647 --> 01:46:17.880
and the original papers.

01:46:17.880 --> 01:46:19.830
But if you would be so kind

01:46:19.830 --> 01:46:23.820
as to tell us what those
experiments look like,

01:46:23.820 --> 01:46:26.070
who these people are who are locked in

01:46:26.070 --> 01:46:27.810
and that you allow to communicate,

01:46:27.810 --> 01:46:31.050
and then especially interesting to me,

01:46:31.050 --> 01:46:33.510
some of the directions that
you're taking this now,

01:46:33.510 --> 01:46:37.260
which is beyond just, you know,
people being able to think

01:46:37.260 --> 01:46:38.460
about what they want to say

01:46:38.460 --> 01:46:40.920
and words coming out on a
screen or through a microphone,

01:46:40.920 --> 01:46:43.110
but actually making the interactions

01:46:43.110 --> 01:46:45.420
between these people and the real world

01:46:45.420 --> 01:46:49.350
more elaborate and more real.

01:46:49.350 --> 01:46:50.790
If that seems mysterious to people,

01:46:50.790 --> 01:46:53.250
I'm going to let Eddie tell you
what they're doing with this

01:46:53.250 --> 01:46:56.250
rather than put any more detail on it.

01:46:56.250 --> 01:46:58.920
- Oh, okay. Well, thanks
for asking about this.

01:46:58.920 --> 01:47:03.210
This has really been some
of the exciting recent work

01:47:03.210 --> 01:47:04.113
from the lab.

01:47:04.950 --> 01:47:06.900
So for the last decade,

01:47:06.900 --> 01:47:09.630
we've really been focusing
on the basic science,

01:47:09.630 --> 01:47:11.400
meaning trying to understand

01:47:11.400 --> 01:47:16.400
how the brain extracts and
produces speech sounds and words.

01:47:18.030 --> 01:47:20.280
We've done a lot of work
trying to figure out

01:47:20.280 --> 01:47:21.760
how these parts of the brain

01:47:22.980 --> 01:47:24.360
control these individual elements

01:47:24.360 --> 01:47:27.120
that give rise to all words and meanings.

01:47:27.120 --> 01:47:30.960
And so it was about six years ago

01:47:30.960 --> 01:47:35.850
where we realized we actually
have a pretty good idea

01:47:35.850 --> 01:47:37.713
of how this code works.

01:47:39.480 --> 01:47:42.090
We had identified all of
these different elements

01:47:42.090 --> 01:47:45.240
that we could decode in
epilepsy patients, for example,

01:47:45.240 --> 01:47:47.490
when they had electrodes on the brain

01:47:47.490 --> 01:47:49.260
as part of their surgeries,

01:47:49.260 --> 01:47:51.570
we could decode all of
the different consonants

01:47:51.570 --> 01:47:52.680
and vowels of English.

01:47:52.680 --> 01:47:54.690
That was about six years ago.

01:47:54.690 --> 01:47:57.360
So a natural question was this,

01:47:57.360 --> 01:48:01.020
which is if we understand
that electrical code,

01:48:01.020 --> 01:48:05.550
can we use that to help
someone who is paralyzed

01:48:05.550 --> 01:48:08.220
and can't get those
signals out of the brain

01:48:08.220 --> 01:48:09.573
to speak normally?

01:48:10.650 --> 01:48:13.560
And that's in the setting
of people who are paralyzed.

01:48:13.560 --> 01:48:16.860
So there are a series of conditions,

01:48:16.860 --> 01:48:19.020
they include things
like brain stem stroke.

01:48:19.020 --> 01:48:20.970
The brain stem is the part of the brain

01:48:20.970 --> 01:48:23.910
that connects the cerebrum,
which is the top part,

01:48:23.910 --> 01:48:26.340
does our thinking and a
lot of the motor control,

01:48:26.340 --> 01:48:28.350
speech, language, everything,

01:48:28.350 --> 01:48:29.880
and the brain stem is what connects that

01:48:29.880 --> 01:48:31.350
to the spinal cord and the nerves

01:48:31.350 --> 01:48:32.970
that go out to the face and vocal tract.

01:48:32.970 --> 01:48:34.920
So if you have a stroke there,

01:48:34.920 --> 01:48:38.460
basically, you could be
thinking all the wild, creative,

01:48:38.460 --> 01:48:41.910
intelligent thoughts you have
in the mind and the cerebrum,

01:48:41.910 --> 01:48:44.700
but you can't get them out into words

01:48:44.700 --> 01:48:48.450
or you can't get them out to
your hand to write them down.

01:48:48.450 --> 01:48:50.460
So that's a very severe form of paralysis

01:48:50.460 --> 01:48:52.470
called brain stem stroke.

01:48:52.470 --> 01:48:54.480
There's another kind of conditions

01:48:54.480 --> 01:48:56.070
that we call neurodegenerative

01:48:56.070 --> 01:49:00.930
where the nerve cells die
basically, or atrophy,

01:49:00.930 --> 01:49:03.693
in a condition called ALS,

01:49:04.680 --> 01:49:08.460
and that's a very severe
form of paralysis.

01:49:08.460 --> 01:49:09.450
In its extreme form,

01:49:09.450 --> 01:49:12.210
people essentially lose
all voluntary movement.

01:49:12.210 --> 01:49:14.070
- So Stephen Hawking
would be a good example

01:49:14.070 --> 01:49:16.140
of someone with ALS, Lou Gehrig's disease?

01:49:16.140 --> 01:49:18.630
- He's an example of someone who had ALS

01:49:18.630 --> 01:49:22.140
but not a great example of
what typical course of ALS.

01:49:22.140 --> 01:49:25.530
So for reasons not clear,

01:49:25.530 --> 01:49:28.380
the progression of his
disease largely stabilized

01:49:28.380 --> 01:49:32.040
to the point where he could
twitch, you know, a cheek muscle

01:49:32.040 --> 01:49:34.860
or move his eyes, let's say.

01:49:34.860 --> 01:49:37.800
In most people, it's very rapid,

01:49:37.800 --> 01:49:40.560
and many people, they
die from it, actually,

01:49:40.560 --> 01:49:43.420
you know, within a couple
of years of diagnosis, so-

01:49:43.420 --> 01:49:45.330
- Yeah, he lived a long time in that-

01:49:45.330 --> 01:49:46.532
- He lived a long time-

01:49:46.532 --> 01:49:47.880
- That slanted-over state

01:49:47.880 --> 01:49:50.100
in his wheelchair.
- Right, exactly.

01:49:50.100 --> 01:49:52.950
But he wasn't breathing,

01:49:52.950 --> 01:49:55.200
you know, through a tube
in his throat, for example,

01:49:55.200 --> 01:49:56.913
because people with severe ALS,

01:49:58.350 --> 01:50:01.290
the muscles to their
diaphragm and their lungs

01:50:01.290 --> 01:50:02.850
essentially give out as well.

01:50:02.850 --> 01:50:05.280
They get weakness there and
then they can't breathe anymore.

01:50:05.280 --> 01:50:08.850
So that's another form of paralysis.

01:50:08.850 --> 01:50:11.880
And so in our field,

01:50:11.880 --> 01:50:13.770
these are kind of like the
most devastating things

01:50:13.770 --> 01:50:14.763
that can happen.

01:50:15.720 --> 01:50:18.450
I'm not going to really try to
compare, like, what's worse,

01:50:18.450 --> 01:50:21.900
you know, having a brain tumor
or a stroke, it's all bad.

01:50:21.900 --> 01:50:26.370
But this condition of what
we call being locked in

01:50:26.370 --> 01:50:27.960
refers to this idea

01:50:27.960 --> 01:50:31.780
that you can have
completely intact cognition

01:50:33.180 --> 01:50:37.590
and awareness but have
no way to express that,

01:50:37.590 --> 01:50:40.950
no voluntary movement,
no ability to speak.

01:50:40.950 --> 01:50:42.240
And that is devastating

01:50:42.240 --> 01:50:45.090
because psychologically and socially,

01:50:45.090 --> 01:50:47.100
you know, you're completely isolated.

01:50:47.100 --> 01:50:49.080
That's what we call locked-in syndrome,

01:50:49.080 --> 01:50:50.250
and it's devastating.

01:50:50.250 --> 01:50:52.140
I've seen that throughout my career,

01:50:52.140 --> 01:50:54.150
and it's really heartbreaking

01:50:54.150 --> 01:50:59.150
because you know that the person is there

01:50:59.910 --> 01:51:02.850
but you can't see, they can't communicate.

01:51:02.850 --> 01:51:07.020
So we've been studying this patterning

01:51:07.020 --> 01:51:09.660
of electrical activity
for consonants and vowels,

01:51:09.660 --> 01:51:13.140
and essentially, once we
figured out a lot of these codes

01:51:13.140 --> 01:51:16.710
for the individual phonetic elements,

01:51:16.710 --> 01:51:20.460
we took a little bit of a detour,

01:51:20.460 --> 01:51:21.480
or at least part of the lab

01:51:21.480 --> 01:51:24.600
started to focus on this
very specific question.

01:51:24.600 --> 01:51:27.120
For people who have
these kind of paralysis,

01:51:27.120 --> 01:51:31.140
could we intercept those signals

01:51:31.140 --> 01:51:33.840
from the brain, the cerebral cortex,

01:51:33.840 --> 01:51:36.960
as someone is trying to say those words,

01:51:36.960 --> 01:51:38.490
and then can we intercept them

01:51:38.490 --> 01:51:42.990
and then have them taken out
of the brain through wires

01:51:42.990 --> 01:51:46.170
to a computer that are going
to interpret those signals

01:51:46.170 --> 01:51:48.900
and translate them into words?

01:51:48.900 --> 01:51:51.723
So about three years ago,

01:51:52.890 --> 01:51:54.510
we started a clinical trial.

01:51:54.510 --> 01:51:57.450
It's called the BRAVO
trial. It's still underway.

01:51:57.450 --> 01:52:00.690
And the first participant
in the BRAVO trial

01:52:00.690 --> 01:52:04.173
was a man who had been
paralyzed for 15 years.

01:52:06.660 --> 01:52:08.010
When he was about 20 years old,

01:52:08.010 --> 01:52:10.080
he came to the United States,

01:52:10.080 --> 01:52:13.710
was actually working in the Sonoma area

01:52:13.710 --> 01:52:15.660
and he was in a car accident,

01:52:15.660 --> 01:52:18.930
and he actually walked out of the hospital

01:52:18.930 --> 01:52:20.373
day after that car accident,

01:52:21.210 --> 01:52:25.320
but the next day, had a
complication related to it

01:52:25.320 --> 01:52:28.683
where he had a very large
stroke in the brain stem,

01:52:29.550 --> 01:52:31.980
and that turned out to be devastating.

01:52:31.980 --> 01:52:34.890
He didn't wake up from that
stroke for about a week.

01:52:34.890 --> 01:52:37.140
He was in a coma for about a week.

01:52:37.140 --> 01:52:38.850
And when he woke up from that coma,

01:52:38.850 --> 01:52:42.093
he realized that he couldn't
speak or move his arms or legs.

01:52:43.110 --> 01:52:47.760
And as he told me or communicated to us,

01:52:47.760 --> 01:52:49.230
that was absolutely devastating.

01:52:49.230 --> 01:52:52.350
He wanted really to die at that time.

01:52:52.350 --> 01:52:55.170
- Could he blink his eyes or
move his mouth in any way?

01:52:55.170 --> 01:52:56.910
- He could blink his eyes,

01:52:56.910 --> 01:52:58.320
he had some limited mouth movements

01:52:58.320 --> 01:53:01.260
but couldn't produce
any intelligible speech.

01:53:01.260 --> 01:53:04.503
It was, like, completely
slurred and incomprehensible.

01:53:05.460 --> 01:53:08.370
And he survived this injury.

01:53:08.370 --> 01:53:09.780
A lot of people who
have that kind of stroke

01:53:09.780 --> 01:53:11.490
just don't survive.

01:53:11.490 --> 01:53:12.660
But he survived.

01:53:12.660 --> 01:53:17.070
And I also realized that he's
just an incredible person,

01:53:17.070 --> 01:53:20.640
like a force of nature
in terms of his optimism,

01:53:20.640 --> 01:53:22.440
in terms of his ability to make friends

01:53:22.440 --> 01:53:23.400
despite his condition.

01:53:23.400 --> 01:53:25.680
The way he actually communicates,

01:53:25.680 --> 01:53:28.140
because he has a little bit
of residual neck movements,

01:53:28.140 --> 01:53:32.340
is that he improvised and had his friends

01:53:32.340 --> 01:53:37.020
basically put a stick
attached to his baseball cap.

01:53:37.020 --> 01:53:38.730
And because he could move his neck,

01:53:38.730 --> 01:53:43.260
he would essentially type out
letters on a keyboard screen

01:53:43.260 --> 01:53:44.093
to get out words.

01:53:44.093 --> 01:53:47.220
In fact, this is how he
communicated was through a device

01:53:47.220 --> 01:53:50.490
that he would essentially
peck out letters one by one

01:53:50.490 --> 01:53:51.960
by moving his neck

01:53:51.960 --> 01:53:54.840
to control this stick
attached to his baseball cap.

01:53:54.840 --> 01:53:58.470
- How many years did he use
that method of communication?

01:53:58.470 --> 01:53:59.760
- [Eddie] For about 15 years.

01:53:59.760 --> 01:54:02.220
He hadn't really spoken
for about 15 years.

01:54:02.220 --> 01:54:03.053
- Oh, goodness.

01:54:03.053 --> 01:54:06.810
- Yeah. So it was a devastating injury.

01:54:06.810 --> 01:54:10.500
But, you know, there's
something to be said

01:54:10.500 --> 01:54:12.750
about the human spirit,

01:54:12.750 --> 01:54:16.020
and if there's anyone who
embodies it, it is Pancho,

01:54:16.020 --> 01:54:19.023
that's his nickname, the first
participant in our trial.

01:54:20.430 --> 01:54:22.440
He has that human spirit, he persevered,

01:54:22.440 --> 01:54:24.600
and, in fact, you know, could thrive

01:54:24.600 --> 01:54:26.940
in his community, basically, and friends,

01:54:26.940 --> 01:54:27.773
being able to communicate

01:54:27.773 --> 01:54:29.733
in this very slow and inefficient way.

01:54:30.960 --> 01:54:33.780
Maybe part of that spirit
is why he volunteered

01:54:33.780 --> 01:54:36.120
to be the first person in this trial.

01:54:36.120 --> 01:54:39.630
It was a clinical trial, an
experiment. It was a study.

01:54:39.630 --> 01:54:42.690
This is not an approved
therapy by any means.

01:54:42.690 --> 01:54:45.990
This was really something
that had not been done before,

01:54:45.990 --> 01:54:50.067
and we had a lot of ideas
about it, but we didn't know.

01:54:50.067 --> 01:54:52.590
You know, we had proven a
lot of this could be true

01:54:52.590 --> 01:54:55.050
in some people who are normally speaking,

01:54:55.050 --> 01:54:57.120
but to actually put it into
someone who's paralyzed,

01:54:57.120 --> 01:55:00.480
number one, where we don't
know the code is the same.

01:55:00.480 --> 01:55:04.920
Number two is someone who's
not been speaking for 15 years,

01:55:04.920 --> 01:55:08.133
whether those signals are
actually still there or not.

01:55:09.720 --> 01:55:11.550
So it was part of a clinical trial.

01:55:11.550 --> 01:55:15.510
It was, you know,
something that our hospital

01:55:15.510 --> 01:55:17.940
and also the FDA, you know, had to approve

01:55:17.940 --> 01:55:19.110
and looked at very carefully,

01:55:19.110 --> 01:55:21.210
but given a lot of the
work that we had done,

01:55:21.210 --> 01:55:24.930
there was some basis
for why this might work.

01:55:24.930 --> 01:55:28.320
And so about two and a half years ago,

01:55:28.320 --> 01:55:32.340
we did a surgery where
we implanted electrodes

01:55:32.340 --> 01:55:35.040
onto the parts of the brain
that we've been talking about,

01:55:35.040 --> 01:55:37.620
these areas that control the vocal tract,

01:55:37.620 --> 01:55:39.030
the areas that control the larynx,

01:55:39.030 --> 01:55:43.200
the areas that control the lips
and tongue and jaw movements

01:55:43.200 --> 01:55:44.280
when we normally speak.

01:55:44.280 --> 01:55:47.100
These are areas that
presumably may be active,

01:55:47.100 --> 01:55:49.110
that was our hope, in his brain,

01:55:49.110 --> 01:55:51.000
but he just couldn't get those out

01:55:51.000 --> 01:55:55.110
to control his mouth in a normal way.

01:55:55.110 --> 01:55:56.310
And he underwent a surgery,

01:55:56.310 --> 01:55:59.370
a brain surgery where we
put an electrode array

01:55:59.370 --> 01:56:04.263
and we connected it to a port
that was screwed to his skull.

01:56:05.430 --> 01:56:08.280
And the port actually
goes through his scalp,

01:56:08.280 --> 01:56:11.790
and he's lived with this now
for the last three years.

01:56:11.790 --> 01:56:13.320
It is a risk of infection.

01:56:13.320 --> 01:56:15.630
These ports eventually
have to become wireless

01:56:15.630 --> 01:56:16.463
in the future.

01:56:16.463 --> 01:56:19.560
But we've figured out a
way to keep that port there

01:56:19.560 --> 01:56:23.550
where we can essentially
connect him to a computer

01:56:23.550 --> 01:56:24.603
through that port.

01:56:26.400 --> 01:56:29.220
So he has an electrode
array that's implanted

01:56:29.220 --> 01:56:32.760
over the part of his brain
that's important for speech,

01:56:32.760 --> 01:56:34.323
it's connected to a port,

01:56:35.370 --> 01:56:39.330
and then we connect a wire
to that port that translates

01:56:39.330 --> 01:56:43.170
those what we call analog,
you know, brainwaves

01:56:43.170 --> 01:56:46.170
and converts them into digital signals.

01:56:46.170 --> 01:56:49.950
And then a computer takes
those digital signals

01:56:49.950 --> 01:56:53.460
from those individual sites
from the speech cortex

01:56:53.460 --> 01:56:56.070
and translates those into words.

01:56:56.070 --> 01:57:00.900
- Can you describe for us the
first time that Pancho spoke

01:57:00.900 --> 01:57:03.720
through this engineered device?

01:57:03.720 --> 01:57:05.670
What was that experience like for you?

01:57:05.670 --> 01:57:07.920
And, at least from what
he conveyed to you,

01:57:07.920 --> 01:57:10.380
what was that experience like for him?

01:57:10.380 --> 01:57:12.360
Because as somebody who
was essentially locked-in,

01:57:12.360 --> 01:57:15.360
except for this, you know,
rather crude pecking device,

01:57:15.360 --> 01:57:19.620
although I'm thoroughly
impressed by how adaptive

01:57:19.620 --> 01:57:21.327
or adaptable Pancho was,

01:57:21.327 --> 01:57:23.850
and his friends engineering
that device for him

01:57:23.850 --> 01:57:26.680
is really nothing short of clever

01:57:27.900 --> 01:57:30.960
because otherwise he would
be truly locked in, right?

01:57:30.960 --> 01:57:34.470
But what was that moment
like? I can only imagine.

01:57:34.470 --> 01:57:36.033
- That moment was incredible.

01:57:36.900 --> 01:57:38.940
It was truly incredible

01:57:38.940 --> 01:57:41.520
to be able to see him
try to get out a word

01:57:41.520 --> 01:57:46.290
that was, for all practical
purposes, unintelligible,

01:57:46.290 --> 01:57:48.210
but to be able to take the brain activity

01:57:48.210 --> 01:57:51.558
and to translate it into text on a screen.

01:57:51.558 --> 01:57:52.391
And that's what we did.

01:57:52.391 --> 01:57:53.400
We took those brainwaves,

01:57:53.400 --> 01:57:56.070
we put them through a machine learning

01:57:56.070 --> 01:57:58.140
or artificial intelligence algorithm

01:57:58.140 --> 01:58:01.500
that can pick up these
very, very subtle patterns,

01:58:01.500 --> 01:58:03.600
you can't actually see them with your eye,

01:58:04.440 --> 01:58:08.010
in the brain activity and
translate those into words.

01:58:08.010 --> 01:58:12.097
And I remember seeing this
happening for the first time.

01:58:12.097 --> 01:58:14.580
You know, it doesn't
happen, like, immediately.

01:58:14.580 --> 01:58:18.960
This is something that took
weeks to train the algorithm

01:58:18.960 --> 01:58:20.433
to interpret it correctly.

01:58:22.350 --> 01:58:26.730
But what was incredible about
it was to see how he reacted.

01:58:26.730 --> 01:58:31.560
And he would be prompted
to say a given word,

01:58:31.560 --> 01:58:35.250
like, you know, outside, for example,

01:58:35.250 --> 01:58:37.770
and then he would think
about it, try to say it,

01:58:37.770 --> 01:58:41.073
and finally those words
would appear on the screen.

01:58:41.910 --> 01:58:43.770
And what was really amazing about it

01:58:43.770 --> 01:58:46.800
was you could really tell

01:58:46.800 --> 01:58:48.450
that he, like, got a kick out of that

01:58:48.450 --> 01:58:51.573
because he would start to giggle,

01:58:52.530 --> 01:58:54.990
you know, his body would shake in a way

01:58:54.990 --> 01:58:56.220
and his head would shake in a way

01:58:56.220 --> 01:59:00.150
that he would start to giggle,
and that was cool to see.

01:59:00.150 --> 01:59:03.420
But then I also realized
that when he was giggling,

01:59:03.420 --> 01:59:05.908
it kind of screwed up
the next word's decoding.

01:59:05.908 --> 01:59:07.470
[Andrew laughs]
[Eddie laughs]

01:59:07.470 --> 01:59:09.720
- Is that a bug you've since fixed?

01:59:09.720 --> 01:59:11.010
- No, we haven't fixed that.

01:59:11.010 --> 01:59:11.843
- Interesting.

01:59:11.843 --> 01:59:12.676
- We haven't fixed that,

01:59:12.676 --> 01:59:15.145
so it's easier just to
tell him to stop giggling.

01:59:15.145 --> 01:59:16.620
[Andrew laughs]
[Eddie laughs]

01:59:16.620 --> 01:59:18.810
- So what was the first word that he said?

01:59:18.810 --> 01:59:20.700
- Well, I think one of the first sentences

01:59:20.700 --> 01:59:21.810
that he put together

01:59:21.810 --> 01:59:24.453
was, you know, can you
get my family outside?

01:59:25.590 --> 01:59:26.790
- Meaning get them out
of the room? [laughs]

01:59:26.790 --> 01:59:27.630
- [Eddie] No, no.

01:59:27.630 --> 01:59:29.550
- All these years, he wanted
to get away from his family?

01:59:29.550 --> 01:59:31.590
- No, I think what he meant
was, can you get them-

01:59:31.590 --> 01:59:32.790
- Bring them in, yeah.
- Bring them in.

01:59:32.790 --> 01:59:35.040
And so the way this worked

01:59:35.040 --> 01:59:39.720
was we trained this computer
to recognize 50 words.

01:59:39.720 --> 01:59:42.480
We started with a very small vocabulary

01:59:42.480 --> 01:59:44.280
that's expanding as we speak.

01:59:44.280 --> 01:59:46.320
I think that this is just a matter of time

01:59:46.320 --> 01:59:50.280
before these vocabularies
become much, much larger.

01:59:50.280 --> 01:59:52.620
But we started with a 50-set of words.

01:59:52.620 --> 01:59:56.220
We created essentially
all the possible sentences

01:59:56.220 --> 01:59:59.250
that you could generate
from those 50 words.

01:59:59.250 --> 02:00:00.390
Why that was important

02:00:00.390 --> 02:00:03.780
was you can use all
those possible sentences

02:00:03.780 --> 02:00:06.810
to create a computational
model, computer model

02:00:06.810 --> 02:00:09.210
of all the different word combinations

02:00:09.210 --> 02:00:12.960
to give different sentences
given those 50 words,

02:00:12.960 --> 02:00:16.530
and then you can essentially
do what we call autocorrect.

02:00:16.530 --> 02:00:18.660
It's the same kind of thing that we do

02:00:18.660 --> 02:00:20.610
when you're texting, for example.

02:00:20.610 --> 02:00:23.220
You get the wrong letter in there,

02:00:23.220 --> 02:00:25.980
but your phone actually knows,

02:00:25.980 --> 02:00:29.070
you know, because of its
context what to correct it.

02:00:29.070 --> 02:00:32.850
So because the decoding's not
100% correct all the time,

02:00:32.850 --> 02:00:34.980
in fact, it's far from that,

02:00:34.980 --> 02:00:36.030
it's really helpful

02:00:36.030 --> 02:00:38.160
to have these other
features like autocorrect,

02:00:38.160 --> 02:00:40.950
the stuff that we use
routinely now with texting

02:00:40.950 --> 02:00:43.740
that makes it correct and then updates it.

02:00:43.740 --> 02:00:45.900
So it's a combination of a lot of things.

02:00:45.900 --> 02:00:47.910
It's the AI that is translating

02:00:47.910 --> 02:00:50.190
those brain activity patterns,

02:00:50.190 --> 02:00:52.500
but it's also things that we've learned

02:00:52.500 --> 02:00:55.080
from speech and speech technologies

02:00:55.080 --> 02:00:57.000
that, you know, we put all together

02:00:57.000 --> 02:00:59.430
and then all of a sudden,
it starts to work.

02:00:59.430 --> 02:01:01.770
And so we were really excited

02:01:01.770 --> 02:01:04.440
because that was the first
time that someone was paralyzed

02:01:04.440 --> 02:01:07.980
and could create words and sentences

02:01:07.980 --> 02:01:10.500
that was just decoded
from the brain activity.

02:01:10.500 --> 02:01:13.500
- Incredible, and I
know you're very humble,

02:01:13.500 --> 02:01:14.700
but I'm going to embarrass you by saying

02:01:14.700 --> 02:01:17.520
I always knew you were
destined for great things

02:01:17.520 --> 02:01:21.480
since the early age of nine
when we first became friends.

02:01:21.480 --> 02:01:26.430
But when I read the news
coverage of your work with Pancho

02:01:26.430 --> 02:01:29.070
and the release of this language

02:01:29.070 --> 02:01:31.200
from this locked-in patient,

02:01:31.200 --> 02:01:33.570
it literally, you know, it
brought tears to my eyes

02:01:33.570 --> 02:01:35.760
because, you know, it's
an interesting thing

02:01:35.760 --> 02:01:37.860
as fellow neuroscientists, right,

02:01:37.860 --> 02:01:41.670
we explore the brain and
we try and find mechanisms,

02:01:41.670 --> 02:01:44.160
and we try and compare those
to what other people find

02:01:44.160 --> 02:01:47.040
and find truths and principles
and build up from those,

02:01:47.040 --> 02:01:50.280
but pretty rarely is there a case

02:01:50.280 --> 02:01:53.670
where that route of exploration

02:01:53.670 --> 02:01:56.850
leads to something of
clinical significance

02:01:56.850 --> 02:01:58.530
within one's own lifetime.

02:01:58.530 --> 02:02:00.180
I mean, that's the reality of science,

02:02:00.180 --> 02:02:03.300
and oftentimes it's a
very distributed process.

02:02:03.300 --> 02:02:06.300
But in this case, it's
been a magnificent thing

02:02:06.300 --> 02:02:09.450
to see you move along this trajectory

02:02:09.450 --> 02:02:11.220
parsing these language and speech areas

02:02:11.220 --> 02:02:14.220
and then to also do the
clinical work in parallel.

02:02:14.220 --> 02:02:17.400
Speaking of which, these days,

02:02:17.400 --> 02:02:21.600
we hear a lot about Neuralink,
Elon Musk's company.

02:02:21.600 --> 02:02:24.510
A neurosurgeon that came
up briefly through my lab,

02:02:24.510 --> 02:02:26.850
but I can't take any credit
for what he knows or does,

02:02:26.850 --> 02:02:29.730
which is Matt MacDougall is
the neurosurgeon at Neuralink.

02:02:29.730 --> 02:02:31.440
There's some other excellent
neuroscientists there

02:02:31.440 --> 02:02:32.640
and engineers there.

02:02:32.640 --> 02:02:33.720
We hear a lot about Neuralink

02:02:33.720 --> 02:02:37.830
because while brain-machine interface

02:02:37.830 --> 02:02:40.890
of the sort that you do and
that other laboratories do

02:02:40.890 --> 02:02:42.590
has been going on for a long time,

02:02:43.500 --> 02:02:47.100
there's been some press around Neuralink

02:02:47.100 --> 02:02:50.460
about the promise of what
brain-machine interface could do.

02:02:50.460 --> 02:02:52.380
For instance, early in our discussion,

02:02:52.380 --> 02:02:54.750
you talked about how, you
know, language is constrained

02:02:54.750 --> 02:02:55.680
by these sound waves,

02:02:55.680 --> 02:02:58.170
and typically, it's a
few people communicating

02:02:58.170 --> 02:03:00.420
or one person with many people

02:03:00.420 --> 02:03:02.670
through a podcast, for
instance, or a speech.

02:03:03.540 --> 02:03:05.730
But the idea has been thrown out there

02:03:05.730 --> 02:03:08.550
that through the use of stimulating chips

02:03:08.550 --> 02:03:12.000
or through other brain-machine devicing

02:03:12.000 --> 02:03:15.180
that perhaps one could internalize

02:03:15.180 --> 02:03:17.940
50 conversations in parallel, right?

02:03:17.940 --> 02:03:20.013
50X communication,

02:03:21.000 --> 02:03:24.210
or that the memory
systems could be augmented

02:03:24.210 --> 02:03:26.660
to remember 10 times as much information

02:03:26.660 --> 02:03:29.970
or even twice as much information
in a given period of time.

02:03:29.970 --> 02:03:32.370
My understanding of what
they're doing at Neuralink,

02:03:32.370 --> 02:03:35.760
which is admittedly crude
and from the outside,

02:03:35.760 --> 02:03:37.260
a few discussions with people there,

02:03:37.260 --> 02:03:41.550
is that they too are going to
pursue clinical goals first,

02:03:41.550 --> 02:03:43.920
things like trying to
generate smooth movement

02:03:43.920 --> 02:03:45.513
in a Parkinsonian patient,

02:03:46.560 --> 02:03:49.290
trying to adjust movement patterns

02:03:49.290 --> 02:03:51.630
in someone with Huntington's
disease, for instance,

02:03:51.630 --> 02:03:52.780
things of that sort

02:03:53.820 --> 02:03:58.820
before they embark on the
more sci-fi-like explorations

02:03:59.700 --> 02:04:03.750
of 50Xing communication or
doubling memory capacity

02:04:03.750 --> 02:04:04.583
and these kinds of things.

02:04:04.583 --> 02:04:05.416
Although, I don't know,

02:04:05.416 --> 02:04:07.343
they may be doing all of
those things in parallel.

02:04:08.250 --> 02:04:13.250
What are your thoughts about
super capabilities of the brain

02:04:13.560 --> 02:04:16.290
or, I don't even know what word to use,

02:04:16.290 --> 02:04:17.580
you know, supercharging the brain,

02:04:17.580 --> 02:04:19.440
you know, giving the brain functions

02:04:19.440 --> 02:04:21.900
for which we've never observed before

02:04:21.900 --> 02:04:23.280
in human history, right?

02:04:23.280 --> 02:04:26.820
We have our Einsteins and our
Feynmans and our Merzenichs

02:04:26.820 --> 02:04:28.440
and, you know, it's unclear

02:04:28.440 --> 02:04:30.900
who to put in along
that line side by side,

02:04:30.900 --> 02:04:34.230
but there are some, the
Michael Jordans and et cetera,

02:04:34.230 --> 02:04:37.560
but we've never heard of or seen somebody

02:04:37.560 --> 02:04:40.080
who can jump 20 feet in the air.

02:04:40.080 --> 02:04:44.370
Or we've heard of people who
have photographic memories,

02:04:44.370 --> 02:04:46.140
but I don't know that we are aware

02:04:46.140 --> 02:04:48.060
of any human being in history

02:04:48.060 --> 02:04:51.660
who could memorize the
entire Library of Congress

02:04:51.660 --> 02:04:55.290
or all the works within
the Vatican within an hour.

02:04:55.290 --> 02:04:57.330
Anyway, you get the idea.

02:04:57.330 --> 02:05:01.740
What are your thoughts about
manipulating neural circuitry

02:05:01.740 --> 02:05:05.100
to achieve suprahuman or superhuman

02:05:05.100 --> 02:05:07.113
or supraphysiological functions?

02:05:08.250 --> 02:05:10.470
Are we there, or should we
even be thinking about that?

02:05:10.470 --> 02:05:13.500
Is it possible given that
neurons simply communicate

02:05:13.500 --> 02:05:14.520
through electrical activity

02:05:14.520 --> 02:05:16.530
and electrical activity can be engineered

02:05:16.530 --> 02:05:17.940
outside of the brain?

02:05:17.940 --> 02:05:19.470
How do you think about it?

02:05:19.470 --> 02:05:20.490
And here, we don't even have to think

02:05:20.490 --> 02:05:22.410
about Neuralink in particular.

02:05:22.410 --> 02:05:25.320
It's just but one example of companies

02:05:25.320 --> 02:05:26.430
and people in laboratories

02:05:26.430 --> 02:05:30.480
that are quite understandably
considering all this.

02:05:30.480 --> 02:05:34.620
- Well, it's a really
interesting time right now.

02:05:34.620 --> 02:05:37.620
The science has been going on for decades.

02:05:37.620 --> 02:05:38.820
The work that we've done

02:05:38.820 --> 02:05:41.370
in this field that you call
brain-machine interface

02:05:42.240 --> 02:05:44.040
has been going on for a while.

02:05:44.040 --> 02:05:45.690
And a lot of the early work

02:05:45.690 --> 02:05:48.870
was just trying to restore
things like arm movement

02:05:48.870 --> 02:05:50.610
or having people or monkeys

02:05:50.610 --> 02:05:53.550
control a computer cursor,
for example, on the screen.

02:05:53.550 --> 02:05:55.200
That's been going on for decades.

02:05:56.310 --> 02:06:01.310
What's been really new is
that industry is now involved

02:06:01.437 --> 02:06:03.690
and some of this is now
becoming commercialized,

02:06:03.690 --> 02:06:08.220
and we're starting to see us
now cross over to this field

02:06:08.220 --> 02:06:11.070
where it's no longer just research,

02:06:11.070 --> 02:06:14.400
that we're talking about medical
products that are designed

02:06:14.400 --> 02:06:17.730
to be, you know, surgically
implanted in some cases.

02:06:17.730 --> 02:06:19.410
You know, there's people
doing this kind of work

02:06:19.410 --> 02:06:22.833
non-invasively as well
that don't require surgery.

02:06:23.790 --> 02:06:26.820
The specific question
that you are asking about

02:06:26.820 --> 02:06:30.000
is an area that we call augmentation.

02:06:30.000 --> 02:06:34.470
So can you build a device

02:06:34.470 --> 02:06:37.530
that essentially enhances
someone's ability

02:06:37.530 --> 02:06:41.403
beyond supranormal, super memory,

02:06:42.240 --> 02:06:47.043
super communication speeds
beyond speech, for example,

02:06:49.590 --> 02:06:54.590
I guess superior precision
athletic abilities?

02:06:56.100 --> 02:06:58.470
I think that these are very
serious kind of questions

02:06:58.470 --> 02:06:59.790
to be asking now

02:06:59.790 --> 02:07:03.750
because, as you mentioned,
the pathway so far

02:07:03.750 --> 02:07:07.890
is really to focus on
these medical applications.

02:07:07.890 --> 02:07:10.680
I personally don't think that
we've thought enough actually

02:07:10.680 --> 02:07:13.890
about what these kind of
scenarios are going to look like,

02:07:13.890 --> 02:07:14.940
and I don't think we've thought through

02:07:14.940 --> 02:07:18.330
all the ethical implications
of what this means

02:07:18.330 --> 02:07:21.003
for augmentation in particular.

02:07:23.010 --> 02:07:26.820
There's part of this
that is not new at all.

02:07:26.820 --> 02:07:30.900
Humans throughout history
have been doing things

02:07:30.900 --> 02:07:32.553
to augment our function,

02:07:33.540 --> 02:07:36.960
coffee, nicotine, all kinds of things,

02:07:36.960 --> 02:07:39.780
all kinds of medications that cross over

02:07:39.780 --> 02:07:42.150
from medical to consumer.

02:07:42.150 --> 02:07:44.250
That is everywhere.

02:07:44.250 --> 02:07:49.110
So the pursuit of augmentation
or performance or enhancement

02:07:49.110 --> 02:07:50.733
is really not a new thing.

02:07:51.840 --> 02:07:53.520
The questions really,

02:07:53.520 --> 02:07:56.610
as they relate to
neurotechnologies, for example,

02:07:56.610 --> 02:07:59.880
have to do with the invasive nature.

02:07:59.880 --> 02:08:01.770
For example, if these technologies

02:08:01.770 --> 02:08:03.840
require surgery, for example,

02:08:03.840 --> 02:08:07.920
to do something that is not
for a medical application.

02:08:07.920 --> 02:08:12.180
Again, there, that is not
exactly new territory either.

02:08:12.180 --> 02:08:15.810
People do that routinely for
cosmetic kind of procedures

02:08:15.810 --> 02:08:18.870
for physical appearance,
not necessarily cognitive.

02:08:18.870 --> 02:08:23.520
So I do think that,
provided the technology

02:08:23.520 --> 02:08:26.730
continues to emerge the way that it does,

02:08:26.730 --> 02:08:29.520
that it's going to be around the corner,

02:08:29.520 --> 02:08:30.900
and it probably is not going to be in ways

02:08:30.900 --> 02:08:32.520
that are super obvious.

02:08:32.520 --> 02:08:34.380
I don't think it's going to be like,

02:08:34.380 --> 02:08:37.230
can we easily memorize
every fact in the world,

02:08:37.230 --> 02:08:40.260
but in forms that are going
to be much more incremental

02:08:40.260 --> 02:08:41.703
and maybe more subtle.

02:08:42.900 --> 02:08:44.880
In many ways, we already have that now.

02:08:44.880 --> 02:08:47.250
Like, for example, you don't
have to have a neural interface

02:08:47.250 --> 02:08:50.670
embedded in your brain to get information,

02:08:50.670 --> 02:08:53.130
essentially access to all
information in the world.

02:08:53.130 --> 02:08:55.800
You just have to have,
you know, your iPhone.

02:08:55.800 --> 02:09:00.573
Whether you could do it faster
through a brain interface,

02:09:02.250 --> 02:09:04.440
I definitely wouldn't rule that out.

02:09:04.440 --> 02:09:05.940
But think about this,

02:09:05.940 --> 02:09:08.220
that the systems that we have already

02:09:08.220 --> 02:09:09.780
to speak and to communicate

02:09:09.780 --> 02:09:14.550
have evolved over, you know,
thousands and millions of years

02:09:14.550 --> 02:09:17.220
and they're supported by neural structures

02:09:17.220 --> 02:09:20.583
that have bandwidth of
millions of neurons.

02:09:21.420 --> 02:09:24.960
There is no technology
that exists right now

02:09:24.960 --> 02:09:27.390
that people are thinking about
that are in commercial forms,

02:09:27.390 --> 02:09:29.790
certainly not even in research labs,

02:09:29.790 --> 02:09:32.070
that come anywhere close

02:09:32.070 --> 02:09:35.850
to what has been evolved
for those natural purposes.

02:09:35.850 --> 02:09:39.450
So I'm essentially
saying two sides of this,

02:09:39.450 --> 02:09:43.170
which is we're already
getting into this now,

02:09:43.170 --> 02:09:44.850
this is not new territory,

02:09:44.850 --> 02:09:48.870
this topic of augmentation,
both physical and cognitive,

02:09:48.870 --> 02:09:49.830
we've already surpassed that.

02:09:49.830 --> 02:09:52.593
That's part of what humans do in general.

02:09:53.760 --> 02:09:58.760
But we are entering this area
of, like, enhanced cognition,

02:09:59.400 --> 02:10:01.590
these areas that I think the technology

02:10:01.590 --> 02:10:04.620
is going to be the rate-limiting
step in how far it can go,

02:10:04.620 --> 02:10:06.720
and we have not had the full conversations

02:10:06.720 --> 02:10:10.023
about, number one, is this
what we actually want?

02:10:11.250 --> 02:10:12.930
Is this going to be good for society?

02:10:12.930 --> 02:10:15.540
Who gets access to this technology?

02:10:15.540 --> 02:10:16.410
These are all things

02:10:16.410 --> 02:10:18.510
that are going to become
real-world problems.

02:10:19.590 --> 02:10:21.330
- There's certainly a lot to consider.

02:10:21.330 --> 02:10:23.460
In thinking about augmentation

02:10:23.460 --> 02:10:26.850
and another theme that
I've yet to ask you about

02:10:26.850 --> 02:10:28.260
but I'm extremely curious about,

02:10:28.260 --> 02:10:30.600
which is facial expressions.

02:10:30.600 --> 02:10:32.190
Before we talk about the relationship

02:10:32.190 --> 02:10:34.920
between the musculature
of the face and language

02:10:34.920 --> 02:10:36.693
and the communication of emotion,

02:10:37.740 --> 02:10:41.070
I'd love for you to, if you
would, touch on a little bit

02:10:41.070 --> 02:10:43.050
of what you're doing
with patients like Pancho

02:10:43.050 --> 02:10:46.290
to move beyond somebody who's locked in

02:10:46.290 --> 02:10:48.570
being able to type out words on a screen

02:10:48.570 --> 02:10:49.593
with their thoughts.

02:10:50.730 --> 02:10:53.130
There's a rich array of information

02:10:53.130 --> 02:10:55.860
contained within the face
and facial expression.

02:10:55.860 --> 02:10:57.630
And while somebody like Pancho

02:10:57.630 --> 02:11:01.020
going from having to, you
know, be completely locked in

02:11:01.020 --> 02:11:03.750
to being able to peck
out letters on a keyboard

02:11:03.750 --> 02:11:05.460
to being able to just
think of those letters

02:11:05.460 --> 02:11:06.390
and having them spelled out,

02:11:06.390 --> 02:11:11.373
that's a tremendous set of
leaps forward towards normalcy.

02:11:12.390 --> 02:11:13.830
It's still far and away different

02:11:13.830 --> 02:11:16.290
than Pancho speaking with his mouth,

02:11:16.290 --> 02:11:19.743
which I think, knowing some
people who are restricted,

02:11:20.940 --> 02:11:21.930
who are quadriplegic,

02:11:21.930 --> 02:11:24.090
you know, a lot of what they
struggle with in the real world

02:11:24.090 --> 02:11:25.830
is actually a height difference sometimes

02:11:25.830 --> 02:11:28.609
because they're seated while
other people are standing.

02:11:28.609 --> 02:11:29.850
We don't often think about this,

02:11:29.850 --> 02:11:31.950
but to always have to look
up to communicate with people

02:11:31.950 --> 02:11:34.650
is a very different
interface in the world.

02:11:34.650 --> 02:11:35.880
They manage quite well, of course.

02:11:35.880 --> 02:11:38.340
But could you tell us what you're doing

02:11:38.340 --> 02:11:41.400
in terms of merging the
brain-machine interface

02:11:41.400 --> 02:11:43.230
with extraction of speech signals

02:11:43.230 --> 02:11:45.630
from people who are locked-in like Pancho

02:11:45.630 --> 02:11:47.070
with facial expressions?

02:11:47.070 --> 02:11:48.150
- Sure, yeah.

02:11:48.150 --> 02:11:53.150
Well, like we described
before, progress is being made.

02:11:54.630 --> 02:11:56.310
The proof of principle is out there

02:11:56.310 --> 02:11:57.810
that you can decode speech.

02:11:57.810 --> 02:11:59.250
That will continue to optimize,

02:11:59.250 --> 02:12:02.430
and I'm very confident that
that's going to improve

02:12:02.430 --> 02:12:04.920
very, very quickly in the coming years

02:12:04.920 --> 02:12:06.600
to the point where it's like,

02:12:06.600 --> 02:12:08.400
you know, not just a small vocabulary,

02:12:08.400 --> 02:12:12.240
but a large vocabulary
and at reasonable rates,

02:12:12.240 --> 02:12:14.070
at a level that's going
to be really helpful.

02:12:14.070 --> 02:12:15.753
I'm very optimistic about that.

02:12:16.650 --> 02:12:17.820
I think it's the right time

02:12:17.820 --> 02:12:20.670
to start really thinking
about a broader vision

02:12:20.670 --> 02:12:22.650
of what communication really is.

02:12:22.650 --> 02:12:26.610
So for example, I'm
here with you in person.

02:12:26.610 --> 02:12:29.100
We could have done this
virtually, probably,

02:12:29.100 --> 02:12:30.360
it's pretty easy to do that.

02:12:30.360 --> 02:12:32.610
We could've recorded this really separate,

02:12:32.610 --> 02:12:33.600
but there is something

02:12:33.600 --> 02:12:36.900
about being able to actually
see your expressions

02:12:36.900 --> 02:12:41.460
and to understand other
forms of communication.

02:12:41.460 --> 02:12:45.333
So another really
important one is nonverbal,

02:12:46.500 --> 02:12:48.270
the expressions that you're making.

02:12:48.270 --> 02:12:49.710
You know, for example,

02:12:49.710 --> 02:12:51.300
if you have a quizzical look on your face

02:12:51.300 --> 02:12:53.280
if I'm saying something not clear,

02:12:53.280 --> 02:12:55.830
that's a sign to me that
I need to rephrase it

02:12:55.830 --> 02:12:57.300
or to say it in a different way

02:12:57.300 --> 02:13:00.210
or to slow down, for example.

02:13:00.210 --> 02:13:02.340
Or if there's something
that really excites you,

02:13:02.340 --> 02:13:04.110
I want to continue to say more about it

02:13:04.110 --> 02:13:06.450
and talk more in detail,

02:13:06.450 --> 02:13:08.910
you know, essentially about a given thing.

02:13:08.910 --> 02:13:13.350
So facial expressions actually
are a really important part

02:13:13.350 --> 02:13:16.380
of the way we speak,
and there's two things.

02:13:16.380 --> 02:13:18.210
It's not just the expressions

02:13:18.210 --> 02:13:21.570
of, like, how you're feeling
and perceiving what I'm saying,

02:13:21.570 --> 02:13:24.957
but it's also seeing my mouth move

02:13:24.957 --> 02:13:27.240
and your eyes actually
seeing my mouth move

02:13:27.240 --> 02:13:29.130
and my jaw move in a particular way

02:13:29.130 --> 02:13:32.823
that actually allows you to
hear those sounds better.

02:13:34.350 --> 02:13:36.930
So having both the visual information

02:13:36.930 --> 02:13:39.750
but also the sounds go into your brain

02:13:39.750 --> 02:13:43.710
is going to improve intelligibility,
also make it more natural.

02:13:43.710 --> 02:13:46.740
- And memory for what is spoken?

02:13:46.740 --> 02:13:47.573
- Perhaps.

02:13:47.573 --> 02:13:50.250
- So here's a call for people
not just listening to podcasts

02:13:50.250 --> 02:13:52.740
but watching them and
listening to them on YouTube,

02:13:52.740 --> 02:13:55.230
I suppose, if we were to
sort of translate this

02:13:55.230 --> 02:13:57.387
to the real world.
- Exactly, exactly.

02:13:57.387 --> 02:13:59.760
And the reason why we're
also very interested

02:13:59.760 --> 02:14:04.710
in this idea of not just
having text on a screen,

02:14:04.710 --> 02:14:09.660
but essentially a fully
computer-animated face

02:14:09.660 --> 02:14:13.890
like an avatar of the
person's speech movements

02:14:13.890 --> 02:14:16.140
and their facial expressions

02:14:16.140 --> 02:14:19.383
is going to be a more
complete form of expression.

02:14:20.820 --> 02:14:23.100
Now, you can imagine right now,

02:14:23.100 --> 02:14:25.590
that might just be someone
looking at a computer screen

02:14:25.590 --> 02:14:26.880
interpreting these signals,

02:14:26.880 --> 02:14:29.760
but I think the way things are going,

02:14:29.760 --> 02:14:31.170
in the next couple of years,

02:14:31.170 --> 02:14:34.950
a lot more of our social
interactions, more than even now,

02:14:34.950 --> 02:14:37.950
are going to move into
this digital virtual space.

02:14:37.950 --> 02:14:40.320
And, of course, most people are thinking

02:14:40.320 --> 02:14:43.380
about what that means for most consumers,

02:14:43.380 --> 02:14:45.360
but it also has really
important implications

02:14:45.360 --> 02:14:47.130
for people who are disabled, right,

02:14:47.130 --> 02:14:50.400
and how are they going
to participate in that.

02:14:50.400 --> 02:14:52.140
And so we are thinking really about,

02:14:52.140 --> 02:14:55.920
for people like Pancho and
other people who are paralyzed,

02:14:55.920 --> 02:15:00.660
what other forms of BCI can we do

02:15:00.660 --> 02:15:03.000
in order to help improve
their ability to communicate?

02:15:03.000 --> 02:15:06.990
So one is essentially building
out more holistic avatars,

02:15:06.990 --> 02:15:11.520
you know, things that
can essentially decode,

02:15:11.520 --> 02:15:14.130
you know, essentially their expressions

02:15:14.130 --> 02:15:18.150
or the movements associated
with their mouth and jaw

02:15:18.150 --> 02:15:21.540
when they actually speak to
improve that communication.

02:15:21.540 --> 02:15:24.300
- So do you envision a
time not too long from now

02:15:24.300 --> 02:15:26.940
where instead of tweeting
out something in text,

02:15:26.940 --> 02:15:28.320
my avatar will,

02:15:28.320 --> 02:15:31.170
I'll type it out, but my
avatar will just say it,

02:15:31.170 --> 02:15:34.230
it'll be an image of my
avatar saying whatever it is

02:15:34.230 --> 02:15:35.615
I happen to be tweeting at that moment.

02:15:35.615 --> 02:15:36.660
- That's what we're working on, yeah.

02:15:36.660 --> 02:15:38.760
So I don't think that...

02:15:38.760 --> 02:15:40.980
That is going to happen and
it's going to happen soon,

02:15:40.980 --> 02:15:43.293
and there's a lot of progress in that.

02:15:43.293 --> 02:15:48.293
And, again, we're just
trying to enrich the field,

02:15:48.690 --> 02:15:50.590
you know, of communication expression

02:15:51.570 --> 02:15:52.737
to make it more normal.

02:15:52.737 --> 02:15:56.760
And we actually think that
having that kind of avatar

02:15:56.760 --> 02:15:59.640
is a way of getting feedback
to people learning how to speak

02:15:59.640 --> 02:16:02.190
through a speech neuroprosthetic,

02:16:02.190 --> 02:16:03.300
that's the device that we call,

02:16:03.300 --> 02:16:05.400
it's a speech neuroprosthetic,

02:16:05.400 --> 02:16:08.310
that that is going to be
the way that can help people

02:16:08.310 --> 02:16:09.900
learn how to do it the quickest.

02:16:09.900 --> 02:16:11.880
Not necessarily, like, trying to say words

02:16:11.880 --> 02:16:13.680
and having it come on a screen,

02:16:13.680 --> 02:16:16.470
but actually have people embody,

02:16:16.470 --> 02:16:18.390
feel like it's part of themselves

02:16:18.390 --> 02:16:20.240
or that they are directly controlling

02:16:21.090 --> 02:16:23.013
that illustration or animation.

02:16:24.000 --> 02:16:25.540
- This idea of an avatar

02:16:26.880 --> 02:16:30.510
speaking out what we would otherwise write

02:16:30.510 --> 02:16:31.410
is fascinating to me.

02:16:31.410 --> 02:16:35.550
On Instagram, I post
videos, I don't filter them,

02:16:35.550 --> 02:16:38.040
but I know there's a lot
of discussion nowadays

02:16:38.040 --> 02:16:41.160
about people using filters to
make their skin look different

02:16:41.160 --> 02:16:42.300
or the lighting look different,

02:16:42.300 --> 02:16:44.970
a lot of filtering and
also the use of captions,

02:16:44.970 --> 02:16:46.320
so that essentially what you end up with

02:16:46.320 --> 02:16:51.150
is somewhere between an actual
raw video of what was spoken

02:16:51.150 --> 02:16:52.600
and an avatar version of it.

02:16:52.600 --> 02:16:54.270
- Yeah.
- I mean, if the mismatch

02:16:54.270 --> 02:16:55.920
between what's spoken
and what's in the caption

02:16:55.920 --> 02:16:58.770
is too dramatic, then
it doesn't quite work.

02:16:58.770 --> 02:17:02.010
But I watch these carefully
when people use captions,

02:17:02.010 --> 02:17:04.560
and oftentimes there's a smoothing

02:17:04.560 --> 02:17:05.820
of what was said into the captions

02:17:05.820 --> 02:17:08.190
so it seems much more
succinct and accurate.

02:17:08.190 --> 02:17:10.230
Oftentimes, the reverse is also true

02:17:10.230 --> 02:17:11.610
where the caption is inaccurate

02:17:11.610 --> 02:17:14.610
and then it creates this
kind of jarring mismatch.

02:17:14.610 --> 02:17:17.460
In any case, I think this
aspect in the clinical realm

02:17:17.460 --> 02:17:19.770
of using an avatar to
allow people like Pancho

02:17:19.770 --> 02:17:21.330
to essentially be a face

02:17:21.330 --> 02:17:23.430
that communicates through spoken language

02:17:23.430 --> 02:17:25.500
from an avatar that looks like them

02:17:25.500 --> 02:17:28.410
is fascinating and indeed important,

02:17:28.410 --> 02:17:32.340
and I think how avatars
emerge in social spaces

02:17:32.340 --> 02:17:34.710
is going to be really fascinating.

02:17:34.710 --> 02:17:37.140
I get a lot of questions about stutter.

02:17:37.140 --> 02:17:39.380
I think that for people
who have a stutter,

02:17:39.380 --> 02:17:42.273
it is itself anxiety-provoking.

02:17:43.260 --> 02:17:45.900
Is stutter related to anxiety?

02:17:45.900 --> 02:17:49.353
If one has a stutter, what can they do?

02:17:50.850 --> 02:17:55.560
Does stutter reflect some
underlying neurologic phenomenon

02:17:55.560 --> 02:17:57.480
that might distinguish

02:17:57.480 --> 02:17:59.790
between one kind of stutter and another?

02:17:59.790 --> 02:18:01.230
What can people with stutter do

02:18:01.230 --> 02:18:02.700
if they'd like to relieve their stutter?

02:18:02.700 --> 02:18:04.143
- Yeah, great question.

02:18:05.700 --> 02:18:08.580
Stutter is a condition

02:18:08.580 --> 02:18:11.610
where the words can't come out fluently.

02:18:11.610 --> 02:18:14.640
So you have all the ideas,
you've got the language intact.

02:18:14.640 --> 02:18:16.200
You know, remember, we
talked about this distinction

02:18:16.200 --> 02:18:18.270
between language and speech.

02:18:18.270 --> 02:18:20.280
Stuttering is a problem of speech, right?

02:18:20.280 --> 02:18:24.510
So the ideas, the meanings,
the grammar, it's all there,

02:18:24.510 --> 02:18:25.440
and people stutter

02:18:25.440 --> 02:18:28.320
but they can't get the words out fluently.

02:18:28.320 --> 02:18:30.960
So that's a speech condition,

02:18:30.960 --> 02:18:32.850
and, in particular, it's a condition

02:18:32.850 --> 02:18:35.910
that affects articulation,

02:18:35.910 --> 02:18:40.170
specifically about controlling
the production of words

02:18:40.170 --> 02:18:42.420
in this really coordinated
kind of movements

02:18:42.420 --> 02:18:44.460
that have to happen in the vocal tract

02:18:44.460 --> 02:18:47.520
to produce fluent speech.

02:18:47.520 --> 02:18:50.880
And stuttering is a condition

02:18:50.880 --> 02:18:55.710
where people have a predisposition to it,

02:18:55.710 --> 02:18:58.650
so there is an aspect of stuttering,

02:18:58.650 --> 02:19:02.250
you are a stutterer or you're
not a stutterer, right?

02:19:02.250 --> 02:19:06.120
But people who stutter don't
stutter all the time either,

02:19:06.120 --> 02:19:07.380
so you could be a stutterer

02:19:07.380 --> 02:19:11.220
who stutters at some times but not others.

02:19:11.220 --> 02:19:15.510
And, really, the main link
between stuttering and anxiety

02:19:15.510 --> 02:19:20.510
is that anxiety can provoke
it and make it worse.

02:19:20.640 --> 02:19:23.160
That's certainly true,

02:19:23.160 --> 02:19:26.690
but it's not necessarily
caused by anxiety.

02:19:26.690 --> 02:19:30.720
It can essentially trigger
it or make it worse,

02:19:30.720 --> 02:19:33.390
but it's not the cause of it, per se.

02:19:33.390 --> 02:19:36.480
So the cause of it is
still really not clear,

02:19:36.480 --> 02:19:40.230
but it does have to do with
these kind of brain functions

02:19:40.230 --> 02:19:42.330
that we've been talking about earlier,

02:19:42.330 --> 02:19:47.163
which is that in order to
produce normal fluent speech,

02:19:48.060 --> 02:19:50.730
we're not even conscious
of what is going on

02:19:50.730 --> 02:19:53.100
in our mouths, in our larynx.

02:19:53.100 --> 02:19:53.933
We're not conscious,

02:19:53.933 --> 02:19:55.590
and if we were, we would
not be able to speak

02:19:55.590 --> 02:19:58.830
because it's too complex,
it's too precise.

02:19:58.830 --> 02:20:02.820
It's something that we
have really developed

02:20:02.820 --> 02:20:04.950
the abilities to do, and
we do it naturally, right?

02:20:04.950 --> 02:20:06.090
It's part of our programming

02:20:06.090 --> 02:20:08.670
and part of what we learn inherently,

02:20:08.670 --> 02:20:11.400
you know, just through exposure.

02:20:11.400 --> 02:20:16.400
So stuttering is essentially a breakdown

02:20:16.890 --> 02:20:18.220
at certain times

02:20:19.140 --> 02:20:21.270
in that machinery being able to work

02:20:21.270 --> 02:20:22.863
in a really coordinated way.

02:20:23.850 --> 02:20:26.610
You can think about, you know,
the operations of these areas

02:20:26.610 --> 02:20:28.500
that are controlling the vocal tract.

02:20:28.500 --> 02:20:30.060
Let's say speech is like a symphony.

02:20:30.060 --> 02:20:31.770
In order for it to come out normally,

02:20:31.770 --> 02:20:35.760
you've got to have not
just one part, the larynx,

02:20:35.760 --> 02:20:37.020
but the lips, the jaw.

02:20:37.020 --> 02:20:39.030
They can't be doing their own thing.

02:20:39.030 --> 02:20:43.890
They have to be very,
very precisely activated

02:20:43.890 --> 02:20:45.510
and very, very precisely controlled

02:20:45.510 --> 02:20:48.143
in a way to actually create words.

02:20:48.143 --> 02:20:49.170
And so in stuttering,

02:20:49.170 --> 02:20:51.660
there's a breakdown of that coordination.

02:20:51.660 --> 02:20:53.130
- If somebody has a stutter,

02:20:53.130 --> 02:20:55.920
is it better to address that early in life

02:20:55.920 --> 02:20:59.760
when there's still neuroplasticity
that is very robust?

02:20:59.760 --> 02:21:02.400
And if so, what's the
typical route for treatment?

02:21:02.400 --> 02:21:04.950
I have to imagine it's not
brain surgery typically.

02:21:05.820 --> 02:21:07.320
I'm guessing there are speech therapists

02:21:07.320 --> 02:21:09.783
that people can talk to,

02:21:10.961 --> 02:21:14.130
and they can help them work
out where they're getting stuck

02:21:14.130 --> 02:21:15.900
and the relationship to anxiety.

02:21:15.900 --> 02:21:16.733
- Yeah, exactly.

02:21:16.733 --> 02:21:19.080
I mean, part of it is about that anxiety,

02:21:19.080 --> 02:21:23.910
but a lot of it really
has to do with therapy

02:21:23.910 --> 02:21:26.100
to sort of like work through

02:21:26.100 --> 02:21:30.540
and think of tricks basically
sometimes to create conditions

02:21:30.540 --> 02:21:33.330
where you can actually
get the words to come out.

02:21:33.330 --> 02:21:36.660
Some forms of stuttering are
really initiation problems.

02:21:36.660 --> 02:21:39.390
Just getting started itself is very hard.

02:21:39.390 --> 02:21:43.290
You want to start with
initial vowel or consonant,

02:21:43.290 --> 02:21:44.823
but it won't emit.

02:21:45.780 --> 02:21:49.530
And so a lot of the therapy
is really just focusing

02:21:49.530 --> 02:21:52.590
on, like, how do you
create the conditions,

02:21:52.590 --> 02:21:54.690
you know, for that to happen?

02:21:54.690 --> 02:21:57.570
There's another aspect to it
that I find very interesting

02:21:57.570 --> 02:21:59.700
is that the feedback,

02:21:59.700 --> 02:22:03.753
essentially, what we hear
ourselves say, for example,

02:22:05.010 --> 02:22:08.070
and every time that I say a word,

02:22:08.070 --> 02:22:09.450
I'm also hearing what I'm saying,

02:22:09.450 --> 02:22:12.000
so that's what we call auditory feedback.

02:22:12.000 --> 02:22:13.680
That turns out to be very important,

02:22:13.680 --> 02:22:15.840
and sometimes when you change that,

02:22:15.840 --> 02:22:18.240
it can actually change the
amount someone stutters

02:22:18.240 --> 02:22:19.920
for better or for worse.

02:22:19.920 --> 02:22:23.820
And it's giving us a clue that the brain

02:22:23.820 --> 02:22:27.300
is not just focused on
sending the commands out,

02:22:27.300 --> 02:22:29.580
but it's also possibly interacting

02:22:29.580 --> 02:22:32.070
with the part that is hearing the sounds,

02:22:32.070 --> 02:22:34.900
and there's something might
be going on in that connection

02:22:34.900 --> 02:22:37.650
that breaks down when stuttering occurs.

02:22:37.650 --> 02:22:41.130
So there are individuals
that are stutterers

02:22:41.130 --> 02:22:43.200
but they don't stutter all the time.

02:22:43.200 --> 02:22:44.220
In those instances,

02:22:44.220 --> 02:22:47.460
there's something happening
in those particular moments

02:22:47.460 --> 02:22:51.000
where this very, very precise coordination

02:22:51.000 --> 02:22:52.650
needs to happen in the brain

02:22:52.650 --> 02:22:54.990
in order to get the words out fluently.

02:22:54.990 --> 02:22:56.550
- We've talked a little bit about caffeine

02:22:56.550 --> 02:22:57.693
and why you avoid it.

02:22:58.770 --> 02:23:01.770
Because your work requires
such precision and calm,

02:23:01.770 --> 02:23:03.570
and, frankly, to me,

02:23:03.570 --> 02:23:06.030
it seems like you're
running a lot of operations,

02:23:06.030 --> 02:23:09.060
no pun intended, in parallel
when you're doing surgery,

02:23:09.060 --> 02:23:11.130
not just thinking about where
to direct the instruments,

02:23:11.130 --> 02:23:13.500
but also thinking like a chess player

02:23:13.500 --> 02:23:14.910
several steps down the line

02:23:14.910 --> 02:23:18.120
what could happen, what
if, if-then type thinking.

02:23:18.120 --> 02:23:21.300
What are some of the other
practices and tools that you use

02:23:21.300 --> 02:23:25.200
to put yourself into state
for optimal neurosurgery

02:23:25.200 --> 02:23:28.770
or for, you know, thinking
about scientific problems

02:23:28.770 --> 02:23:29.730
for that matter?

02:23:29.730 --> 02:23:31.380
We keep threatening to
go running together,

02:23:31.380 --> 02:23:33.900
but I know you run, correct?

02:23:33.900 --> 02:23:35.100
- Yeah.

02:23:35.100 --> 02:23:37.500
- Do you find running
to be an essential part

02:23:37.500 --> 02:23:40.320
of your state regulation?

02:23:40.320 --> 02:23:41.940
- Absolutely, yeah.

02:23:41.940 --> 02:23:45.810
So for me, most exercise that I do,

02:23:45.810 --> 02:23:48.180
I really don't do for physical reasons.

02:23:48.180 --> 02:23:50.820
I do it for mental reasons.

02:23:50.820 --> 02:23:52.860
I can tell, for example,

02:23:52.860 --> 02:23:57.860
if I don't go on a run or a
swim just after a day or two,

02:23:58.170 --> 02:23:59.610
and it can have translation,

02:23:59.610 --> 02:24:02.400
for example, in the way I
feel in the operating room

02:24:02.400 --> 02:24:04.800
or even the way I interact
with other people.

02:24:04.800 --> 02:24:06.570
So there's no question

02:24:06.570 --> 02:24:09.870
that, you know, the mind and
body are deeply connected,

02:24:09.870 --> 02:24:11.760
and for me personally,

02:24:11.760 --> 02:24:15.810
being able to have opportunity
to disconnect for a while,

02:24:15.810 --> 02:24:18.450
it turns out to be
really, really important.

02:24:18.450 --> 02:24:22.983
Now, the operating room,
for me, is another space,

02:24:24.120 --> 02:24:26.430
kind of like running or swimming,

02:24:26.430 --> 02:24:30.210
where I'm disconnected
from the rest of the world.

02:24:30.210 --> 02:24:33.183
I don't bring my cell phone
into the operating room.

02:24:34.320 --> 02:24:37.110
I'm disconnected from the external world

02:24:37.110 --> 02:24:39.330
for that time that I'm in the surgery,

02:24:39.330 --> 02:24:41.880
and all I am doing is just focusing.

02:24:41.880 --> 02:24:44.970
Now, that doesn't mean that
I'm having complex thoughts

02:24:44.970 --> 02:24:47.310
or doing something very complicated.

02:24:47.310 --> 02:24:50.793
Sometimes it is like that,
but it's not always like that.

02:24:51.720 --> 02:24:53.490
There are things that we do in surgery

02:24:53.490 --> 02:24:57.420
that are, like, routine and rote

02:24:57.420 --> 02:24:59.070
and are from muscle memory.

02:24:59.070 --> 02:25:01.170
So, for example, suturing skin

02:25:01.170 --> 02:25:04.230
or doing certain kinds of dissection

02:25:04.230 --> 02:25:06.090
or drilling part of the bone, for example,

02:25:06.090 --> 02:25:09.270
these are all things that
become very rote after a time.

02:25:09.270 --> 02:25:12.270
So, for me, even being
in the operating room

02:25:12.270 --> 02:25:14.280
actually can sometimes
fulfill that purpose.

02:25:14.280 --> 02:25:18.600
So I really look forward to
being in the operating room

02:25:18.600 --> 02:25:22.560
because that intense focus

02:25:22.560 --> 02:25:24.330
allows me to sort of disconnect

02:25:24.330 --> 02:25:26.220
from all the other things
that I'm worrying about,

02:25:26.220 --> 02:25:30.120
you know, that are happening
out in the outside world.

02:25:30.120 --> 02:25:32.310
You know, we all have those
kind of things that happen,

02:25:32.310 --> 02:25:35.400
and I'm certainly no exception to that.

02:25:35.400 --> 02:25:40.020
But, strangely, the operating
room, for me, is a sanctuary.

02:25:40.020 --> 02:25:41.940
I love being there

02:25:41.940 --> 02:25:45.963
because we have some control
over the environment.

02:25:48.510 --> 02:25:52.863
I know what is there, I know
the anatomy of the brain,

02:25:54.210 --> 02:25:57.630
my motions are going through routines.

02:25:57.630 --> 02:26:00.570
And so for me, that's not
actually very different

02:26:00.570 --> 02:26:01.770
than going on a run

02:26:01.770 --> 02:26:05.880
and letting my, you know,
legs move in specific ways.

02:26:05.880 --> 02:26:08.520
It's just the same thing for my hands.

02:26:08.520 --> 02:26:11.280
- Do you listen to music
or audiobooks when you run,

02:26:11.280 --> 02:26:14.610
or are you divorced from
technology when you run?

02:26:14.610 --> 02:26:17.940
- Well, music helps me,
like, just stay motivated

02:26:17.940 --> 02:26:21.570
and distracted from being out
of breath and other things.

02:26:21.570 --> 02:26:23.850
And for me, it's a way to just catch up

02:26:23.850 --> 02:26:25.410
with, like, the world.

02:26:25.410 --> 02:26:28.080
So sometimes I do, but I do notice

02:26:28.080 --> 02:26:30.630
that, like, I don't run
as well, for example.

02:26:30.630 --> 02:26:32.670
In the operating room,
it's a little different.

02:26:32.670 --> 02:26:35.520
You know, different
surgeons have preferences.

02:26:35.520 --> 02:26:38.820
I'm more of the camp

02:26:38.820 --> 02:26:41.220
where I don't like any
distraction whatsoever.

02:26:41.220 --> 02:26:44.310
I like people to be able to
hear the words that I'm saying

02:26:44.310 --> 02:26:46.443
without having background noise.

02:26:49.290 --> 02:26:52.170
I don't really think about
relying on music or other things

02:26:52.170 --> 02:26:54.300
to try to put me in a state of mind.

02:26:54.300 --> 02:26:57.150
You know, I think just being there alone

02:26:57.150 --> 02:27:00.330
and just, you know, trying
to treat it the way it is,

02:27:00.330 --> 02:27:03.270
it's a sacred moment where someone's life

02:27:03.270 --> 02:27:06.210
is really directly under your hands,

02:27:06.210 --> 02:27:09.270
That enough kind of
focuses me very quickly,

02:27:09.270 --> 02:27:11.190
and I like that.

02:27:11.190 --> 02:27:13.470
It really detaches me
from a lot of the things

02:27:13.470 --> 02:27:15.300
that are preoccupying me,

02:27:15.300 --> 02:27:18.870
and for those couple of
hours that we have a surgery,

02:27:18.870 --> 02:27:21.180
we're just focused on one thing only.

02:27:21.180 --> 02:27:22.410
- That's fantastic.

02:27:22.410 --> 02:27:25.740
Again, I think of, in the
range of brain explorers,

02:27:25.740 --> 02:27:28.950
the neurosurgeons, those
of your profession,

02:27:28.950 --> 02:27:31.920
are, to me, like the
astronauts of neuroscience

02:27:31.920 --> 02:27:33.120
because they're really going

02:27:33.120 --> 02:27:35.370
to the farthest reaches possible,

02:27:35.370 --> 02:27:36.750
and they're testing and probing

02:27:36.750 --> 02:27:40.650
and really at the front edge
of discovering from the species

02:27:40.650 --> 02:27:45.210
that we arguably care about
the most, which is humans.

02:27:45.210 --> 02:27:46.263
Eddie, I have to say,

02:27:48.180 --> 02:27:52.590
from the first time we became
friends 38 years ago...

02:27:52.590 --> 02:27:53.423
- Something like that.

02:27:53.423 --> 02:27:54.380
- Something like that.

02:27:55.380 --> 02:27:57.630
I'm almost reluctant to say,

02:27:57.630 --> 02:28:00.990
so I only reveal it in part
that Eddie and I became friends

02:28:00.990 --> 02:28:05.040
because both he and I
shared a love of birds,

02:28:05.040 --> 02:28:07.980
and we had a club at our school

02:28:07.980 --> 02:28:11.100
of which there were only
two members, Eddie and I.

02:28:11.100 --> 02:28:11.933
- Small club.

02:28:11.933 --> 02:28:12.780
- Small club.

02:28:12.780 --> 02:28:14.280
There was one honorary member,

02:28:14.280 --> 02:28:16.380
and there were certain
requirements for being in this club

02:28:16.380 --> 02:28:17.370
that we won't reveal.

02:28:17.370 --> 02:28:19.050
We took a pact of secrecy,

02:28:19.050 --> 02:28:21.960
and we're going to obey
that pact of secrecy.

02:28:21.960 --> 02:28:23.860
But to be sitting here with you today,

02:28:24.810 --> 02:28:27.573
for me, is a absolute thrill,

02:28:28.530 --> 02:28:30.210
not just because we've
been friends for that long

02:28:30.210 --> 02:28:31.530
or that we got reacquainted

02:28:31.530 --> 02:28:34.080
through literally the halls
of medicine and science,

02:28:34.080 --> 02:28:37.290
but because I really do
see what you're doing

02:28:37.290 --> 02:28:42.290
as really representing that
front, absolute cutting edge

02:28:43.020 --> 02:28:45.030
of exploration and application.

02:28:45.030 --> 02:28:48.360
I mean, the story of Pancho is
but one of your many patients

02:28:48.360 --> 02:28:52.350
that has derived tremendous
benefit from your work.

02:28:52.350 --> 02:28:53.700
And now as the chair of a department,

02:28:53.700 --> 02:28:58.410
you, of course, work alongside individuals

02:28:58.410 --> 02:28:59.610
who are also doing incredible work

02:28:59.610 --> 02:29:01.770
in the spinal cord, et cetera.

02:29:01.770 --> 02:29:04.410
So on behalf of myself
and everyone listening,

02:29:04.410 --> 02:29:07.320
I just really want to thank
you for joining us today

02:29:07.320 --> 02:29:08.640
to share this information.

02:29:08.640 --> 02:29:09.960
We will certainly have you back

02:29:09.960 --> 02:29:12.540
because there's an entire
list of other questions

02:29:12.540 --> 02:29:14.130
we didn't have time to get to,

02:29:14.130 --> 02:29:18.030
but also just for the work you
do, it's truly spectacular.

02:29:18.030 --> 02:29:19.803
- Andrew, thanks so much.

02:29:20.910 --> 02:29:23.850
You know, I'm very humbled
basically by what you just said,

02:29:23.850 --> 02:29:28.440
and I feel that it's really an
extraordinary honor actually

02:29:28.440 --> 02:29:30.270
and privilege, you know,
to be here with you

02:29:30.270 --> 02:29:33.780
and reconnect and talk
about all these ideas.

02:29:33.780 --> 02:29:35.460
It's probably not random,

02:29:35.460 --> 02:29:39.540
you know, that we ended up in
similar spots and interests.

02:29:39.540 --> 02:29:40.650
I think when we were kids,

02:29:40.650 --> 02:29:42.850
you know, it starts
with some deep interests

02:29:43.830 --> 02:29:47.370
and kind of nerding out on topics,

02:29:47.370 --> 02:29:50.520
and it's probably not a coincidence,

02:29:50.520 --> 02:29:54.840
you know, that we have such
deep interest in this work now.

02:29:54.840 --> 02:29:57.390
I just feel really lucky
to be able to do what I do.

02:29:57.390 --> 02:30:00.930
It's fun every day, almost every day,

02:30:00.930 --> 02:30:03.480
be able to go to work
and take care of folks

02:30:03.480 --> 02:30:06.540
and learn at the same time
and then just close the loop,

02:30:06.540 --> 02:30:08.250
you know, how do we apply the knowledge

02:30:08.250 --> 02:30:11.670
that we learn one day to
someone who comes in next week?

02:30:11.670 --> 02:30:12.600
It's really fun.

02:30:12.600 --> 02:30:17.190
And we don't know everything,
we're not even close to it,

02:30:17.190 --> 02:30:19.923
but the journey to figure this out,

02:30:21.180 --> 02:30:22.590
it's really extraordinary.

02:30:22.590 --> 02:30:27.510
I mean, it's like you said,

02:30:27.510 --> 02:30:29.670
it's exploring new lands.

02:30:29.670 --> 02:30:31.500
Literally in the operating room

02:30:31.500 --> 02:30:35.610
when I'm looking at the exposed
cortex trying to understand

02:30:35.610 --> 02:30:40.610
is it safe to walk down this
part of the cortical landscape

02:30:40.650 --> 02:30:42.570
or this other trail?

02:30:42.570 --> 02:30:44.460
You know, which one is going to be the one

02:30:44.460 --> 02:30:45.600
that is going to be safe

02:30:45.600 --> 02:30:48.090
versus the other that results in paralysis

02:30:48.090 --> 02:30:49.383
and inability to talk?

02:30:50.850 --> 02:30:52.200
Well, maybe I shouldn't call it fun,

02:30:52.200 --> 02:30:56.040
but it's very important, too,

02:30:56.040 --> 02:30:58.410
in addition to being really
